      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>AvoTytiirtion mcDouGALL'S Pateo sELF-RAISING FLOUR p:t3:y. The Examiner, Saturday, December 4, 1909.</head>
        <p>AvoTytiirtion mcDouGALL'S Pateo sELF-RAISING FLOUR p:t3:y. The Examiner, Saturday, December 4, 1909.</p>
        <p>get their stories out of them? All sorts of people ean tell stories who break down when they try to put them on paper." "But they don't tell them to a stranger who assails them point-blank, like a highway- man, with your story or your life.' The man would laugh at me .and the girl would call a policeman." .</p>
        <p>"Than go to some place where etiquette is not so rigid as on the roof of an omnibus— some Bohemian place where people unbend quickly under genial influences. ' "Where, for instance ? " "Try the Covent Garden Fancy Dress Ball. There's one to-morrow."</p>
        <p>"I know. I've got a Press ticket for it, but I wasn't meaning to go." "Take my advice, and go. You can make acquaintances pretty easily there. Make them, and offer a champagne supper as a prize to any, lady ' who can tell you a love story with a happy ending. You can alter the story to suit your readers if it's too Bohemian."</p>
        <p>It was an idea, if not a very hopeful one. The only alternative courses were, as it seemed to the author, to throw up the com- mission, or to plagiarise from a back number of • some forgotten periodical. He did not want to do either of these things, so, as he had the Press ticket in his pocket, he resolved to go to Covent Garden.</p>
        <p>"I'm not very sanguine, but I'll try the experiment," he said. " It's worth trying. Let me know the result," responded the philosopher. So the author went, though not in, fancy dress, nor in the mood of reckless gaiety that is proper to such diversions. He cut a sombre figure as he stood with his back to the wall, gloomily drawing on his gloves, and the thoughts that coursed through his mind were also of a sombre hue. It was only with the greatest effort that he could bring them to bear upon his errand. They drifted back continually to his .own sorrowful case, and he found himself soliloquising: "A pretty love story indeed—to be cut out by that worn-out rout. Does she know? But of course she doesn't. Women never do know until it is too late. And men mustn't tell them. The great conspiracy of silence must be kept up at all costs. If she were in love, of course, she wouldn't even care. But is she in love—with anything except the family diamonds, and the rest of it I Would it have made any difference if I had told her in time that I " . .</p>
        <p>Thus he ruminated - bitterly, uncertain whether he pitied Dora Myrtle or despised her; and then his thoughts were thrown in another direction. . "if she knew he were in this place to- night would that please her, I wonder 1" For there was Lord Edward Chevenix stalking past him. He was a man whom most Bohemians knew by sight; and, the author knew his Bohemia tolerably, though he did not dwell in it. -So he recognised his rival, and took stock of him, noting , that he was well groomed in spite of his somewhat fishy eyes; and presently he overheard scraps of his conversation. He was replying to the remonstrances of a youth who suggested that a Covent Garden Ball was a strange resort for a man on the eve of matrimony. "I know, dear old chap, I know. Jt's, posi- tively my last appearance in these haunts., But Dolly Mayblossom made me promise to come—positively wouldn't give me any peace unless I, did. It's. the last ,night in Bohemia before settling down to a respectable old ace." The other ridiculed respectability, and Lord Edward defended it/ "I've put it off long enough," he, said. "No doubt that's why I'm so .fascinated by the prospect of it. I'm really very angry with Dolly for dragging me here to-night. But there she is. I must go and speak to her. Good-bye." The author glared after him as he moved on and . merged in the crowd. Bohemianism was all very well in its way; but for a man just about to be married to attend. a Bo- hemian ball to meet a lady of the Gaiety chorus—that, he felt, was carrying Bohemiat- ism rather too far. If Dora knew—but ob- viously he could not , tell her. He had no locus standi. The reflection was very galling to him, and he felt further removed tha:i ever from his love storywith a happy ending.. He forced himself, however, to resume life quest for it, though only to find himself laughed at for his pains. „ The lugubrious manner in which he proceeded with his in- quiries did indeed fairly afford food for mirth, and most of the ladies to whom he addressed himself responded with frivolity. The. only really 'philosophic sentiment that he lighted upon was this: There is - only one way of making sure that a love story will end happily." " And that is ? " "Not to care." But he was quite sure that the fiction ' editor would not be satisfied with a happy ending arranged upon those lines, and rest- ing upon that 'hypothesis, so • he replied: Thank you. But if one does not carei then there is no love story," and the only rejoinder to that observation Was a sigh that admitted its truth. " The prize of the champagne supper, there- fore, remitined unwon. In fact, • the author had not even got to the point of offering it. He was not only conscious of failure, he even felt as if the , gaiety of the whole assemblage were being damped by the wet blanket of his despondency. He made up his mind to go, and then changed his mind, and decided that he would try once more. A lady who wore a domino and was masked, and who stood alone as if she entered into the spirit of the festivities 'as little as himself, attracted him. Since those who in appearance were gay had failed to help him, he would try this one, who alone seemed to be sad; Perhaps, by some curious law of contrasts-- , At any rate, he would make the • experiment. He could only waste ten 'minutes over it, at the worst. "Excuse me. You seem tired. Can 1 be of any assistance to you ? " he asked, by way of breaking the ice. "It is very kind of you. I think not, thanks," was the reply. The author started. He almost jumped. The domino , and the mask are very effective disguises. One cannot recognise one's dearest - friends through them. But there is one thing which they do not dis- guise, and that is the voicm. The author knew the voice, or thought he knew it, though he 'hardly dated to be sure. If it Was not Dora Myrtle's voice, then be would never again trust the evidence of his own sepses. It was incredible that she should be found in such a place. •• Her fiancé most obviously had not brought' her, and certainly would be shocked .and angry if he knew that she was there. And yet-- He could, discover nothing by peering into her eves. The disguise was too impenetrable for that. Arid he had no right to ask ques- tions—n 6 right to display astonishment. So he tried to compose himserlf, and merely said : "Your secret is your own, of courFte, but will you forgive me if I say that I thought I recognised your voice ?" - " You thought that, really ?, " "I still think so. It seems to me to be the voice of someone whom I used. to know quite well." "But you can't.- be sure. So speak to irke as if you did not think you knew Me." • Then he was quite sure, though 6till de- voured by enriosity as to the reason of her presence. Perhaps ohe would tell him presently—if he did not ask , her. So he refrained from asking, • but talked on •as to a stranger, gently feeling his way towards a more intimate' acquaintance. " You must be tired of standing. There are some . seats over there. You are not waiting for anyone ? " "No. lam alone hero." "Then if you would let me take you tc have some claret-cup, or an ice—" She accepted, and took his arm. His blood tingled at the touch. It recalled the day when he had first taken her down to dinner, and had beuu to realise. how much might come to mean to him. This was, in. deed, a strange sequel to that meeting. TYRE ;there to be a further sequel? He Wondered and for the moment he was happy. " Shall we sit here 1" he asked, . indicat- ing a reee_ss where they were unlikely to bt interrupted. Did you say you would Lave claret-cup ? " "No, thanks. Only an' He gave the order, and they sat on r divan of crimson plush, 'separated by</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>BRITISH WORKERS WORN OUT.</head>
        <p>BRITISH WORKERS WORN OUT.</p>
        <p>Oyer-Tasked Strength Shortens Life. A National Danger and its Remedy.</p>
        <p>British working men earn other people's fortunes at the cost of all that makes their own lives worth living. Rising in the grey dawn, they toil long after their strength holds out. Grim resolution keeps them at it. By noon, their reserve strength is being used. This means that they are wearing out their own bodies, instead of working on the food they took at breakfast. This wearing out ci our best strength is a national danger. But what concerns you, the working man who reads this, is your own danger. Do you want to be worn out before you are old? If you don't the remedy is to make your breakfast stay with you. Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa at breakfast will husband your strength. It is extra food, and as it contains Kola, malt, hops, and the finest cocoa, it sustains bodily strength and prevents fatigue. If you had nothing else but a cup oi Vi-Cocoa for breakfast you could work longer without being tired than if you ate a full breakfast without Vi-Cocoa. Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa is absolutely the "last word" in the department of drinks, for it is both food and &amp;ink, and a packet can,,be obtained from any Grocer for the small sum of sixpence. Do not ask your grocer for cocoa. Ask for</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>OPIJPI erica Aw</head>
        <p>OPIJPI erica Aw</p>
        <p>makes all the difference. Every grocer Eells Vi-Cocoa in 6d. packets and Od and is. 6d. tins. I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above Council was held in the LL.P. Rooms, Friars' Green, on Tuesday• night. Mr. J. Haley presided over a fair attendance which included the secretary (Mr. C. Plinston).</p>
        <p>THE CO:OPERATIVE SOCIETY. The CHAIRMAN said that two of the members of that Council were candidates for, ofEcial positions on the Warrington Co-operative Society and the nomination was to take place at the next quarterly meeting. He earnestly asked for the stipport of the Council to be given to those two men. Mr. JONES said that the Cabinet-makers' Union,which be represented, were extremely anxious that the two delegates proposed by that Council should be elected, as his Union hail had reliable information that the Co-operative Society were buying their furniture from a certain firm who did not make their goods under fair conditions. Mr. DELANEY moved that literature be printed and -distributed among the members of the Society, calling attention to the claims of the Trades Council candi- dates. He thought that a little money should be spent in that way. The CHAIRMAN said that by the time the matter could be printed it would be too late for the election. Mr. LEACH said that with regard to nomination, in some places canvassing is done just the same as at a municiple election. Several members were against -the motion and the matter was put to vote. Five voted for and eight against and the motion was lost. CORPORATION WORK AND UNION MEN.</p>
        <p>In answer to a letter sent from the Council. risking that if there were any contracts, etc., for public work such as paving and sewerage work to give preference to the Trade Union men.—The Borough Surveyor wrote that he would lay their suggestion before the Paving and Sewerage Committee at their next meeting. He concluded" Do I understand that a deputation of your Council are to meet the Paving and Sewerage Com- mittee ? If so I think it is almost useless, inasmuch as the contract has beEn entered into and much of the work executed." The SECRETARY said there was another letter in prospect. The CHAIRMAN said that the councils letter did not only apply to the particular contract mentioned but to any other work that might have to be done in the 'future. He thought that all work unless expert work or a special class of work, should be executed by the men in the town. Mr. CLARKE a representative of the Plasterer's Union drew attention to the number of men out of work in the tow !rand asked if it was fair to employ inexperi- enced men from other places ? The CHAIRMAN said that a great deal of new property erected in the town should never h.rve seen the light of day. The workmanship and material used for the purpose was positively rotten. He related a particular instance when he went to where some new property was being built and there saw a load of supposed new bricks 'tipped on the floor, which when taken into the band could have been crumbled between the fingers. He called that kind of property" pneumonia" property. (Laughter,) Why was it that that triad of property was being put up? The Borougn Surveyor could give an answer, yet he had denied that he had anything to do with it. It was resolved that the same deputation who had been appointed on a previous occasion, should again have an interview with the Paving and Sewage Com- mittee.</p>
        <p>ELECTION ruso BALANCE SHEET.</p>
        <p>Mr. LZACH submitted the above balance sheet which was very satisfactory. The contributions were as follows, wire drawers, £2 10s.; painters, Os. 2d.; moulders, £1 35.; printers, ; hairdressers, Os.; steam engine engineers, 55.; sheet metal ,Aorkers, Gs. ; prudential agents,. Bs. 3d. ; cabinet makers, Os. 6d.; musicians, Os. ; total £6l2s. Bd. The total balance in hand now was £22 12s. Id.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>BOWLING GREENS.</head>
        <p>BOWLING GREENS.</p>
        <p>Mr. JONES moved a resolution, that a letter be sent to the chairman of the Parks and Baths' Committee (Alderman Bolton) asking them to put into execution their promises already made, to construct the five bowling greens, as early as possible and so tend to alleviate the d;stress, of the coming winter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>ONE THING NEEDED.</head>
        <p>THE ONE THING NEEDED.</p>
        <p>Without fire, no beat ! Without digested food no strength ! That's Nature's law, and it serves every- body alike. Digested food makes us strong, vigorous and healthy. Undigested food makes us weak and ill. Dyspeptics are always weak and ailing. All they heed to make them strong and well is the power to digest food and that is just what Mother Seigel's Syrup gives. It helps the stomach, liver and kidneys to do their work properly. That is all, but</p>
        <p>it is enough ! _ Mrs. Rodway, of Hillesley, Wotton-under-Edge, says, July 28th, 1909 :—" We have known the value Of Mother Seigel's Syrup for over 20 years, and have never koown it fail to cure stomach troubles. I used to have frightful paius in the chest and between the shoulders. I had painful wind in the stomach and water-brash. Sometimes, when I ate a bit of tart, for instance, I felt I should go mad. When I was bilious, and threw up my food, the pain would go; but of course, as I got no nourishment, I grew thin. Even after a piece of bread and butter, I felt heavy and in pain, until I dreaded meal times coming. " I turned—as I really should have done long before—to mother Seigel's Syrup, and a few bottles soon restored me to health." - - - -</p>
        <p>And Mr. Wm. Ilodway, her husband,says :—" I had for some time been suffering from a feeling of depression, loss of appetite, etc. and I grew very thin :and lost weight. %Then I tried Mother Seigel's Syrup. Since then, I feel a different person. My appetite lufs returned and I now enjoy my meals. The feeling of depression has quite passed away and I can do my daily work with pleasure. I have-also gained weight and feel decidedly more robust." Mother Seipel's Syrup, by acting gently, but directly upon the stomach, liver and bowels, restores sound digestion. the true foundation of heafth and strength. The 2s. 6d. bottle contains tin ee times as much as the is. 111 size.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>Late Mr. Joseph Taylor.</head>
        <p>The Late Mr. Joseph Taylor.</p>
        <p>The funeral of the late Mr. Joseph Taylor (who had been employed by Messrs. Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd.) for the last 45 years, and occupied the position of foreman carter. took place on Sunday at the Cemetery. He was also one of the oldest Mfm- bers of the National Order of Oddfellows, which was -well represented. The chief mourntrs were : Mrs. Joseph Taylor (widow), Messrs. Luke, William and Joseph Taylor (sons), Mrs. W. Jackson and Mrs. A. Looker (daughters), Messrs. W. Jackson and, A. Looker (son-in-laws)... Amongst others present were t 'Messrs. P. C. Price, J. Gibson, W. Dennett, J. Pharoah, William Hewitt, T. Pemberton, S. Allen, J. Blackledge, H. Taylor. and J. Lawton, Many floral tributes were received, and the deceased was carried to the grave by eight of his workmen, viz., Messrs. J Steele, J, Mather, W. Proffitt. F. Taylor, H. Clayton, G. Wright, L.,Spencer, and B. Gibbons.</p>
        <p>You may rely upon it that you are getting the. highest poEsible quality when you buy "GOLDEN FLEECE" MARGARINE.—Advt.</p>
        <p>[ALL RIG:ITS RESERVED.!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>HAPPY ENDING.</head>
        <p>A HAPPY ENDING.</p>
        <p>IT FRANCIS GRIDDLE,</p>
        <p>Author of "The_ Red Spell," "The Things That Matter," " The Lower Life," "Sun- light and Limelight," "Stromboli," Sre.</p>
        <p>A certain rising young author had received instructions from • a certain "fiction editor" —a wise man, who knew exactly what his readers wanted to read—and those instruc- tions were rigidly precise. He was to write; at a stipulated length, " a love story (scene laid in the United ingdom) with a happy ending." The last sfords were underlined; and the rising young author in consequence went about pulling a long face, and. calling upon his friends to pity him. A happy ,ending? And why not? What's the matter with happiness they asked him, derisively. "Nothing," he answered, cloomily, "ex- cept that it is so confounclealy uneventful. The course of true love either runs smooth or it does not. If it (lees there's no story, and' it it doesn't there's np happiness. That's the dilemma." 'They jeered at him unsympathetically, and be turew out a challenge.</p>
        <p>as "Very well," he said: "If you think it's as simple as all that, tell me a love story with a happy ending:" At that they only jeered the more. For he was speaking at a literary club, and the members were not to be. drawn. Love stories with happy endings were to them valuable assets—a part of the stock-in-trade—not things to be given away with careless gene- rosity like cigarettes. A philosopher, however, offered a sugges- tion. "Go to life for your story," he said. "Live it first, and write it afterwards." "The notice is too short," he replied. "1 have to deliver the manuscript in six weeks. Besides, suppose I did set out to live a love storys in six weeks, who guarantees the happy ending ? " Naturally no one would undertake that grave responsibility, and the author with- drew in dudgeon. For the fact was that he had a love story of his own, and that the prospects, of its ending happily were not bright. • her name was Dora Myrtle. She had come quite lately into his life like a sudden ray of', sunshine. He had thought that he was quite sure of her, . and therefore had not hurried. ! They were both young, and time seemed end- lessly long. Before he asked her to be his wife, he wanted to win a success that would be worthy of her. "Phenomenal sales" would make all the difference to her mother's opinion of his eligibility, even if it made none to hers, and he did not doubt that these would come to him in due course. . Meanwhile .he dallied delightfully with his happiness, and while he dallied with it. another had stepped down before him. He opened the Morning Post while he was breakfasting, and read: " A marriage has been arranged, and will shortly take place, between Miss Dora Myrtle, Grove-terrace, Bayswater, daughter of the late Colonel Myrtle, .of the 189th Light Infantry, and Lord Edward Chevenix, youngest ,son of the Marquis of Walmer." His first impulse was to utter a profane ,ejaculation, and he uttered it. His second impulse was to jump into a hansom cab, and drive round to remonstrate; but he reflected, and refrained. " I can't. I've no toms"staedi. fool I've been ! " he told himself. Ile had been a laggard in love (though from the best of motives), and now there seemed nothing for it but to accept the situa- tion and send a wedding present. But first I'll call and offer my congratu- lations. - Perhaps—" He pictured the congratulations leading up to reproaches; the reproaches drawing tears; a stormy, tragic scene ensuing. But even that doubtful consolation was denied to him. it was not Dora but her mother who re- .ceived him, and gushed effusively. It was • such an excellent match for Dora. The dear girl was so happy about it. It was so good of the author to call and congratu- late. Dora would be so sorry to have missed him. And Lord Edward was so nice. A little wild in his youth, but quite range now. Or, at all events he had promised to ranger him- self when he got, married. He had met Dora at a church bazaar, and fallen in love With her at first sight. It was most touching to see how fond they were of one another. Did the author know Lord Edward?. No? Then she should look forward to introducing him. She would send ,him a card for the wedding. What was all. She gave him no chance whatever to make, a scene. He could not de- mand an interview with Dora, seeing that he had, as he had admitted to himself, no locus standi. Nor could he tell Mrs. Myrtle the things that he had heard about Lord Edward Chevenix and his career of gallantry in the demi-monde. Either she would not believe him, or else she knew already, and did not care. Evidently the girl whom he loved was to be sacrificed to a middle-aged roué; but she was apparently a consenting party to the sacrifice. In that case, she would, as the vulgar put it, "deserve all , she got." But that was another of the things that he could not say. All that he .could say was: " Gooal-bve, Mrs. Myrtle. You will assure Miss Myrtle of ray sincere wishes for her happiness ? " To which Mrs. Myrtle replied: "I will be sure to do so. She is very happy, and very fortunate. Her friends could have wished for nothing better." So he took his hat and gloves and went; and that was the stage at which things had arrived when the philosopher advised him to go to life for his "love story (scene laid in the United Kingdom) with a happy ending." It was the only love story that he could bring his mind to bear upon at all; and he did not sea how he could possibly twist it to suit the purposes of the fiction editor. For, if he dwelt upon the happiness of the roue who terminated a career of dissipation by marrying an innocent girl, or upon the happi- ness of the girl who sold herself to the roué for diamonds, and a title, and a house in Eaton-square, the fiction editor would say that "cynicism was not what his readers wanted." The story had to be written, however, from whatever source he derived it; and an in- genious thought occurred to him,. He would circularise his friends—friends who, not be- ing authors, would be only too proud to help him—and inquire if any of them had any personal knowledge of a "love-story (scene laid in the United Kingdom) with a happy ending." It seemed as reasonable for an author to do this as for an ,editor to circularise celebrities with requests for lists of "hymns that had helped them," or of "the six worst books that they had read in 1904." He did so, and, got a certain number of answers, but no answer a that was of the slightest use to him; ad he realised the great mistake that he had made. Every one of the friends whose story he did not use would feel slighted. He would have to spend days in writing letters of s regretful apology, and none of the apologies would be unre- servedly accepted. 'How could he explain to a lady who wrote to say that she once knew a girl who misrried a man who saved her from a mad dog, and they had five children, and the husband was ostrich-farming in South Africa, that he did not see his way to make anything very striking or original out of that? And most of the stories sent to him were equally unsuggestive. He summed the matter up tt himself with the reflection: It's as I told them at the club. When it ends happily there's no story, and when there's a story it , doesn't end happily." Meanwhile, the days were running on, and the thought of his unwritten story was haunt- ing him like a nightmare. His own life had failed him. His friends' lives had failed him. The springs of invention seemed to be dried up. He really did not know what to do. . The philosopher who had counselled him to •• go to life " offered a fresh suggestion. "Try strangers," he said. "Somebody is is sure to know the sort of story that you want. Stop them in thd street and ask them. Try the man in the train. Try the girl on tba roof of the omnibus." "How • can 1 ? the author protested. "They'll take me for a wandering lunatic." "What does that matter, so long as you</p>
        <p>What a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>marble-topped table from those who lanced the Lancers. The circumstances were sufficiently strange to make conversa- ticip enxbarrasng and difficult; but his literary mission gave the author his open- ing, which might lead anywhere. So he plunged abruptly. "You would never guess what brought me here," he began, and she shook her head.</p>
        <p>"I'm an author,' he continued. : "I know," she answered, and th quickly corrected herself. "I mean to say, are you really? It is very interesting to meet an author." _ _ . .</p>
        <p>He smiled, and pursued his point. " Then now, perhaps, you can guess why I am here —what lam looking for, I mean." . "I suppose you are looking , for what you call 'local colour' " she suggested. "No, I am looking for a love story with a hapTir ending. I have to .write one, and I don't know one, and I can't invent one. So far .as my own experience • goes, love-stories don't end happily. But my own experience isn't everything; and I thought that if I came here, where people enjoy themselves so .much, I might meet someone who could help me. Could you?" She sighed beneath her mask. It had never before pleased him so much to hear a woman sigh. "Does that mean that you are not happy " he made bold to ask. I - don't know," she answered. story hasn't ended yet." • "But you can foresee the end " "The end did seem inevitable, but I want to change it. That is the reason why I am here to-night. You didn't think I came without some special reason, did you 7". "On the contrary, I have been racking my brains to guess the reason; but the one which you have giver only puzzles .the." _</p>
        <p>" The</p>
        <p>You will understand it when I unmaski" _</p>
        <p>That puzzled him the more. It was strange enough that Dora . Myrtle should have come to this place in disguise. That. she should challenge the censorious opinion of the world by throwing the disguise off seemed quite inexplicable. Unless- But he did not like to question her directly, after his promise to pretend that he had not reccignised her. - Then an idea occurred to him. "Sup- pose," he said, " you were to tell me the unhappy part of your story, and let me see if, as an, author, I could fit a happy ending to it." You are interested ? _ _</p>
        <p>am interested in nothing else." "It is a story of a girl who is engaged to be married, and wishes she were not." . "is not the girl a free agent, then? It is not men who bring actions for breach of promise." , ," But if the girl has a mother who first made her give her promise, and now holds her to it, so that she dare not even appeal to the man to set her free:'</p>
        <p>He nodded. "That explains something, but not everything. It does not explain what the girl is doing here to-night." "The man is here to-night," she said.</p>
        <p>He nodded again. "Amusing himself very much." "It •seems so. But, still-" He could not believe that she had followed her suitor to this haunt of dissipation merely in order to •convict him of being there, and to return to her mother With tnat proof vi his unworthiness. Yet what alternative ob- ject could she have? She asked him a ques- tion which supplied a hint. " What line do you think the man would take if he knew that the girl were here ? "</p>
        <p>tie stared at her, amazed by her audacity, wbile she talked on, flinging disguises and pretences to the winds. , "I'm not brave, but I'm reckless. I've screwed myself up to do it, and I mean to. No one knows what I'm -doing except 'ka girl friend with whom I'm staying; and whom I can trust. I'm going to unmask:---only for a moment—and let him see. It is the only way. He'll change his mind about me then, Ah ! here he comes. Don't leave me."</p>
        <p>He would have tried instinctively to dis- suade her, but there was no time. Lord Edward Chevenix was approaching, and she was . already rising from her place. He caught at her hand, but felt his clutch evaded. For the rest, he could only look and listen while the end of the love story was being changed. Lord Edward Chevenix was advancing, ith a strange Bohemian lady hanging on . his arm. As Dora Myrtle removed her mask, the author saw him start, first like a man detected in a crime,,• and then like a man who. could not believe his eyes. He trembled, ' fearing that there wotild be a scene. No doubt a scene was what the Bohemian lady would have enjoyed. Luckily she did not understand what was happening, but accepted the hasty excuse made to her, and consented to it down.; while her com- panion stepped forward and spoke. "I am . surprised," were his first words. "And shocked, no doubt ? " was the un- blushing answer. - • - "Shocked too, if you will make me say so. This certainly is not the place in which I should have wished to meet the lady whom I am to marry." "is it the place where I should be pleased to meet the man who-" "Of course, if you came here to act as a spy upon my movemenfs-" I am- not criticising your actions. Why should you criticise mine ? " "The two cases are different." "Suppose I say that I don't think so ? " "But you do think so." "Suppose I don't ? " His position was very awkward. In the first place his conscience was not clear, and in the second place the Bohemian lady was waiting for • him, and ,might display im- patience in such a manner as to cause the scene he dreaded.</p>
        <p>"Put on ' your mask again," he said; and that order she obeyed. "Now come with me, and I. will see that you are sent home." "But I am not alone."</p>
        <p>His brow blackened. A scene seemed very imminent, and the author was wondering what part he would be called upon to play in it. But the other controlled himself, and merely retorted with veiled anger: .. "In that case; Miss Myrtle, I think it must be clear. to you that it will be neces- sary -for both of us to reconsider our rela- tions to each other," -</p>
        <p>It was the speech that she had been play- ing for, yet now that she heard it, she was ashamed; and she felt still more ashamed when he pressed her with: " Of course, if that is what you wish-" "if only you understood, she stam- mered; and he knew from her voice that she vas crying. He stood before her, puzzled and em- barrassed, as well as angry, the plain truth filtering slowly through his brain. All the little facts of their courtship began to have their meaning for him. He perceived now why 11... had found her irresponsive. Evidently it was because ' she had obeyed her 'mother, not her heart. He was still very angry, but ,he was a gentleman. Perhaps he, too, found that he cared less than he had thought, and vas not so anxious to say good-bye to Bohemia as Ile had imagined. So he bit his lip, and said: You ,'do wish • it. Very well. We'll find a pretext. For your own sake, go home, and dcn't remove the mask again. Your secret sill be safe with me." "Thank you," she replied, and took his hand in gratitude and let him go. "I feel so ashamed of myself," she said, when she returned to the author. "He was very good about it., It might all have been settled without my doing • anything so scandalous." "But the end of the story is changed," replied the author. "It is a happy ending for you, and if only it might be a happy ending for me as well-" And that point was settled in the cab, in Which he took her back to her friend's house, and he was able to go the next morn- ing to the nhilosonher at the club and say: "um man, t took your acmce. I -went to life for my love story with a happy ending, and I found ,it at the eleventh pour; and when I've written it out, I shall come down on you for a wedding present.". [TEE END.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>SIR J. W. CROSSLEY, BART, M.P., AT LYNlfill,</head>
        <p>SIR J. W. CROSSLEY, BART, M.P., AT LYNlfill,</p>
        <p>The Lords and the Budget.</p>
        <p>Sir William J., Crossley, Bart, M.P., addressed a public meeting in tbe Drill Hall, Lymm, on Tuesday .evening. There was a large and enthusiastic audience, Mr. R. H. Gotobed presiding. In addition to ,Sir William and Lady Crossley, there were also present on the platform: Mrs. R. H. Gotobed, Mrs. J. Radcliffe, Miss West, Rev. A. Barrett, Messrs. W. E. Thompson, Frank Milne, J. D. B. Smith, J. Radcliffe and others. Mr. J. D. B. Smith, hon. secretary of the Lymm Liberal Association, read several letters of apology for non-attendance.</p>
        <p>THE END OF THE CHAPTER. Mr. Alfred Watkin wrote: Sir William Crossley will receive my vote at the coming general election. My opinion is that the present Government has done excellent work during its four years of office and would have (fork more but for the House of Lords. However, we are now getting to the end of the chapter and in a few weeks time we shall know if the august 'assembly is to be" mended or ended." ,</p>
        <p>" DESTRUCTION OF THE VETO."</p>
        <p>Mr. G. L. WeHord rote: I should like to show my appreciation of the King's recognition of the worth of Sir William in the Baronetcy which has been bestowed on him. In the next place, I should have been glad of the opportunity of saying to Sir William in the presence of my L)mm fellow Liberals and voters that I bope the Covernment are sincere in their inten- tion to make an end of the veto of the House of Lords. It is time this power of unrepresentative men to block and destroy the Bills brought into Parliament by Liberal ,Governments should be ended once for all. Destruction of the veto must be the policy of the Liberal party. Unless the Government mean business and mean it thoroughly, I for one shall not care even to vote for a Liberal candidate at an election. Even the Budget must give place to this matter cv destroying the power of the Lords. TfiE STRUGGLE FOR FREEDOM."</p>
        <p>Mr. William Long wrote from NVindermere : It would have given me much pleasure to have supported our good member, Sir William Crossley, at Lymm on Tuebday evening, and to have joined in hearty congratu- lations to him and Lady Crossley en the well-merited honour lately bestowed by the King—an honour richly deserved (if ouly for winning the seat for the Altrincham division to the Liberal party, a great feat in itself.) ,At this special crisis I hope you will have a good muster to strengthen his hands in the coming important struggle for freedom from the domination of the House of Lords, which we believe can only have that end. I have no doubt that Altrincham fully recog- niseb its obligations to its member, and will be proud to retain his services so long as he wishes. For myself, it was in 1868 that T first took a part in the candidature of Mr. John L. Warren, for what was then the Mid- Cheshire Division, and I feel now that I must stand aside from the active work of politics and leave it to younger men, though my heart is still in the cause of Liberal principles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>PEOPLE'S CAUSE.</head>
        <p>THE PEOPLE'S CAUSE.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said his first duty was to tender to Sir William Crossley their warm congratulations upon the honour which the King had been pleased to confer upon him. (Applause). It was not a political honour but a well-merited honour, one which Was the due recognition of a life well spent in high and honourable citizenship. (Applause). They met at a very moment- ous time. That night he supposed the Second Chamber would reverberate with the cheers of the "back woodsmen." The Budget would be killed. For the first time for three hundred years the Peers were stand- ing between the Throne and the Commons and by their action were refusing tbe supplies which the King asked the Commons for. They were taking upon themselves something which they had absolutely no right to do. Why was it they objected to this Budget? What were the taxes which were so obnoxious? They were the land taxes and the liquor taxes and if only those taxes had been missing nothing would have been heard of the opposition to the Budget. He believed that the land had too long escaped its fair share of taxation, and tbe the time had come when the broad back should bear the heavy burden and the poor should receive more consideration. The Lords were indulging in the last desperate throw of the gambler in the hope that when the people were appealed to they would return to power a majority pledged to tariff reform. He believed that to be a vain and delusive hope,but if it should happen the Peers would see a way of shelving their responsi- bilities and placing the burden upon the necessities of the poor. He thought they would be disappointed. (Hear, hear.) There was nothing more contemptible than the action of these wealthy men, many of them rich beyond the dreams of avarice, in trying to evade their just share of taxation. It was a most wicked thing, and they must fight it to the finish. They had long tolerated, with very sore hearts and uneasy minds, the, spectacle of the Lords mutilating and rejecting Liberal measures—measures aiming at social reform and the perfecting of our educational system and dealing also with licensing and other questions. They were determined not to toler- ate hereditary taxing masters. (Applause). The people's representatives must have sole control of the nation's purse. (Applause). The curtain would he rung down that night on a victory for the Lords, but as Lord Morley put it in his noble speech, it would not fall upon the completed drama; the note would be sounded for a very angry and perhaps prolonged battle. Liberals did not intend to flinch from the conflict and</p>
        <p>their message to the Government was: "Stand firm. Be loyal to the people's cause and the people will be loyal to you." (Applause).</p>
        <p>SIR WILLIAM CROSSLEY AND THE CRISIS. Sir William Crossley,who was very cordially greeted. began his speech with a reference to the constitutional crisis: This is a day of days. It is an epoch-making day of all others. This is a day when a step will be taken, one way or another, which will make history and I suppose you want to he in at it. (Hear, hear.) So do I. (Applause.) I shall go to London early on Thursday to be there in order to back up my Government in taking the resolution that they will take. There is no doubt, I cuppose, that the Lords to-night will reject the Budget. They tell me that even to-day at the clubs in Manchester there has been some betting that they would not—that they may at last turn and avoid a revolution which will be so bed for the Lords and which must be so bad for trade I are afraid. I don't care for the Lords, but Ido care for trade. It would spoil our improving trade and so I should be delighted if at the last moment they were to withdraw. But I am afraid that is too much to expect from the pride of a peer—the leading peer.who has gone F.O for and who has done so, I believe, against his own will. If this Budget is thrown out there will come a struggle which you will remember all the days of your lives. I am going to be in at it if I can. T hope you are going to follow and back me up. (Applause.) There never will be an election when I shall need your backing more. All the powers of the enemy will be exerted against us. Think of it. All the landlords with their acres and the influence they possess; all the drink trade con- centrated upon us with all their millions ; all the tariff reformers who see in this opportunity the only chance of earring their nostrums, knowing that our Budget is so good that if we prevail and carry it TARIFF REFORM WILL DISAPPEAR for another century. Then think of all the dwellers in eaves, discontented with the Government—men like Sir Robert Perks, who have not got all they wanted and they got millions. Still again, there are all those on whose toes unfortunately we have trampled in trying to bring out reforms and do the good we said we would do. They will all be against us and they will come from all sides—a great continuous flow and a mighty power. And what have we got on our side? Well, we have got the voice of the people. (Applanse.) We are fighting for the people's rights and if we fight on the Budget, as I suppose we will after the Lords have thrown it out, we shall fight on the greatest principle of the English constitution— the ' right of the people to be taxed through their elected representatives. (Applause.) Why should the Lords tax us? We cannot get hold of a Lord and show him we are angry. We cannot bring votes against him. He is there for ever, but if I vex you. you can soon put your heel on my neck and get rid of me. (Lenghter.) Therefore the poople claim the right to be taxed by their own representa- tives. The Lords put it in a very specious way. They say that there are new things here and that they won't take the responsibility of passing the Budget without showing it to the people. It is a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>VERY CLF.VER _RUSE</head>
        <p>VERY CLF.VER _RUSE</p>
        <p>indeed but I am sure you are quite clever enough to see through it. (Applause.) What does that mean ? That they will oblige us to appeal to the people,— because there is no such thing as referendum in this country. I think Switzerland is the only country with the referendum, where you can. take one Bill to the country and get their opinion on it. So the Lords know that when they say they will appeal to the people. it means they will make us destroy our own Bill. From the moment we dissolve Parliament, our Budget is dead. About 12.30 to-night the sky will be red with rockets in London and all the great centres, sent up to show that the Budget is dead, or rather that the Lords have cast it down. Very well,then, let us pluck up our courage and pull up our socks—(laughter) —and go in for work. (Applause.) Sir William pro- ceeded to explain the Budget in some detail. He said that Mr. Balfour told them that if he had his way he would take out the land cud liquor taxes, substituting for them tariff referm, and adopt the other taxes of the Budget. The other people did not want to he taxed at all—they had only to read Lord Rothschild's speech to find that out. Dealing with the land clauses, Sir William strongly urged the justice of a portion of the wealth which the landlords derived through the enterprise and necersities of the people being returned to the com- munity in the way of taxes. Ho pointed out that the land proposals would not take a single sixpence which tbe landlord had already got; they would only apply to value that would accrue after the Butlget had passed. It was always a painful thing to ftx•anyone, but they were bound to have the money for the navy old age pensions, better education, and a thousand and one other things. The Chancellor refused to impose more taxation on the food</p>
        <p>of the people; instead he sought a new basis of taxa- tion. Mr. Austen Chamberlain made a fierce speech in which he blamed the Government for smiling while their (the Opposition's) houses were burning. "I could not help, laughing at his metaphor," continued Sir William, because, as a matter of fact, if there was any" truth in it there were more houses burning on our side than on his, for more of us were hit by the taxes. I am hit by every one of thern,except those on liquor and royalties." Sir William Robson gave utterance to a pregnant sentence when he said the Government came to the conclusion that by going in for tariff reform: they would lose more by income tax than they would gain by tariffs. He also said tariffs would lay burdens, on general necessities and thus constitute a tax on labour in its most dangerous form. The Government resolved to tax unearned increment and in doing so they ,afould not tax what a man bad got but wholly unex- pected wealth. Continuing, Sir William said the Tories; denounced the Budget as spoliation and robbery, but they talked as if it were highly ethical and moral, highly consistent with shat they called great world politics, to go in for taxing the people under the head of tariff reform, and taxing the operations of trade which meant</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>TAXING WAGES.</head>
        <p>TAXING WAGES.</p>
        <p>Lord Rothschild, who was said to be the richest man-in England, objected to the super-tax, the death duties and everything else. Where had the Government to go to. get the money? The only people who made very little objection were the poor, and they were willing to stand their shot like men. (Applause.) If they apportioned' the new taxation, they would find that Si millions were placed on the rich and 4 millions on the poor. That meant that they were putting 4 millions on 40 millions of people,, while the 4 million rich people had to pay SA. millions. He thought that was very fair, heciuse the 4 millions were very well able to pay and it would make very little difference to them. The Tories, con- tinued Sir William, wale trying to</p>
        <p>STEAL THEIR CLOTHES. Sir Gilbert Parker had written a book about it. Why,. he (Sir William) thought the Liberals bad been struggling for small holdings,to give land to the people. Now the Tories wee on the job. They wanted to give the people land—any amount of it. (Laughter.) He was glad because whether the Liberals gave it to tbe people or not the people were going to get land. As illustrating the great advantage, of land reform, Sir William referred to the, extraordinary development of Denmark. A Century ago Denmark was prostrate at the feet of the landlords, the people being in a state of serfdom. The coantry woke up to its position and to-day nine-tenths of the agricultural land belonged to the people, and was dis- tributed into something like 75.000 small holdings. The result was that never was there such prosperity as there was in Denmark to-day ; it was the best-farmed, the best managed, the happiest and most contented country in Europe. There wal not a pauper in the whole place. The Liberal Govetnment had been work • ing on those lines. They realised that the way to save. England wes to get more people living on the land and to get'more out of the soil. Uuder a had system of land tenure we had neglected that questionlong enough; and had been importing millions of eggs and other produce which, under better management, we ought to have been able to produce to a great extent for our— selves. Sir William spoke of the proposed</p>
        <p>LABOUR EXCHANGES AND STATA INSURANCE SCHEME pointing out that the most demoralising feature of our present system was that the poor fellow out of work had to tramp all over the country, depreciating• his constitution and making himself less competent for a show ion. He claimed that the schemes which Mr. Churchill had in hand would lessen the terrible misery. of unemployment. So far as he knew we were the- only country in the world which had lagged behind in that matter, and we wanted to do something by these, proposals to make England a better place to live in.. If the Liberals were beaten at the next election</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>TARIFF RMFORAI</head>
        <p>TARIFF RMFORAI</p>
        <p>would be a certainty—it the Liberal Government Nvas returned the Budget would be passed, and all its beneficent legislation would be continued. There was. any amount of work before them. He was longing to- see a re-arrangement of the poor laws. Turning to, Tariff Reform Sir William sald it was the worse thing that could happen to the country. He admitted that a young country just beginning to manufacture required Protection or else its legs would he cut from under it by countries like England ; but England was the oldest manufacturing country in the world and the needed no Protection. Sir William instanced the motor car trade, contending- that if tariffs had been imposed the English manu- facturers would have taken things more easily and, would have taken longer to arrive at anything like. their present perfection. Free Traders said 'Let foreign cars come in until the English can make them."' So the English buckled to, copied the foreign cars and improved on them, and now they were second to none. (Applause.) He was told on good authority that the- total number of people engaged in the English motor trade was about 1,000,000. (Applause.) What an enormous trade we had made of that without Protec- tion ! (Applause.) In his opinion, an Englishman should scorn Protection. If they put on a tariff they were bound to make things dear in the country which_ imposed D. Impressing the gravity of the present situation upon his hearers, Sir William said they had to fight for the right to protect thernsAves from taxa- .</p>
        <p>tion by the Lords and to maintain the supremacy of the Commons. The election wns upon them and every vote would be of the greatest value. All be asked of them was that they would carefully consider the whole case and conscientiously give their votes for the right and for the benefit of the country. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>VOTE OF CONFIDENCE.</head>
        <p>VOTE OF CONFIDENCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Frank Milne proposed a vote of thanks to Sir William Crossley and of confidence in the Govern- ment. Ile gave an exhsustive review of the work of the Government in the direction of social reform and said their record entitled them to ask, with clear up- right hearts, for the support of the people. (Applause). The workingmen were paying their share of taxation cheerfnlly. He (the speaker) had gone about the country is gre d deal and he had not heard any whinings on their part. On the contrary he had seen something which in his long political experience had never been equalled. In the last dog days of this year— if there were any dog days—some of the largest halls in the land were crammed to suffocation with enthusiastic audiences of working people and others, all ready to support this great Budget. (Applause). The Rev. A. Barrett seconded and the vote was passed with about six dissentients.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. W. E. Thompson seconded by Mr. J. idclitYe, a heat ty vote of tlitaks was pAsied to the Chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH 2ND V. ALTII IN`CHA7II RGARET'S.—PIayed at Altrincbam on Saturday. The game was keenly fought, but Stcckton Heaih had much the better of the argument, goals being scored 1:y Hodgkinson, Smith, Meldrum, Dodds and Richmond. Herd played a good game in goal, clearing effectively on more than one occasion. When the uhistle blew the Heathens had scored seven goals to their opponents two.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>COCOA IN THE CHRISTMAS HAMPER.</head>
        <p>COCOA IN THE CHRISTMAS HAMPER.</p>
        <p>Christmas is drawing near, and soon many of our readers will be preparing small hampers for their friends, or to give to thuse who would not otkerwise participate in the joys of Christmas. In former years when the value of Cocoa, was less widely known, it was invariably the custom to include a pound of tea among the other articles in the yearly bumper. No doubt this was acceptable, but to-day a tin of Cocoa is often included by those who desire to give a beverage of recognised food value—one that will do good and at the same time give pleasure to the recipients. There are many reasons why this should be so. A Cocoa, like Rowntree's, is an appetising drink, and a food too ; it is nourishing and sustaining, it warms and cheers. Besides it has the delicious "Rowntree Flavour, which would be much appreciated by the recipients of these Christmas Hampers.</p>
