      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>NOTES BY " MUST."</head>
        <p>NOTES BY " MUST."</p>
        <p>• Mere bad luck and an aggravating defeat by a single point was Warrington's fate at Wakefield. They lost Tilley early in the game when they were showing all round superiority, and this unquestion- ably gave Wakefield the victory. Tilley had a finger broken in taking the ball from the feet of Bennett, the Wakefield right wing, and took no further part in the game. His retirement took the heart out of Warring- ton for a time, and the homesters pressed their advantage to such effett that 3 goals and 2 tries were rucorded in Trick time. Warrington made a rally and O'Malley scored 'a try which Thomas failed to improve, and the interval arrived with Tr ii,ity leading by the comfortable margin of 12 points to 3.</p>
        <p>The game seemed as good as over from a Wakefield point of view, but Warrington thought differently. They put.their batls into the work on resuming and soon gave Wakefield something to think about. Frawley raced over after fine combination, but again the goal kicking failed. Wakefield found themselves getting over-played and some despet ate merisures resulted in Anton being sent off. Warrington played up grandly and another fine piece of work was finished by Frawb y behind the posts, and Fish lidded a goal. Only one roiut now separated the teams, and for the final ten minutes there was a continual bombardment of the home line. Nearly all the Warrington backs were within inches of scoring, and Trinity were the luckiest team in the world to keep their one point lead. Warrington made a gallant effort and by sewing three tries to two they retired with the honours of the gam,) if not with the paints.</p>
        <p>I have given Tilley's accident as the main cause of the defeat, and so it was. Warrington did temark- ably well to come so near victory with so heavy a handi(ap. But they ought to have won and they would have won if they had had a goal-kicker. This has been the weakness all the season and on Saturday it cost two valuable points and a leading position in the league. NEither Fish nor Thomas is as reliable as last season, and goals galore are being missed in heartrending fashion. Perhaps Tilley's accident may be a blessing in disguise, forllclntyre should be equal to the full back position by this and 'he at least, can kick goals. I think the Committee are bound to play "Mac "—notwitb4anding ideas about Taylor—and give him every opportunity to kick goals. By careful play he can, I am sure, win the confidence of,the club as a reliable custodian.</p>
        <p>Rather cur:ously Warrington's Australian players go all three tries-0 Malley one, and Frawley two. Both played cons' icuously good football and seem to be improving every watch. Cartwlight also played a good centre game and showed that he is fit to fill Taylor's position at any time. The forwards did some grand work in the second half and were much ahead of the beme lot. Fish was lazy or he would have got his usual try and won the match. Why he should kick instead of run when he is in Yorkshire is a puzzle to his arlmhers, and it only encourages people to think he is 'crating done. He could have done much better on Saturday, and only the best is expected of him.</p>
        <p>The League table up todate is as follows : For. A gst._ Per-</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. (31-. P. P. G. T. P. centage Oldham -.12 10 2 0...23 49 193...11 11 61... 83.33 Runcorn 11 8 2 1...11 24 100_15 21 93... 77.27 Wakefi'dT. 12 9 3 0...27 36 162... 9 20 78... 75.00 Salford l2 9 3 0...26 37 163...16 22 98... 75.00 Leeds l2 9 3 0...32 39 179...16 21 104... 75.00 Warringtonl3 9 3 1...21 53 207...11 16 76... 73.07 Keighley 11 8 3 0...21 31 160...1G 17 83... 72.72 Hull 14 10 4 0...32 48 208...16 29 119... 71.42 Leigh lO ,6 2 2...11 21. 85... 8 7 37... 70.00 Huddersfl'dl2 8 1 0...19 43 167...14 21 91... 66.66 Hunslet —ll 7 4 0...16 30 122...23 13 85... 63.61 Hull K.lt 15 9 5 1...39 32 174...22 31 135... 63133 Halifax l2 7 5 0...21 28 132...22 25 199... 58.33 Wigan 9 5 1 0...21 32 138...10 14. 62... 55.55 St. Helensll 5 4 2...28 40 176...21. 45 117... 54.541 Batley 11 5 6 0...21 21 111...16 19 89... 4515 Ebbw Valell 4 6 1...10 19 77...18 19 93... 4090 Swinton —ll 4 6 1...12 25 99...15 28 111— 40.90 Dewsbury 12 4 7 1...17 18 88...17 31 127... 3750 Widnes ll. 3 6 2... 8 11 49...15 27 111... 3636 Bradford Nll 4 8 1...14 13 67...21 33 141... 31.61 Barrow lO 3 7 0-11 15 67...22 34 146... 30.00 Rochdale 11 3 8 0... 8 17 67...22 34 146... 27.27 Broughton 12 3 9 0...21 23 111.. 31 49 215... 25.00 York l3 3 10 0...22 18 98...28 43 185... 23.07 Merthyr -13 1 11 1...10 13 59...26 47 193... 1P53 Treherbertlo 010 0... $ 12 42...26 41 185...10000 Bramley ll 011 0... 8 11 49...33 47 207-100'00</p>
        <p>If Warrington had won at Wakefield instead of just losing, they would to-day have been second instead of sixth, with a percentage of 80... I am not without confidence that they will regain the leadership by the end of the year. To do so they must win every match this m-mtb, and the task should not prove any too big for them. The Christmas day match at Leigh is the stiffest, but I expect Warrington to show the Mather-lane crowd a thing or two tbat day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>MORE ABOUT STUNTZ.</head>
        <p>MORE ABOUT STUNTZ.</p>
        <p>Clear evidence of the enthusiasm for football in Warrington was provided on Saturday, when about 7,000 people assembled at Wilderspool to see Stuntz play his first game on English soil, and £59 were taken in twopences and fourpences. The Australian created an excellent impression. He strips well, and in the course of the game he showed wonderful pace and plenty of pluck. There can be little doubt that he is the speediest player in the Union. Unfortunately he was troubled with sickness as a result of his long voyage and want of training, and did not turn out again after the interval.</p>
        <p>An interesting interview with Stuntz by " Forward " appeared in the "Athletic News" on Monday. In the course of the article the writer says : Before touching on personal matters, he informed me that Australia's call to the Northern Union could not be ignored. The Colony was particularly keen on seeing Northern footballers, and they cannot understand the delay in accepting what they regard as generous teams. Two Colonial teams .have visited England for a six months' tour on a guarantee of £3,000. "Australia," proceeded Stuntz, "offers 4,000 for a three months' tour, and a good time., Your men will breathe real air, will have room to play, and, above all, have firm and level grounds." No bogholes in Australia," interposed O'Malley. Daniel Frawley added his •opinion. "Northern Union players- are speedy and clever in tactics, but their play will improve wonderfully on Australian and Nen Zealand grounds. There will be some thrills, and the Australian football public are look-</p>
        <p>ing forward toa huge treat next season." I questioned Stunts regarding the reported dis- sensions in League circles. He partly confirmed the reports, but declared that harmony now pre- vailed, and the possibility of an English team for next season was uniting all sections. John Stunts, who is 21 years of age next June, commenced his football career in 1904 when he played with the Womerah club in the "B " grade competition of the Juniors. His club were champions, and they also took honours in the " A " grade competition the season after. In 1905 ho joined the Eastern Suburbs second grade team, and success again attended the efforts of Stunts and his colleaguas, for they won that competition. In the same season his abilities were so pronounced that he received promotion into the first grade, and was now fairly established in New South Wales football. In 1906 he became a permanent member of the Eastern Suburbs team, and in 1907 secured honours for his State, playing for New South Wales against Queensland both at Sydney and Brisbane.</p>
        <p>Then followed the formation of the League, the breaking away from the Rugby Union to play the Northern Union game and the three matches played against Baskerville's combination at Sydney prior to the professional "All Blacks" leaving for England. Stuntz took part in all games and also in the return matches after the team games, completed their English tour. He was -out of football for a season owing to injuries, and various engagements, but resumed last season, playing four games for Australia and one for New South Wales.</p>
        <p>Stuntz is a player who scores tries and in these five representative games bad ten to his credit. His best record in a club game is five tries, in an inter-Calonial match three tries, and in a junior grade final for Womerah he 013C3 scored all the nineteen points made by bis side. I may say that English clubs have sadly depleted the Eastern Suburb's team for Stuntz, Frawley, O'Malley, Rosenfeld, and Brown were all members of the famous Sydney combination. Stuntz is a club colleague of Messenger, and has played for two seasons at wing three-quarter back, with" Daily" as his centre.</p>
        <p>My story would not be complete were I to omit reference to the great reputation of Stuntz as a runner. In Australia a professional runner is allowed to play amateur Rugby football, and Stuntz in 1905 was recognised as a professional runner. Ile is credited with doing 120 yards and 130 yards in even time, but does not himself claim to have accomplished that feat. He is the recognised quarter mile hurdler of Australia, is a champion, and allows 42 yards in the quarter mile. He is also a splendid swimmer, but of course lost his amateur status in the natatorial sport.</p>
        <p>Thu matches for to-day with the corresponding results last season. are es follow :</p>
        <p>37 8 Broughton Rangers v. Barrow. 2-13 Hull Kingston Rovers v. Batley. 11-14 Bradford v. Huddersfield. Bramley v. Merthyr Tydvil. 36 0 Dewsbury v. York-. 8— 3 Halifax v. Keighley. 44-10 Warrington v. Hull. .Hunslet v. St. Helens. 8— 0 Oldham v. Leeds. 27 5 Salford v. Leigh. 8— 5 Rochdale Hornets v. Swinton. 13— 7 Runcorn v. Widnes. Wigan V. Wakefield Trinity. _ One of the most popular matches on the Warring- ton programme is that down for decision to-day, and it is to be hoped that the gale will have blown itself out before the kick-off. Hull always play fine football here, and though they got such a heavy beating last season, they were quite clever on the attack. At present they are going very strongly, and have the honour of beating Warrington by a point for the highest aggregate in the League. Those figures must be altered to-day. I think War- rington are pretty sure to win, but they will have to play for all, they are worth and not slacken a moment until the " final " sounds. The international match at Wakefield deprives Warrington of the services of Jenkins, O'Neill and Shugars,and Tilley is an enforced absentee.</p>
        <p>The selected side is a strong one and an interest- ing one. The cotnnuttee are tak;ng the opportunity of trying Taylor as custcdian, while Frawley and Stuntz ate to be partners on the right wing. They know each other's play, and I hope they will be a success together. Warrington have bad to rely too long on the left wing for the bulk of the scoring. A keen attack on the other side will make the team immensely more dangerous.</p>
        <p>The Warrington team will be : Taylor, full back; Fibb, Dickenson, Frawley and Stuntz, three-quarters; Lloyd and Brooks, half backs; Boardman, Thomas, O'Malley, Naylor, Chesters and Belton, forwards.</p>
        <p>The "A " team has a Combination match with Leigh Shamrocks, and should easily win with the following strong team : Mclntyre, full back ;` Smith, Cartwright, Jordan and Bradshaw, three-quarters; Kay and Dabatith, half-backs; Harmer, Bradshaw, Worrall, P. Boardman, Hewitt and Gibbs, forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>ST. HELEN'S MUSICAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>ST. HELEN'S MUSICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The members of the St. Helens Musical Society, with their famous orchestra, gave a most interesting concert on Monday night at the Town Hall. Unfortunately the attendance again fell below the necessary paying standard, and the society will have to devise means for dealing with a growing deficit. rhe chorus, under Dr. Siddall, acquitted themselves very creditably in several interesting items. The soloists were Miss Gertrude Lonsdale, Mr. J. A. Walmsley, and Mr. Joseph Lycett</p>
      </div>
