      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>Results of Saturday's Matches.</p>
        <p>YORKSHIRE SENIOR CUP.-FINAL.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. G. T. P. HUDDERSFIELD 3 5 21 BATLEY At Headingley.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE SENIOR CUP.—FINAL. WIGAN At Broughton.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Rochdale Hornets 3 3 15 Leeds 1 1 5</p>
        <p>Hull _ 3 418 Broughton R. ... 0 0 0 _</p>
        <p>Hunslet 1 4 14 Yoilt- Wakefield T. Oldham 5 7 31 St. Helms 1 1 5 Bradford N'thern 1 1 5 Hull K. R. Runcorn Salford 1 3 11 Swieton 0 1 3 Keighley Halifax 1 1 5 Merthyr Tydvill 0 1 3</p>
        <p>YORKSHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>Dewsbnry A</p>
        <p>LANCASHI RE COMBINATION. Wigan A Oldham A 3 5 21 Leigh A Wigan Highfield... 0 1 3 Broughton R. A 0 1 3 Ince Runcorn A .... 12 8 Leigh Shamrocks 01 3 Warrington's Hard Luck at Wakefield.</p>
        <p>Warrington went to Wakefield on Saturday, and after losing Tilley in the first five minutes through a broken finger, they were beaten by 12 points to 11 points. Both sides had absentees. Warrington played Cart- wright and Lloyd for Taylor and Jenkins, and the Trinity bad substitutes for Metcalfe and Slater. The weather was fine, but the Yorkshire Cup Final at Leeds spoiled the attendance, which was not more than 2,000. The teams and officials were Warrington: Tilley, back; Fish, Dickenson, Cart- wright and Frawley, three-quarter backs; Brooks and Lloyd, half-backs ; Boardman, O'Neill, O'Malley, Thomas, Belton and Shiners, forwards. Wakefield Trinity: Holmes, back; Bennett, Lynch, Poynton and Sunderland, three-quarter backs; Newbould and Stephenson, half-backs; Anton, G. Taylor, J. Taylor, Kershaw, Burton and Killinbeck, forwards. Referee, Mr. J. F. May, St. Helens ; touch-judges, Messrs. H. Ferrer, Keighley, and H. Schofield, Leeds. Warrington started in strong fashion and the home team were at once forced back. Newbould had his kick charged near . the, line, and Holmes was lucky to save from the Warrington forwards. Warrington maintained the attackby good football, but only about five minutes had elapsed when disaster occurred. Tilley was picking up when Bennett made a reckless kick at the ball and caught the Warrington custodian's hand, with the result that one of his fingers was broken. Tilley had to retire and take no further part in the game O'Neill coming out of the forwards to take the full back position. Wakefield took advantage of their extra man in the pack and rushed' the game to the Warrington end. O'Neill defended pluckily, but had no chance with a penalty kick which *Homes converted into a goal from near half-way. The Trinity team continued to have the better of it, and another score was not long in forthcoming, for rapid transfers, started by Sunderland, let in Poynton, Holmes again adding the goal. Warrington rallied finely, and dashing forward work resulted in a fine try to O'Malley. Thomas missed the goal. Tke game was carried on at a rapid rate, and after Warrington had again threatened danger through Brooks, Stephenson kicking across, Lynch gathered well and scored, for Holmes to land his third goal. Fish was tackled in possession. but effected a fine recovery, and near the interval a fine effort was made for a score. Holmes, however, defended well, and at half-time Wakefield led by three goals two tries (12 points), to Warrington, one try (three points). George Taylor started operations for Wakefield, and a very weak kick by O'Neill let Trinity in. The Warrington forwards rose to the occasion, and the ball was taken the full length of the field; where the Wakefield custodian, was responsible for a magnificent save. Scrummages took place on the Wakefield line, but Brook's pass went astray. Sunderland relieved to near the centre. Wakefield now attacked strongly but overran the ball with only O'Neill in front. Warrington responded brilliantly in the closing stages of the game and by delightful passing tuey kept Trinity on the defence. First Frawley got splendidly away after clever passing, and scored a grand try. Thomas missed the goal shot. Next came a magnificent burst by the backs and Frawley again crossed, near the posts. Fish added the goal, so that Warrington's record was within a single point of that of the home team. The Wakefield defence was hard pressed to the close. Anton was sent off for striking, and the Warrington men made desperate efforts to get the winning point. Several men were grassed within inches of the line, but they could not manage another try, and Wakefield won by 3 goals, 2 tries, (12 points) to Warrington 1 goal 3 tries (11 points). RUNCORN WIN AT BARROW.</p>
        <p>At Barrow, before 1,000 spectators. Both teams had several changes, and the constant rain showers made accurate handling of the ball very difficult. Play fluctuated from end to end, Gifford and Richardson being prominent with clean fielding. Dobson and Hannah gave Barrow a footing in their opponents' quarters, but this was neutralised by loose dribbles by the Runcorn forwards. Brown made a capital save, and even play followed. At the end of thirty-live minutes Runcorn pressed, and Hughes scored near touch,Butter- worth's kick falling short. Interval—Runcorn 3 points, Barrow nil. - _</p>
        <p>In the second portion Hughes made a fine attempt to score again, Dobson saving on the Barrow line. The home halves held the hall too long, and short kicking was favourable to the visitors. White set his backs in motion, a round of passing being checked by Gifford. The Cestrians persisted, however, and from a pass Walker scored. Butterworth improved. Result : Runcorn 1 goal and 2 tries (8 points), Barrow nil. Teams and officials : Barrow—H. Gifford, back ; R. Bamber, D. Brown, G. Dobson, and S. Treloar, three-quarter backs; J. P. Burns and W. Barns, half backs; A. Logan, D.Thompson, C. Brown, J. Dobson, A. Hannah, and S. Little,forwards. Ruucorn—Richardson, back; Deakin, Butterworth, Moran, and Hughes, three-quarter backs; White and Barmer, half backs; Walker Kennedy, Langley, McDonald, Williams, and Dixon, 'forwards. Referee, Mr. J. C. Lumley, Leeds. Touch judges, Messrs. W. Cross, Freston,and C. Abraham,Kendal. SR. HELENS COLLAPSE BEFORE OLDHAM. The meeting of St. Helens and Oldham attracted a record crowd of 8,000 to the" Saints" ground. The conditions were very bad for the handling game when Pope started for the "Saints "in a deluge of rain. Oldham got dangerous through Anlezark and from some grand forward play Turtill failed to gather the greasy leather, and Ferguson opened the score, and kicked a grand goal. Immediately afterwards the home side took the ball to the Oldham end, and from a rush of players Flanagan scored, Barton failing to convert. Half-time score :—Oldham 1 goal and 1 try (5 points), St Helens 1 try (3 points). Changing ends, Ferguson started, and from some loose play in the visitors' quarters Jimmy Greenwood picked up and made the scores equal with a dropped coal. By tricky play Anlezark scored twice in quick succession, Ferguson failing in front of the, posts and Jardine improving one. Oldham adapted themselves to the miserable conditions which prevailed more so than the "Saints," and from a dribble several home players failed to touch down, the result being that Ferguson dropped on the leather. Jardine goaled. Oldham again crossed through Jardine, the same player adding the extra points. The visitors coming with a rush at the close. Dean and Avery added to their score. Jardine was successful in improving one from the touch line. Result :—Oldham 5 goals and 7 tries (31 points), St. Helens 1 goal and 1 try (5 points). Teams:— St. Helens—H. S. Turtill, back; J. Flanagan, Jas. Greenwood, C. Creevey, and T. Barton, three-quarter backs ; "Mat " Creevey and H. Greenwood, half backs, J. Prescott, E. Helsby, W. Briers,J. Pope, J. F. Mavity and W. Bradley, forwards. Oldham—A. E. Wood, back; G. F. tyson, Sid Deane, T. Llewellyn and G. W. Smith, three-quarter backs ; T. White and E. A. Anlezark, half backs; J. Ferguson, A. E. Avery, W. B. Jardine, J. 0 wlns, T. Helm and A. Smith forwards. Referee, Mr. R. Robinson, Bradford.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE CUP FINAL. LEIGH'S POOR SHOW AGAINST WIGAN.</p>
        <p>There was plenty of incident and 15,000 spectators at Broughton. Leigh were defending for the greater portion of the game, but at the interval were only 3 points behind the " C6lliers." Their opponents, however, were the better stayers and the better players. Notwithstanding this superiority, the issue was open enough till right on time, four tries being added in the course of as many minutes Leigh being utterly non- plussed in this period. The Wigan forwards were more vigorous, and the backs showed a greater amount of science. Leytham was the mainspring of the Wigan attack, scoring a try and kicking a couple of goals. Miller (2) and Jones whose work all through was admirable, also got over, and Francis was another prominent player who scored. S. Johnson obtained a clever try for Leigh, this being converted by Battersby, while Jenkins and Clarkson made continual efforts, which, however, were unrewarded. Wigan, 2 goals, 6 tries (22 points). Leigh 1 goal,l try (5 points). YORKSHIRE CUP FINAL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>BATLEY OUT-PLAYED.</head>
        <p>BATLEY OUT-PLAYED.</p>
        <p>The result in the corresponding Yorkshire event at Headlingley, was also pronounced, Huddersfield taking 21-0 out of Batley. The players rather overstepped</p>
        <p>the bounds, and two were sent off, while a series of regrettable accidents put Batley in a very weak position. They made a gallant fight of it, bnt their opponents were the better lot, and the odds were altogether too great. A crowd of 24,000 watched Huddersfield gradually piling up the score, thanks to Wrigley, Rosenfeldt, Ainley, Sykes, Kitchen, and Holroyd. Batley put up a good defence in the first half, and their tackling throughout was of a high order.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH. STUNTZ MAKES HIS FIRST APPEARANCE.</p>
        <p>There was a record" A" team gate at Warrington on Saturday, when Cadishead were the visitors, and Stunts made his first appearance in the Warrington colours. He had a great reception from quite 7,000 people, the bulk of whom had come specially to see the Australian sprinter. Cadishead had a strong team. including several men who have played for Warrington, and they made no secret of their intentions to check the champions if possible. The visitors started strongly and Mclntl re caused uneasiness when he passed back on his own line, Daintith saving. Smart footwor's relieved the line, and Stunts made a great effort from a pass by Kay, but Harris brought him down. The home side forced play to the line, where Kay tricked the opposition and got over near the posts. Mclntyre added the goal. Cadishead got back to centre, through Forster and Kidd, but were outplayed in the back division. Stunts followed up a kick with great speed and collared the full back, but there was no more scoring before the interval, when Warrington led by 5 points to nil.—The homesters resumed without the Australian, who was feeling unwell, and Hewitt took the wing position. Warrington were soon attacking and Mclntyre early placed a penalty goal, closely followed by Daintith dropping a goal. The Cadishead men were outclassed and before the close Landor raced in for a couple of tries. Warrington won by three goals three tries (15 points) to Cadishead nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>RYLANDS RECS V. ST. HELENS RANGERS.</head>
        <p>RYLANDS RECS V. ST. HELENS RANGERS.</p>
        <p>Rylands Rees played the St. Helens Rangers away last Saturday, and beat them by three points to nil.</p>
        <p>PEARSON AND KNOWLES V. STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Pearson and Knowles played Stockton Heath away on Saeurdny last. Roiult : —Pearson and Knowles, 5; Stockton Heath, 13.</p>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAIL</p>
        <p>Pearson and Knowles v. Westfield Amateurs, at home. Rylanps Rees v. Thatto Heath, away.'</p>
      </div>
