        <p>BOOTS' 6D. DIARIES FOR 1910.</p>
        <p>We would call the attention of our readers to these wonderfully cheap and useful Diaries published by Boots Cash Chemists and obtainable at each of their establishments. There is nothing better on the market at the price. For those who do much writiug the "Scribbling Diary "is worth its price for the blotting paper alone. The "Home Diary " is indispensable to housekeepers who are wise enough to keep accounts, while its information, legal and otherwise in relation to domestic affairs renders it a most valuable companion. The many thousands who have used the little pocket diary for engagements etc., for years, can speak from experience of its value.</p>
        <p>HERNIA.—The new Fluid Pad Truss never gets hard and is effectual in most difficult cases. For slight cases the new Waterproof Washable Truss, which is always clean and most durable, is strongly recommended.— WHITE &amp; WRIGHT, Surgical Instrument Makers, 93, Renshaw at., opposite top of Bold at., Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>FOOTBALL GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>BY B. DENNISON.</p>
        <p>ILLUSTRATED BY JACK DOD WORTH.</p>
        <p>It is easy to understand that the going of Vivian J. Woodward to Chelsea has not con- duced to pleasantness at Tottenham. It was as- sumed, with every good reason, when the fam- ous amateur at the end of last season declared that he had had enough of League football, that, except now and then, he would not engage in any match of importance; . and it was taken for granted—by the followers, of the 'Spurs, at all events—l cannot say that there is an inner history to Woodward transferring his services to Stamford Bridge; I have an idea that there is— that, if he should ever return to big football again, it would be with the team with whom he had made his reputation. But, whatever be the cause of Woodward emerging from virtual retirement, and his wearing the Chelsea colours, there are no two opinions about his re- appearance being genuinely welcomed. I am afraid that in their present mood the Chelsea followers as a whole will expect too much from their new player. They will be very misguided if they imagine for a moment -that Woodward play the part of a wizard, and, by his genius ammo make what I am sure is quite an ordinary team into a strong one. They may be sure of this, however, that wiless Brawn has lost the better part of his ability, they will have about as good a right wing as there is ia the country. But what of their defence? Here is the funda- mental weakness of - the team, and this will be made more strikingly apparent when Windridge</p>
        <p>and Hilsdon return to the attack. When they do the side will be top-heavy; excellent for- ward, very groggy behind. I daresay that any money within reason would be paid for good, sound defenders by the Chelsea management; but one knows too well that there is a dearth of good material. The most annoying part of the situation is that there •ale abundant riches but little talent if Sheffield people were asked, I have little doubt that they would say that Chel- sea are about as poor a team as has been to Owlerten this year. And one wonders what is going to happen in the West End of London. "Tiny" Joyce goes to Tottenham.</p>
        <p>After what took place in, their recent games it was inevi'able that Tottenham Hotspur should look round for a new goalkeeper. Joyce, who, because ho is so big and bulky—he is a second edition to the mountainous Foulke—is called "Tiny," has crossed over from Millwall to White Hart-lane, and I daresay he will do much better than Boreham. As a matter of fact, Tottenham have not had e great goal- keeper since Clawley left them to go to South- ampton, now many seasons ago, and who, so far as I know, has dropped out of the game. When I first knew Joyce he was playing in the Burton district, and it will be remembered that he once played for Blackburn Rovers. But his best work has been done for Millwall, who, without the great "Tiny" wilPngt seem the same. But</p>
        <p>Tirtip4mAN ‘siTion</p>
        <p>Joyce, Tottenham's New Recruit, Is popularly called "Tiny." He is a second edition of '• Baby" Foulkes.</p>
        <p>then Millwall are most excellently served by Carmichael. It may be taken for granted that Joyce is only the first of several recruits to tho Tottenham team, who, I cannot help thinking, were lucky to beat Sheffield United. The gods were certainly on their side in this game. How very completely and unaccountably a crack team can go flop was demonstrated by what happened to Manchester United against Notts Forest. The result-6 goals to 2 against the Cupholders—is the wonder of the season; but perhaps the most remarkable feature of the affair was that as many as 6 goals were scored in as many minutes—which is prodigious. Notts Forest, in this game at any rate, exploded the idea that goal- scoring is a lost art. Well, all this wonderful result proves is that no team can make such a sorry failure as a good one. The better the footballer the greater propensity has he for falling all of a heap. One remembers what Sunderland did to Newcastle on one memorable occasion last season.</p>
        <p>Crompton's Understudy. It might have been that Bradford City would not have beaten Blackburn. Rovers had Bob Crompton been able to play. Mind, when the territorial advantage is with the present York- shire club, it will take an exceptionally good side to beat them, but I cannot help thinking that. the splitting up of the great trio—Aslr- croft. Cowell, and Crompton had everything to do with the fall of the Rovers. It must not be imagined, however, that Suttie, who was called upon to deputise for the one and .only Crompton, is only good enough for a reserve team. Suttie is a young Scotsman for whom I have a high regard, and I know many clubs who would pay handsomely for him. It is a great pity that he has to fill the role of an emer- gency man. That he is left out of the Rovers' team is a fine tribute to the prowess of the wee Cowell.</p>
        <p>The Triumph of Dourness. There can be few features of the present posi- tion of the First- League table so strange to the man who has a weakneps for standing on " form " than the fact that Notts County are in the second position. No one will pretend that</p>
        <p>Notts Forest beat Manchester U</p>
        <p>0 to 2.</p>
        <p>they are a brilliant or unusually clever combi- nation. Ido not think that they would care to make claims to either virtue, but they can E. 3.3, with every truth that they are determined to do well. Their exalted position is a triumph of dourness. Everyone must wish the old club well, and for sentimental reasons the progrees which Notts Forest have made s very pleasing. And we have the old club, Preston North End, coming along in convincing style. It is like old times to see them beating Aston Villa; and one who has followed the doings of the young Celtic player, McLean, is wondering how he came to leave Glasgow. I do not think he hail failed to score in any one inatcn in winch he has piayea for the Deepdale club so far, and now Danson is well again, and with the help of Bannister, there is every promise of North End wunwpf Quite a re6pectel)le place. _</p>
