      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>FROM WARRINGTON TO WIEN AND BACK.</head>
        <p>FROM WARRINGTON TO WIEN AND BACK.</p>
        <p>WITH SOME IMPRESSIONS BY THE WAY. (SPECIALLY WRITTEN FOR "THE EXAMINER" BY ALDERMAN BENNETT).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>ARTICLE VII.</head>
        <p>ARTICLE VII.</p>
        <p>When morning came, the rain had passed away; we rose betimes and breakfasted, and, sauntering slowly by the great broad river, rolling now in sirift green splendour under the great iron bridge, we reached the landing stage immediately beyond it, and took mar places on the comfortable river steamer for the long day's sail to Wien. The day was dull at starting, but gradually the weather brightened, and the sun, which nad attended us with regal splendour all the way, except upon the previous afternoon and during that wet day an Stuttgart,once again asserted his supremacy, and never left us _ now (except at night) till we got back to England. And so we slipped sway from Linz, and its sweet sister city, Urfahr, on the other shore; and watched the massive castle on the hill behind- us, and the to*er of the great church which I had visited amidst the darkness disappearing gradually; and glided out into the level country which, in its still Sabbath morning calm, fell on any weary frame like some soft soothing hand, and filled me with a pensive sense of restfulness and peace. For I was quite tired out with all the manifold exertions of the week ; too tired to talk ; almost too tired to gaze at the continually changing scenery ; and so I seized a deck, chair, found a quiet corner at the stern of the big paddle steamer, and sat there dreaming to the soft plash of the bright green waters as the spires and houses of the city faded in the distance and the far blue Alps came into v!ew beyond the green and pleasant meadows through whose level pastures we slipped slowly</p>
        <p>ON TO WIEN. Down the Danube drifting in a dream ; How its bright green waters dance and gleam; How they leap and sparkle in the sun; Still the grand old river floweth on ; Pleasant are the meadows gliding by; Tranquil is the river and the sky ; Yonder rise the mountains, capped with snow, Here the peasant peoples come and go; Now the troubled waters move in wild unrest; Still we glide, contented, on their breast ; Pleasant are the mountains and the meadows green, Down the Danube dlifting on to Wien. On to the great city where our journey ends; Etill, through all its changes, thitherward it tends ; There undreamed of glories crown the closing:scene ; Down the Danube drifting on to Wien.</p>
        <p>Words fail me to describe the charm of that delightful sail. Shortly after leaving Linz, we glide beneath the pillars of a railway bridge, and presently</p>
        <p>roof of its peculiar church. Next comes artistic Weissenkirchen, familiar to students of the Thirty Years' War, its minster built upon a rock, and com- passed with old watch towers, walls, and moats, and very curious in its intermigled architecture. • And then the Danube makes a mighty bend, and Rossatz opens out, upon the right,a Gothic church and ancient castle notable amidst its pleasant vineyards ; and after that, delightful Diirnstein, "a jewel among Austria's historic places," and interesting to Englishmen especially by reason of its ruined castle, once the prison of the Lion-Hearted King. One seemed to hear its legendary minstrel singing underneath its timeworn walls, and I could almost picture Cceur-de-Lion listening, with rapt attention, as we passed it in the soft sweet sunshine of that bright September day. Two ruined churches add to the unquestionable charm of this most interesting place, and just beyond it we could see the plain of Loiben, the scene of a 'most bloody fight between the Russians and Austrians and the French, who were completely routed. Then far across the intervening country, we see the mighty Benedictive Monastery of Gothweih ; and then we pass the ancient Roman station called Mauterm' and come to Stein, the scene of many a dreadful light, its massive houses, towers, arcades and gateways forming a delightful picture of the middle ages. And, finally we notice Krems, almost as quaint, and very ancient too, and round it, spreading gradually, a lovely modern town in which the plants of tropic climes are said to flourish in the open air. And now the river widens out, we leave the hills behind, and traverse a great fertile plain. The poetry and idyll which adorn the Wachau fadeaway. lie traffic, too, increases, and, for an hour or so, before the summons comes to tea, I doze in my deck chair, and glance from time to time at the brown floating timber, and the monster rafts .of pines and other forest trees, one piloted by seven strong Austrian peasants, which all the day we had seen drifting slowly downwards; and then we see more bills, the last low fringes of the Alps ; and 3ot another monastery ; and far away, in front, upon a vast, low, level plain a distant dome which tells us that we are in sight of Wien at last. The sun was lighting up the hills and woods and castles on our right and casting evening shadows on the mighty meadows on our left as, somewhere close on six o'clock, our voyage ended and we disembarked at one of the great Danube quays, passed with cur luggage through a bustling crowd, and, climbing on to various vehicles, rode down the famous Prc ter</p>
