      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>OUTSKIRTS OF VIENNA.</head>
        <p>THE OUTSKIRTS OF VIENNA.</p>
        <p>In accordance with a recently adopted policy, the forests on the outskirts of Vienna (or Wein) are to be preserved so that they will form for ever a girdle of woods and meadows round the town. Above we reproduce a view of the Kobenzl estate, one of those purchased for the purpose.</p>
        <p>we sail by Ebelsberg, and note its castle, dating back, tradition says, a thousand years. Then, passing Enna, the Laureaeum of the Romans, which stands upon the stream of the - same . name dividing Upper and Lower Austria, we reach Mauthausen, with the quaint old castle of Pragstein standing on a rock just by the river's brink. We sail beneath another railway bridge, and, now, the hills appear on either bank, and, on the right, we note the castle of Nieder-Wallsee, the property of the Archduke Franz Salvator, situate at Wallsee, famous for its manufacture of mill-stones. And then the scenery becomes magnificent,"and the mountains come right down to the banks. The great cliffa overhead throw their dark green shadows over the foaming waters of the great river, which rushes past in wild exnberacce, unmindful of all obstacles." So says the little Guide supplied to us upon the steamer ; but, as a matter of fact, the major " obstacles " have been removed by blasting, and the banks of the Danube are as well protected as the banks of the Ship Canal. I looked in vain for "the flying bridge of Tiefenbach " connecting Upper and Lower Austria, a bridge through which the boat is said to pass ; but, when we came to Grein (by name and by nature), and find its castle nestling among those verdurous cliffs and smiling dells so peacefully, we understand why it is spoken of so lovingly as " the Pearl of the Danube." For a pearl it really is in its calm beauty and completeness. The great cliffs now approach so closely that you seem to be sailing on some far Norwegian fiord or up some - narrow mountain lake. But, curving suddenly, it opens out again, and, breaking rudely on the tall cliffs opposite, creates the Greiner Schwan a rough backwater in the swirl of which the hapless, traveller according to the Guide, had frequently been drowned. But, to my eyes, the stream did not appear to be especially alarming, and I almost felt as though I could have swum across it. The scenery, however,. is extremely wild and beautiful, and the river flows on once again, through channels ever narrowing, beneath dark forests and through moss grown rocks, and I observe a strange austerity in all the lands- cape which contrasts remarkably with the more genial scenery of the earlier portion of the sail, But all the scenery was so delightful that I grudged the very time for lunch, and, whilst we ate it in the bright saloon, we passed by Rabenstein, a place at which, at periods of high water, the great river splits in two around the Isle of Worth. Beyond we crossed the Wirbel, or vortex, transformed into an ordinary rapid, as proclaimed by a tablet above the Votive Chapel, by the Emperor Franz Joseph I, who "relieved navigation on the Danube from all danger from the whirlpool by blasting the river bed at Hanstein-Felseninsel." Beyond the Wirbel, we approach St. Nikola, with its tiny hillside church; and, now, "the Danube once again asserts its claim to be the most beautirul river in Europe," and, as my comrades, some of whom had seen them both admitted readily, "is here by no means inferior to the Rhine." Soon we sight the two great towers of the majestic monastery of Melk, which practically covers .a great granite rock some 60 metres high, and stands upon the threshold of Wachau, " the Sleep- ing Beauty," as some poet brain has termed it, the loveliest region of the Danube, a region which is rapidly awakening to a fuller sense of its incompar- able-charms. Some Housing friends had come from Wien by rail to join us here; and, leaving Melk, we see Schonbiihel, a castle rising with its fine four- store yed tower directly from grim, wall-like rocks; and, by the water's edge, a beautiful asylum for Catholic priests. At Ag,gsbach, yet another castle rises from the very top of the sheer cliffs, at an enor- mous height, and seems a part of them, a stronghold almost inaccessible and rich with robber legends of the past ; and then we _pass the little church of St. Johann, supposed to be the tomb of St. Albin, its weathercock commemorating an old story of the crow- ing of a cock which saved the valley of the Danube from a flood which the devil intended to effect by building a great wall across the river. Another ruin called the Hinterhaus is seen above the little town of Spitz, a place of vineyards ; and then,the river,narrow- ing once again, upon the left, we see sweet vine-clad cliffs, and, on the right, great wooded slopes,,and, at the narrowest Fart, St. Michael's, with seven figures wrongly spoken of as " hares " perched on the</p>
        <p>Strasse, into the heart of gay Vienna. For Wien,as I may now explain, is the Austrian name for the great Austrian capital—the native calls it Vien —and 1 am not surprised that many a Warringtonian has wondered where this unkaown city really was. A lofty monument saluted us as soon as we had left the boat, and presently we came to a great river channel, skirt' d this, then crossed it by one of many noble bridges, and, a little later, pulled up at the Metropole, which stands at the corner of a sort of sqnare, one side of which is open to the rolling emerald stream. Our evening dinner had, we found, so much delayed us that, to-night, we once again dispensed with the accustomed meal, and set forth, in the light of the innumerable lamps, to make the definite acquaintance of this famous city. With my Australian friend, and Mr. Spencer, of Tynemouth, I sauntered slowly up the Prater Strasse, a scene of life and animatim, with many cafes and a crowd of citizens of every class, and made my way to Venedig in Wien (or Venice in Vienna), a sort of second-rate Earl's Court with an immense Big Wheel. From this we gazed, amid the darkness at the fairy scene below, and out acrois a sort of great, black, nether firmament, begemmed with the innumerable lights of earth, a sort of brighter reflex of the innumerable lamps of heaven. We also visited Parisiana, a sort of Frencby Theatre, and, from an elevated platform cafe, watched, at intervals, a popular variety performance in the open air, and then strolled back through the still busy streets to our Hotel.</p>
        <p>The morning broke again in quite unclouded sun- shine, and, with kindly friends to guide us' we set forth to view the further wonders of this famous capital, which is certainly one of the most beautiful capitals in Europe. Vienna has a population of two million souls. Originally hemmed in by two great lines of ramparts, it is now encircled by three verdant " Rings " the charm and grace of which words fail to adequately paint. The first of these, familiar as the Ringstrasse, runs in a splendid semi-circle from the river channel round the oldest portion of the city. The Metropole was situated almost at the centre of this green and beautiful diameter. The second circle makes a wider sweep, and, out beyond it, a gigantic ring of woods and meadows holds the city in its soft, green, beautiful embrace. First,we drove right round the Ringstrasse, which is, in many places,much more like a park than a thorough- fare, a park in whose recesses all the noblest buildings in the city have been grouped in beautiful proportions, making up a tout ensemble which the fairest scenes in Paris do not equal, or at any rate, surpass. We rode just by the site of the Ring Theatre, the horror of whose burning haunts me still ;.and passed in turn, the Bourse' the Maximilian's Platz, the University, the great Burg Theatre, just opposite the stately modern Rathaus; and then the noble Parliament House, in front of it a monument of wise Minerva, and about its classical facade some fine equestrian statues. A little to the left of it, we caught a glimpse of the Palace of Justice ; and, in front of these, we paused to visit the Volksgarten, with its lily pools and lovely statue of Elizabeth, the murdered Empress, once familiar on many a British hunting field; and then, upon the left, we see the Royal Palace, and, upon the right, two great Museums, on either side of a most pleasant square, its centre dominated by a noble monument to Maria Theresa, surrounded by the famous statesmen of her reign. And then, still riding round the Ring, we passed the stately Opera House, and reached the Stadt Park,right in the midst of which the little river Wien emerges from below a mighty thoroughfare, and runs down in a deep stone channel, to the larger stream; and visited the home of the Vienna Chamber of Commerce, itself a sort of Palace, the Vienna Housing Council occupy- ing a large portion of its upper floor. Then, journey ing further still, we drove into the lovely Prater, a siprt of Viennese Hyde Park, with its own Rotten Row and manifold attrac- tion., and noticed, in its leafy glades, the early signs of a great Coming Exhibition. Returning to the central portion of the town, we sauntered through its handsome business streets, some quaint enough, some strangely London-like; and then we strolled into the Stephansleirche, the spire of which reminded me of Ulm, its great interior rich with noble arches and with rainbow windows; and, here, we ,</p>
        <p>paused to note how many of the pious citizens, with home-like confidence, were clustering on the very steps of the High Altar, whilst the incense rose and I filled the place, and all the mighty congregation bowed before the splendour of the Host. Then, passing through the ancient courtyard of the Palace, we were fain to pause, amid an eager crowd, to listen to delightful music and to watch the changing of the Guard, and peer up curiously at the big windows at the back of which bright uniforms moved to and fro, and wonder whether iumonr lied in saying that the Emperor himself was there. Then, going on a little further, we all lunched al freaco in a Garden Restaurant just by the great Museums ; and then we took our seats once more in the awaiting carriages, and rode out to the big block dwelling built a dozen years before in honour of the Emperor's Jubilee, and part of a gient scheme which is intended to eventually accommodate 4000 persons of the poorer class. From thence we drove to Schonbrunn, where the Emperor resided. The Palace is a somewhat unimpressive building, but the Park behind it (something like Versailles, I under- stand) is very flue, and notable for its interminaEle pathways lined with closely clipped and hedge-like forest trees. About the very time that we arrived, the aged Emperor, we heard, was to receive the new Imperial Chancellor of Germany, and I was fain to see them both ; but there was little sign of anima- tion in the courtyard, as we passed, and my com- panions did not seem to share my curiosity, and time was short; and so we wandered slowly through those endless vistas, walled with greenery, and passed a mighty Palm House and a great Menagerie to reach at last, a Restaurant immediately beyond the gates, and take a cup of tea, although, of course, in my case, it was coffee, for I never tasted tea upon tl.e journey, and the flavour of that Coutitientml coffee, haunts me yet. Some hurried off to dress for the Opera. The others followed on a little later, and I spent an hour in seek- ing to obtain a real icroplano for my expectant !eddies in my home a thousand miles away. Although I did not find the thing I wanted in those handsome shops, eventually I came across a model Zeppelin the poweis of which a pleasant and attractive damsel demon- strated to my satisfaction, and the mode of working which she courteously explained to me in very charm- ing English. I had not got a ticket for the Opera,and did not want to hurry back to dress, which some of my superior friends appeared to think essential; but I sauntered round the Ring until I reached the stately edifice, and, paying eighteen pence or so, was shown by dazzling lackeys to a ganely with spacious seats and promenade, quite filled with an exttemely well- dressed an enlightened audience; and for an hour or more, I had my soul altncst pulled out of me by the enthralling strains of Wagner's beautiful Tannhauser. There were two hundred playeis in the orchestra. The icenemy and the stage effects were wonderful in the extreme,and the voices of those Austrian singers, singing the eternal strife 'twixt Love and Duty, the glamour of the story, and the unrivalled power of the heart-moving ciaorsee3 come back to me and haunt me yet. I noticed, in the promenade, a sort of fountain, with uumeroua glasses of fresh water provided for the patrons of the place, amid I had previously observed that, aim At everywhere, a glass of water usually accompanied a chink. I tore myself away to meet two comrades at the lionacher, a famous music Hall the pit of which was occupied by chairs and tables where you could enjoy your supper as you watched the entei tainment. We spent the following morning at the Rathaue, and were received with altno t ro3al dignity by the Oberburgornaster, Dr. Karl Lueger. At his sugges- tion, we were all conducted by the grave Carator round the, fine Municipal Museum which is housed within the building, and is rich with relics of Beethoven, and Mozart, and G:tick, and Schubert, all of whom are more or less associated wih "the musical capital of Europe " ; and then we spent two hours or so in studying Vienna's civic polity mid its town pleas; and then, returning to the 'Oberburgomaster's sta'ely room, their walls adorned with noble portraits of his p:edecesso. s and the city's greatest men, tho map tly old gray-bearded doctor welcomed us once mere, and took the arm cf Mrs. Leicester (she was the only lady left to us), and with old-world courtesy ct nducted her to lunch. I had noticed a strange look in the old man's eyes, and that he did not seem to see us as we were presented to him one by one. I had also neticed that he leaned upon a stick. I now discovered that he was lame, and practically blind, and, as we sat at the well- filled tables, I observed that a nun who had accom- panied him was seated on his left and feeding him. Several Burgomasters joined us at this final scene lof civic hospitality, and, in addition to a beautifully illustrated album of the city, each of us was now presented with a handsome and well-filled cigar case as a further souvenir. In every town which we had visited I had been much impressed by its official head, but none of them impressed me quite as much as Dr. Karl Lueger, the man to whom Vienna's beauty is so largely due ; and who, in his increasing years, is honoured by ete peeple he has served so well with an affectionate devotion which it is not difficult to either understand or share. And Vienna is not slow to show what she thinks of those who serve her. The lovely square in front of the Rathaus is "adorned with eight statues of men well deserved of the town "—to quote the expressive language of the album—and is called the Dr. Karl Lueger Platz ; a fountain called the Siebenbonnen, built in 1904, commemorates his sixtieth birthday; a Municipal Home for aged people, situate at Lainz, which Dr. Karl Lueger foundel, and which accommodates 3,500 people,contains a handsome bust in recognition of his splendid work ; all through Vienna one may trace his wisdom statesmanship and high sagacity ; but, for all time his noblest monu- ment will be that outer ring oiwoods and meadows which are never to be built upon, and which Vienna owes to this old Oberburgomaster whom I shall remember to my dying day. The usual toasts were drunk; the usual courtesies expressed in language of unusual charm, our hearts all bubbling over with a gratitude and friendliness which Mr. Aldridge put into a speech as graceful and sincere es any I have ever heard ; and then we bade good-bye to the old veteran and were taken in a sumptuous special tramcar out to see one portion of that belt of woodland for ourselves. A small light railway took us from the tram right into the green heart of it, and to a castle from whose pleasant terraces we gazed, in mute delight, at all the glories of the lovely city, nestling, in its many-sided splendour on the margin of its mighty stream. The castle was to be transformed into a civic restaurant • new noble roads were being made amid the abounding trees ; and, here, as long as the green Danube washed its quays, the happy citizens of Wien might rest in peace and feast their grateful eyes upon as fair a scene as any city in the world can show. "I can see the people walking in the shadow of those trees to-day,' said Cecil Rhodes, when somebody rebuked him for his folly in planting a great avenue of saplings in a young Rhodesian settlement. And I can see the unborn generations of Vienna resting in the shadow of these woods and hills, and blessing the old Oberburgo- waiter aa they gaze, with grateful hearts and kindling eyes, upon the Bills Delectable and the Enchanted Forests which their queenly and incomparable city owes to him.</p>
        <p>We left Vienna, on another golden day, with infinite regret and memories which will not fade. "It has been one continual crescendo all the way," I said to my friend, Mr. Pepler, as we drove together to the Station. And he said that the expression suitably summed up the Tour. All day we rode amid the radiant sunshine through the heart of Austria on our journey home. At Melk the railway runs just by the river, and we saw the mighty monastery at closer quarters, and could gaze, for the last time, at the great river which had wound itself into the hearts of all of us, and rolled in silent majesty in the deep valley just below. And, in the golden afternoon, we rode for hours along those great Bavarian Alps, sublimely. beautiful in the bright autumn air—a long fair vision of blue mountain glory culminating in the noble heights which frown above the frontier town of Salzburg. There, strangely disappointed that I could not stay,l parsed the Customs Officers again; endeavoured to get rid of my super- fluons krone and holler and reduce my marks and pfennigs to the needful minimum to last me till I reached Deutsch-Avricourt; and then we moved along the banks of the delightful Chiemsee, visible in all its steely length and breadth to-day, and on along the smaller Siemsee, and so back through Rosenburg, until the darkness fell, and close on eight o'clock, we pulled up once again at Munich. With Mr. Pepler, Mr. Williams, and my old friend, Councillor Walker, .I went forth to a Munchen Restaurant, and had a satisfying German supper and then we bought some Miluchen relics, and strolled slowly back, and quietly resumed our places in the train. Some members of our party; wiser perhaps than we, had taken berths in the sleeping car, but, young and enterprizing, we bad all decided to con- tent ourselves with the companionable ordinary car. Of course. I never slept a wink—l never do in a night train—and when, in the gray dawn, we pulled up at</p>
        <p>the frontier station, I was just as tired as it was possible to be. But I bad noticed in the continental issue of the "Daily Mail" that this was Aviation Week at Nancy, and had long ago resolved to break my journey there. My friends were going on to Paris, and, accordingly at eight o'clock, I bade them all good-bye with great regret, for a pleasanter and better set of fellows I do not. expect to meet; deposited my luggage at the station ; and, sauntering out into the green Place niers, made for the nearest restaurant, and, flung my weary limbs across a chair,and smoked, and mused, and rested for an hour or so, and then I had a shave, and went out to the Aviation Ground to find the big dirigible—the " Ville de Nancy," sister to the big balloon at Rheims—deflated, and the teroplanea departed days ago. There were, however, many interesting photographs and models of these all-absorbing craft, and I chanced to learn from an obliging lady, who had over- heard my que-tions and spoke English well, that five balloons were to ascend, that afternoon, from beautiful La Pepiniere. Here, top, I heard of t%e sad death of Ferber, in the trials which were now proceeding at Boulogne. I spent three hours or so at the adjacet Exhibition, which was typical of Exhi'ritioes every- where; and bought some bergamotes and other "specialities de Nancy " ; and then, conducted by a kind old Frenchman, I proceeded to the famous Park, and for two hours or so was greatly ieterested in observing how these five balloons were gradually got ready, took their passengers aboard (a lady in the daring company), and, one by one, sailed off into the almost winaltss air to the inspiring strains of "Partant pour la Sync" and the cheers of the assembled crowd; then, hastening towards the Station, met, by accident, the man who had piloted the big dirigible from Paris to the place where we hal seen it in its mighty terodrome at Rheims. I rode back in the tramcar with the gallant fellow and his wife and boy, and then I hauled my luggage out and got into the f,ix o'clock express for Paris Through Toni and Bar /c Due and beautiful Chalons-sur Marne again, and then through sweet Epernay and through busy Meaux, the train sird pleasantly, but it was dark, and I could only picture all their chartn of vines, and churches, and historic buildings, and have not space to more than hint at them; and then a galaxy of lights ate, eared, and, close on midnight I dismounted at the Gore de L'Est, secured a bed at an hotel immediately acr,ss the way, and sauntered down the boulevards to have another peep at all the strange life of the cafes. I had found the German towns of Stuttgart, Ulm and Munich singularly quiet and respectable at night. Vienna half suggc sled London, but Paris, "Great city of the midnight sun, Whose day begins when day is done," is quite unique in its nocturnal habits. I was, how- ever very weary. The endless cafes looked, for once, a little lonely and neglected; and so I hailed a voiture, drove clown to La Madeleine, and, strolling onwards, passed the great Egyptian obelisk, and crossed the Seine, and gazed into its heaving breast - half ink, half fire—with thronging memories ; and then retracing my tired r.teps, I pausA in reverie below the monument to the lr at provinces - Alsace- Lorraine-still decked with wreaths, and still the object of pathetic hopcs and aspirations. But I had been through both of them and seen their Conquerors. And France will never rule those provinces again! Seine three hours sleep, a final breakfast of delightful rolls and coffee, a drive, on yet aoother golden meriting, to the Post Office ; and then, towards ten o'clock, I journeyed to the adj icent Gore du Nerd, with all its line of handsome monuments, met Peter Wright, the champi m wrestler, once again, with one of his co-delegates fro o Newp rrt ; and, tilking pleasantly with them and with a Spanish couple who were taking their wee laddie to an English school, rode quickly on to Dover. I looked in vain, as we passed by Boulogne, for arty Eigns of zero- planes, and gave a passing thought to poor dead Ferber ; at Calais I was tAd that, on that very morning some one had once more ersayed to tempt the waters of the Channel, but had found the day unfavcurable ; I bait&amp; some luscious ,pears ft orn one of the French peasant women on the quay; and then I said good-bye to La Belle France, and crossed the narrow straits which had been lately crossed by Bleriot and to nearly crossed by brave Hugh Latham ; sailed into the big new Dover Harbour; rode once more through those dismantled hopfields ; and, at tix o'clock, I said good-bye to my two last companions; went to the Imperial Exhibition ; found it half deserted,not to say depressing,after all the glories of the previous year; and then I had a lobster salad, hailed another cab, and, leaving Enston as the clock beomed twelve, reached Warrington at six o'clock and home in ample time for breakfast.</p>
        <p>Now that Butter is so dear,you should try "GOLDEN FLEECE MARGARINE. It is just as good as Best Butter and only costs Bd. per pound .—Advt.</p>
      </div>
