      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>FAMILY FREED FROM DISEASE.</head>
        <p>FAMILY FREED FROM DISEASE.</p>
        <p>Zam-Buk Relieves 5 Children in one:Famlly From Shocking Scalp sores.</p>
        <p>A typical example of the extraordinary" wholesale" cures effected by Zam-Buk recently in single districts where eczetin, and similar virulent skin and scalp diseases have run riot is furnished by Mrs. M. Goldsmith, of 28, Desford Road, Canning Town London, E. A pressman who visited her learns that Mrs. Goldsmith's five children, Ernest (14), George (12), Arthur (10), Fred (7) and Daisy (5), all suffered for many weary months from shocking scalp sores. "Arthur started first with ringworn on his head," Mrs. Goldsmith stated. "The ringworm turned to nasty running sores which spread all over the boy's head. One after another the other children caught the disease which in a few weeks had eaten almost all their hair away. They had, of course, to stay away from school. I covered each child's head with some cheap ointment I got from the chemist, but this was quite useless. The poor children were in constant pain and almost distracted me by their crying and moaning. There was very little sleep at night for any of us, and in the daytime I got scarcely any rest at all. " At last I took the four boys to the London General Hospital, where they received every attention. The hospital ointments and medicines however, had no good effect, and after a loner trial the doctors told me there was no cure possible' except by X-Rays. I didn't know what to do. but I could see nothing else for it than to let the children go under the X-Rays. "One day my husband came home and told me he had heard that Zam-Buk was a splendid thing for curing skin diseases. We talked it over and decided, before consenting to the X-Rays treatment, to try this balm. We watched anxiously to see what effect Zam- Buk would have, and were delighted to notice an early improvement in all the children. I first washed their heads with Zam-Buk Medicinal Soap, and when they were dry I smeared Zam-Buk over all the sore places. Zam-Buk soothed the children and they stopped crying. At night, too, we all got good sleep. "I had no further anxiety about the children having to be operated on after I started with Zam- Buk. This rare balm worked wonders! The sores on each child dried up and the scabs that formed peeled away easily. A new skin then appeared and the hair grew again. All the children were cured by this magic Zam-Buk, and all are back at school with clean scalps and splended heads of hair. The neigh- bours know of these wonderful cures and are astonished that anything could have cured such bad cases."</p>
        <p>Zam-Buk, the wonderful balm which brought unexpected happiness into the Goldsmith home is sold by chemists at Is. 11d. 2s. 9d., or 4s. 6d. a box, or is obtainable direct at the same prices from the Zam- Buk Co., 45, Cow Cross Street, London, F.C.</p>
        <p>TRAGIC DEATH OF MUSICAL CONDUCTOR. The death occurred at Hanley, on Sunday evening, of Mr. James Whewall, the distinguished conductor of the North Staffordshire Choral Society. The circumstances of his death are particularly tragic, when it is remem- bered that just a fortnight ago he had the honour of conducting a special command performance of his choir at Windsor Castle, before the King and Queen and the King of Portugal, and was personally complimented by King Edward. Mr. Whewall was seized with appendicitis a few days after his visit to the Royal borough. On Wednesday Mrs. Whewall received a messfige of sympathy from the King and Queen. It was : " Buckingham Palace, November 30. "Dear Madam,—l am commanded by the King and Queen to express to you and your family Their Majesties' regret at the death of Mr. Whewall, and to assure you of their sympathy in your affliction.— Yours faithfully, " CIIA RI.I.CS _FREDERICK." . _ _</p>
        <p>At the funeral on Wednesday five choirs were at the graveside, and sang with the public " Jesu, Lover of my Soul," Mr. Mewsll,s favourite bymm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>IMPERIAL DEFENCE BILL PASSED.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL DEFENCE BILL PASSED.</p>
        <p>The debate on the Bill "to consolidate the Empire for the purpose of Imperial defence" was con- cluded at the meeting of the Warrington Parliamentary Debating Society on Monday, when the measure was passed without a division.</p>
        <p>Captain G. R. CROSFIELD (Rochiale, I), who reopened the discussion, said the Bill came at a singularly opportune time because at the present moment we were in danger, owing to internal conflicts, not giving a thought to possibre external conflicts. Europe, which vim already armed to the teeth on land, was arming on sea. We had vittually abandoned the two-power standard and it had been abandoned without a murmer —and he said rightly, because the two-power standard, as was pointed out the other day for the first time by Mr. Amery, pre-supposed a two-power standard of wealth, and that we had not got any longer. This fact was making people realise very clearly that-as it was no longer possible to have a two-power standard we must without delay build up a sound second line of defence behind the navy. The Bill recognized that fact clearly and simply. It recognized that imperial defence was not the affair of the navy alone or of the army alone, but of the navy and army acting harmoniously together. He felt that if the Bill wore passed we would in future be spared the undignified and unwholesome panics to which we bad been subjected of late. The member for Normanton (Mr. Nightingale) seemed to think that national defence was simply a question of paying taxes and his remedy for the solution of our difficulties was " Sack the War Office." Probably a great many of them would like to eack a certain portion of the War Office tut he thought that if they read history aright they would see that the Empire was not going to be saved :imply by the paying of taxes but by personal service. There were some things which a man could not do by rft3 ing money alone but which he must do himself, and the defence of the country was one of them. It was the refits ii to recogeise that fact which had been res- ponsible so largely for the downfall of Empires in the past. The member for Normanton seemed to ridicule the idea that we were not safe. Ho referred to the mines which were law to guard our commercial harbours —mines which formerly existed hut which had been abolished because the people of this country did not think that invasion was a passibility and our members of Parliament on both sides did not take a proper interest in defence. He maintained that if we had national training all our politicians and statesmen would would gain knowledge to enalaTe them to take sufficient interrst in the question. The member for W. Belfast (Ir. W. Rigby) quoted from countries which had not adopted the scheme of the National Service League, but failed altogether to quote from the one country— Switzerland—on whose system the National Service idea was 'lased. Why? Simply because it was impos- sible to find any Swiss authority who ran down the scheme. There was a great difference between a system which took a man away for two years and put him under professional officers who were altogether removed from the civilian life of the people, and a system under which a man was taken away for only two or three months and trained under civilian officers. Captain Crosfield repudiated the suggestion that instrnctions had gone forth from the League headquarters that the argument as to the physical benefits of national training must be dropped. is it were only for the good of the physique of the people, he (the speaker) would advocate the training. He believed that after three or four months' training the capacity of the lungs of recruits increased on the average by 13 per cent. In conclusion, Captain Crosfield said he could not help feeling that we had suffered in the past by allowing party feeling to run away with us too much and blind us to the true interests of the nation. He felt that if we were to prevent the sceptre of Empire and the trident of Neptune falling from our grasp we must build up our moral and physical well-being : we must train and organize our man power, for only by such training should we be fit to attend to the development and to the salvation of our Imperial heritage. ( Applause).</p>
        <p>Mr. BLACK (North Donegal, N.) said the Bill would mean further taxation for Ireland and they had it on the authority of a Commission appointed by a Tory Government that Ireland had already been over-taxed to the extent of £2,750.000. Until those conditions bad been altered the Nationalists could have nothing to do with a Bill which involved further taxation of Ireland. The Bill was absolutely worthless and in seeking to dictate to the Colonies it was unconstitutional. They would simply be following in the wake of that unfortunate Parliament which sacrificed the American Colonies. Captain Crosfield was unfortunate in quoting Switzerland. What stake bad the people of England in their own country? What had they to fight for? Had they any interest in the source of all wealth—the laud? In Switzerland the soil was fairly equally divided among the populatiens there being at least 300,000 peasant proprietor, In Switzerland it was not merely the plutocracy who made wars and then called upon the people to sacrifice their liven. Every citizen who entered upon his 21st year in that country was entitled to vote. It would not be fair to impose a Bill for military training upon the manhood of England until they gave them all a chance of saying whether they would have it or not. Switzerland needed a citzen army because of its land frontiers. We were an insular power and once the backbone of the navy was broken, an army of 1,000,000 men would be useless. The invaders would only have to "sit tight" and starve them out. Mr. E. ENGLAND (Warrington, L) said that while he was prepared to acknowledge that in many respects the proposals of the National Service League would do a great deal of good, he was not persuaded that the policy of teaching young men to shoot was wise. As to tbe invasion scare, they knew that after the recent official inquiry Mr. Asquith declared that there was no fear of invasion and that if an attempt took place it could not possibly prove successful. Mr. Balfour con- firmed that. The statement that to be prepared for war was the strongest safeguard for peabe was re-iterated like a parrot cry. It was one of the greatest fallacies ev4r uttered, because history did not substantiate it. He instanced the wars between France and Germany, Japan and Russia. The ancient Romans insisted that every man up to forty years of age should be called upon to bear arms, but that did not save Rome. There was more truth in the old Gospel that" those who take up the sword shall perish by the sword." If they wished the people to defend the nation, they must give them something worth defending. Millions of people in this country had practically nothing to defend; they had certain liberties, but of possessions they had none. There was nothing in the Bill to say in what proportion the Colonies and the mother country should contribute to the expenditure.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. MASON (Newcastle-under-Lyme, I) said that justas it took two people to make a war, so it took two people to make a peace. No matter how much we desired peace, if another nation was working in the opposite direction our efforts were not going to be entirely successful. He never heard a more pre- posterous statement than that if they taught a man how to use a rifle he would always be wanting to shoot some- one. They might as well say that if they taught a man to handle dynamite he would want to blow up the Town Hall. (Laughter). If every nation that had taken up the sword had perished by the sword, there would only have been the Hottentots left. (Laughter.) With regard to the argument that they should give the people something to fight for, no man in the House was keener on social reform and the taxation of land values than he (the speaker) was. At the same time he said every Britisher had an undoubted stake in the welfare of the country for which it was his duty to fight. He belonged to the most glorious Empire that the sun had ever shone on. This land had given him his birth, his =tare and protection; it had given him a greater measure of liberty, a higher standard of com- fort and more luxuries thad obtained in any other nation of the world. Mr. Mason contradicted the con- tention that wars were invariably caused by the plutocracy. He also remarked than it was wonderful how the opposition to the Bill came from a party which bad been labelled "little Navy," "little Englander ,"</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>and to a large extent pro-Boer and pro-everything else that was not English. The Premier (Mr. W. H. Stansfield) said tie Bill as a not the result of a scare; it was brought forward ;ng matter of common-sense. They had got someth'e worth keeping and they were prepared to try and ko it. The new scheme would liberate the regular army as a striking force In any part of the Empire and the warships which were at present kept in Coloniil waters would be available for other service. If one country was prepared for war and another was not, he did not see how that was to guarantee peace. They had bten told that those who took up the sword would perish by the sword, but there was another statement in the New Testament that he strong man armed kept his goods in peace until a stronger man came and despoiled him. At present we were the strong man, cut other nations were trying to be stronger. There was something even more valuable than land to fight for—liberty and free institutions. Once this country was conquered by a foreign Power we would soon find we had lost something. The Govern- ment proposed to delete the clause which enabled a colony to declare war on its own authority and also to insert a provision making the scheme subject to the consent of the Colonies.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. MAGINN (W. Clare, N.) said the Nationalists could never vote for a measure which made it com- pulsory upon Irishmen in Ireland to give military service until they had first the opportunity of valun- teering. Since the union 110 years ago they bad not received permission to raise a volunteer battalion in Ireland and the Territorial scheme did not apply to that cauntry. He utterly denied that the national physique would be improved by military training. It was dependent upon the conditions under which people lived. The discharged soldier was not generally a very splendid specimen of physique—in fact he often asked for ea=y jobs on the plea taut, he bad been worn out by service.</p>
        <p>Captain CROSFIELD : You can have to much -of a good thing. Mr. MAGINN, continuing, said the Prime Minister stated that it England were conquered by a foreign nation we would find we had lost a groat deal. And 'et the Conservative party was busily engaged in prov- ing what a splendid pl..ce Germany was. (Laughter.) Ceptain WARING (Plymouth, C), in supporting the Bill said that from his personal observationa he was convinced that 61n.alany would have no did:ulty in despatching from 50,000 t 3 100,000 men within twenty- four hours from her ports to this country, together with three to six month provision. Mr. F. GARSIDE (L.) supported the Bill, and said there was cause for serious thought in the Finance Bill which Germany had passed for naval purposes. Mr. LLOYD (Foreign Secretary) replying on the die- cusaion, urged the [louse to work for patziotiam and not for party motives. The amendment that the Bill be read that cliy three months wag then put and defeated, no division being challenged, and the Bill was then passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>AUTO-VIVISECTION.</head>
        <p>AUTO-VIVISECTION.</p>
        <p>DO YOU DELIBERATELY TORTURE YOURSELF ALIVE BY NEGLECTING YOUR STOMACH?</p>
        <p>REMEDY FREE FOR THE ASKING.</p>
        <p>A great outcry has arisen during the rlt few years against scientists who torture dumb animals alive in the interests of science. How many people are there, however, who needlessly torture themselves by causing their stomachs to over- work! Indigestion, dyspepsia, heartburn, sleep- lessness, loss of vim and energy are the inevitable result.</p>
        <p>Are. you one of these? Why not give your stomach a rest? Stuart's Dyspepsia Tablets will relieve your stcmach of all its work and do it for you. They are compounded of the very best elements and do the work which the stomach performs when in normal health—aseptic pepsin, malt diastase and like ingredients. A doctor, in writing a thesis on treatment of indigestion, closes his remarks by saying: "For those suffering from acid dyspepsia, shown by sour, watery risings; or for flatulent dyspepsia, shown by gas on stomach, causing heart troubin and difficult breathing, as well as for all other forms of stomach trouble, the safest treatment is to take one or two of Stuart's Dyspepsia Tablets after each meal. I advise them because tbey -:ontain 110 harmful drugs, but are composed of minable digetives which act promptly upon the food eaten. I never knew a case of indigestion or even chronic dyspepsia which Stuart's Tablets would not reach."</p>
        <p>Cheap cathartic medicines claiming to cure indigestion can have no effect whatever in actively digesting the food, and to call any cathartic medicine a cure for indigestion is a misnomer. Every chemist can supply Stuart's Dyspepsi* Tablets, and they are not only the safest and most successful, but the most scientific of any treatment for indigestion and stomach troubles. Price 2/9 and 4/6 a box. Free sample on application to F. A. Stuart Co., 86 Clerkenwell Road, London, E.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>HONOUR FOR SIR JOHN T. BRUNNER, M.P.</head>
        <p>HONOUR FOR SIR JOHN T. BRUNNER, M.P.</p>
        <p>Sir John T. Brunnner, Part., M.P., who recently announced his decision to retire from Parliament, was on Saturday unanimously elected Pro-Chancellor of the University of Liverpool in succession to the late Sir Edward Lawrence. His conspicuous services to the University, both by gifts of money and personal share in the work of administration, were eloquently recognised, and the fact that he claims Liverpool 88 his native city was spoken of as a happy feature of the choice. Sir John was born at Everton in 1812, a son of the Rev. John Brunner, of Canton Zurich, Switzerland, who became an Everton schoolmaster. Educated at his father's school, he entered mercantile life at Liverpool in 1857. In company with that famous chemist, Dr. Ludwig Mond, he established in 1873 the alkali works at Northwich, which have become the largest in the world. In these works the system of theoretical and practical education for apprentices is combined in rare degree and with admirable results. Sir John was created a baronet in 1895. His benefactions have embraced liberal donations to the Liverpool University where he endowed the Chair of Economic Science with £lO,OOO, and more recently the Chair of Egyptology; while to Northwich be has given a library at a cost of £7,000. He has also liberally endowed libraries at Nantwich,- Winsford, and Mrddlewich. To Runcorn and Winsford he has given guildhalls for the use of trades unions and friendly societies. He has represented the Northwich Division in Parliament, 1885-6, and since 1887, and is a D.L. and Privy Councillor. As chairman of Brunner, Mond, and Co. he occupies a prominent commercial position, but his interests in the educa- tional advance of the nation and in healthful recreation have always been as keen as his activity in business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>VENO'S</head>
