      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>OUR</head>
        <p>OUR</p>
        <p>. ~. . , • . j'A. .. . .. , , 6 ki, i 1: $ 1 t • .••; "., . ...7 ..,.. r.... F, .., t'• ~,,! ... L 4 • . (.. ' A . i ',.., ... 1:'. rir I.i. 1114 ..,, ..• ~ t • Is S' . : ; • irr .. ~..,,,,,, Y V, ‘..‘. 9 , o . ) (.. • ~C. e .'+' . f . .J .i. 4,4\ N. . 1- ..,..• • t ;• c. .14 x il :t.,•, ..,.. ••• .</p>
        <p>, --- • . • • - 1P . . .e .„ • •, AI, .1 0 •ak. . .• • , . • , , ,„ ak,;. 1! I• I a.' • hall t , . ;_ . . • •..?,!/., 4 •</p>
        <p>YOUR ANSWER IS REQUIRED IN JANUARY,</p>
        <p>It throws out of gear the whole financial machinery of the year. Those five points are destined to be the five nails in the coffin of the hereditary usurpers. Lord Morley drove them home with sledge-hammer force. Then he warned the Lords of the gravity of their position. Said he: The Marquis of Lansdowne must have foreseen that the success of this amend- ment and its consequences must lead pretty straight away to constitutional revision. . . . It is the first step on a tremendous journey, and . to-morrow night, as we troop off home, when this theatre is empty and the curtain has fallen upon a completed drama, we shall all know in our hearts that the note has sounded for a very angry and perhaps a prolonged battle.</p>
        <p>guarded by force of statues—(Ministerial cheers)—which will prevent thp indiscrimi- nate destruction of our legislation, of which your work to-night is the climax.</p>
        <p>TARIFFS— THE MOTHER OF TRUSTS. -Published by the Free Prede Union.</p>
        <p>AN AMC4 VL.w C) PRCATI:_- cioN.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Thurs- day the Prime Minister moved : That the action of the House of Lords in refusing to pass into law the financial provision made by this House for the service of the year is a breach of the Constitution and a usurpation of the rights of the Commons.</p>
        <p>After a great scene of enthusiasm in the House and a great historic speech by the Prime Minister, this resolution was carried as follows For 349 'Against •</p>
        <p>Majority 215 And so the war has begun—war between representative government and rule. by a mob of hereditary nonentitiEs—between the dukes fight- ing to evade just taxes and the People —between Food Taxes and Free Trade. THE PROROGATION.</p>
        <p>Both houses met at 2 o'clock yester- day afternoon for the Prorogation. . It is announced by Mr. A. J. Pease that the proclamation of the King dis- solving Parliament would be issued in time to permit of the General Election taking place immediately upon the new register coming into force. \ This Means that the dissolution will take place about the 28th or 29th of December, and that the first elections in the boroughs will be on January Bth, and the first elections in the counties on January 17th or 18th. "LOADED DICE."</p>
        <p>Premier's Scathing Reply to " Worst Insult for 200 Years." Very moving was the spectacle which the House of Commons presented on Thursday</p>
        <p>afternoon (says H. J.in the“ Daily Chronicle.") It was crowded in every part—floor, side- galleries, strangers' galleries, peers' gallery. An indescribable thrill of excitement was in the ,air. It was perceptible through all the confused murmur of voices at question time. Outbursts of laughter could not extinguish it. In moments of silence it persisted like a loud heart-beat. Most of the Cabinet Ministers were in their places during question time. There were two conspicuous absentees. The place of the Leader of the House is empty. Nor is the Chancellor of the Exchequer to be seen. Suddenly (the clock marks eight minutes past three) a cheer breaks forth, soon swell- ing into a deep and sustained roar of welcome. It heralds the advent of Mr. Asquith. As the honoured and trusted leader advances to his seat and comes into full view of the House, Liberal Members in their enthusiasm leap ,to their feet and again vociferate their boisterous hearty welcome. A minute . later, Mr. Balfour, lookinc, a little pinched in feature (he has been suffering from a severe cold) enters, to be the .object of a similar demonstration on the part of his followers.</p>
        <p>It was 3.10 when the Prime Minister• rose to move the motion of which he had given notice, solemnly protesting against the rejec- tion of the Budget by the House of Lords as "a breach of the Constitution and a usurpation of the rights of the Commons." As the familiar and dignified figure, with the strongly-carved face, strangely reminiscent sometimes of Gladstone, stood at the table, the cheers rose. loud and long, and right through the speech they broke forth like peals of thunder, with resounding reverbera- tion. Mr. Lloyd-George entered after Mr. Asquith had been speaking for four or five minutes, at the moment when he was declar- ing with the accent of passionate conviction that the House of Commons would be unwor.thy of its great traditions if it allowed another day to pass without making it clear that it does not mean to brook the gravest indignity and the most arrogant usurpation to• which for more than two centuries it had been asked to submit. A burst of approving cheers greeted this senti- ment, and it was continued as a shout of welcome to the Chancellor of the Exchequer. The Prime Minister has never spoken witft more passion and eloquence, and as usual with Mr. Asquith, the speech was admirably compacted. Its argument marched steadily, irresistibly, from the exordium to the ringing peroration. He began with a reference to the "unexampled circumstances" under which the House met; spoke of the laborious work of the Session, the free, unrestricted discussion of the Budget. At length the Finance Bill left that House—the sifted and deliberate work of an overwhelming majority of the representatives of the people upon a matter which, by unbroken custom, is the "province of this house, and this House alone." In little more than a week this fabric of work and thought was thrown down by the hereditary Chamber. Then came a powerful appeal to the Commons to resist this</p>
        <p>"ARROGANT USURPATION." ." The House of Commons, in the judg- ment of His Majesty's Government, would be unworthy of its power and its traditions if it did not make it clear that it did not mean to brook—(loud Minis- terial cheers)—the gravest indignity, and, I would add, the most arrogant usurpation for more than two centuries, to which it has been asked to submit."</p>
        <p>The offer of the House of Lords to help the Government out of the chaos which the Lords have created was dismissed with scorn by the Prime Minister. It was an invitation to bring in a new Budget trimmed and pruned to suit the prejudices of the House of Lords. (Scoffing laughter from the Liberals). A pretty pass indeed! In burning words Mr. Asquith repelled the suggestion of so gross a humiliation. It would have been treachery to the House of Commons to give it a moment's countenance.</p>
        <p>DECISION TO DISSOLVE PARLIAMENT. Mr. Asquith proceeded to outline the course of action. That course is to advise, as we have advised, the Crown to dissolve this Parliament at the earliest possible moment. (Prolonged Ministerial cheers and counter cheers.) His Majesty has , been graciously pleased to accept that advice, and the result, I trust, will be a new House of Commons assembled at such a time as to make it possible for it to provide both retrospectively and pros- pectively for the needs of the current financial year. If we are fortunate enough to enjoy its confidence—(Ministerial cheers)— then our first act will be to re-impose as from this week all the taxes and duties that were embodied in the Finance Bill, and to validate all past ,collections and deductions. In the meantime all persons desiring to do so may deposit with the proper officers the appropriate duty at the rates so sanctioned, and full instructions and information will forthwith be issued by the Departments concerned. After this crisp and lucid statement on the question of taxes, the Prime Minister entered into the constitutional argument, which has never been more weightily or impressively stated. A trenchant passage followed on the unfairness of the system under which when the Tories are in office we have an omnipo-</p>
        <p>tent House of Commons, when the Liberals are in office we have an omnipotent House of Lords. "We are living under a system of false balances and loaded dice."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>ANOTHER TORY STALWART.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER TORY STALWART.</p>
        <p>LORD JAMES OF HEREFORD AGAINST THE LANDSDOWNE MOTION.</p>
        <p>But the day held further disasters for the usurping oligarchs. Lord James of Hereford threw the whole force of his character and learning upon the side of the Commons. He piled authority upon authority, precedent upon precedent, quotation upon quotation. He told the peers that the financial supre- macy of the Commons is founded upon usage, practice, and acquiescence. He endorsed the Lord Chancellor's distinction between law and constitutional custom.</p>
        <p>He went on to develop with crushing force the case for the Commons and the case against the Lords. He quoted Pym's retort to the Lords in 1640. "You are meddling with finance. We won't allow you to do it !" He quoted Chatham's solemn declaration that to the Commons alone belongs the power of taxation. He quoted Pitt's refusal in 1786 to give the Lords the slightest information. He quoted the words of the King's Speech. He told the peers flatly that they had ,</p>
        <p>NOTHING TO DO WITH THE BUDGET, and that they would not be allowed even to take part in the conflict they were provok- ing. He conjured up the sturdy ghost of John Russell,and made the Painted Chamber ring with his defiance of the Lords.</p>
        <p>With vibrating scorn, this old man, with long white locks flowing picturesquely on his shoulders, told the Lords, that they had no power.</p>
        <p>“-The balance of authority—the conclusive balance of authority—is that there is no competence in this House to reject the Budget.” Finally, Lord James, in solemn tones warned the Lords that they would be at the mercy of their opponents, of men who would not spare them. He stubbornly refused to follow Lord Balfour of Burleigh into the woods. He would vote foi• the Budget, for the Government and for the Commons. He would stick to the path of the Constitution.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>CLOSING SCENES.</head>
        <p>THE CLOSING SCENES.</p>
        <p>ARCHBISHOP LANG AND "THE WRITING ON THE WALL.-</p>
        <p>Tuesday saw the last act, in the drama of disaster staged with so much pomp and circumstance by a dying oligarchy. Not all our prelates have lost their ancient independence. The Archbishop of York .did not deliver the ark of the Constitution into the hands of the Philistines. He defended it with a scholarly eloquence which extorted the respect of his adversaries. His tall, majestic presence dominated the opening scene The Archbishop demolished he plea that there is " tacking" in the Budget. He destroyed the allegation that the Budget is revolutionary. He justified the super-tax, the land taxes, and the liquor taxes. He derided the pretence that the Budget is the thin end of the wedge. In a passage full of gentle irony he told the peers that their old friend "the Thin End of the Wedge," had been invoked against Factory Acts, ' Compulsory Education, ' Protection of Women and Girls, Hours of Labor, Sale of Liquor, Regulation of Sweated Industries. _ _</p>
        <p>Pas-sing from his vindication of the Budget the Archbishop warned the Peers that the handwriting is on the wall. He told them soon the whole position of their house would be submitted to the judgment of the people. Not only their right to control taxation, but also their right to control lesislation would be challenged. Would not their case be stronger if in defending their legislative con- trol they were careful not to assail the taxa- tion and the privileges of the people.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>CLOSED EARS.</head>
        <p>CLOSED EARS.</p>
        <p>The solemn entreaties of the Archbishop fell upon closed eal4. Like the other prophets who have prophesied during this fateful conclave, like Lord Cromer, like Lord Rosebery, like Lord Balfour of Burleigh, like Lord James of Hereford, he appealed to men whose minds were congealed into obduracy, and whose hearts were frozen hard. PEERS IN TRAFALGAR SQUARE.</p>
        <p>LORD COURTNEY'S PICTURE OF THE FUTURE.</p>
        <p>Lord Courtney warned their lordships that they should remember that each one of them was summoned to this Chamber. If</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>ACCEPTING THE CHALLENGE.</head>
        <p>ACCEPTING THE CHALLENGE.</p>
        <p>The peroration of the Premier's great speech sounded the battle-cry in ringing tones:</p>
        <p>Well, Sir, the House of Lords have deliberately chosen their ground. (Opposition cheers.) They have elected to set at nought, with regard to finance the unwritten but time-honoured conventions of our Constitution, and in so doing, whether they foresaw it or not they have opened up a wider and more far-reaching ,issue. (Ministerial cheers,) • We have not provoked the challenge —(cheers and counter cheers)—but we welcome it. (Ministerial cheers.) We believe that the first principles of representative Government, as embodied in our slow and ordered but ever broadening constitutional development, are at stake, and we ask the House of Commons by this resolution to-day—as at the. earliest possible moment we shall ask the constituencies of the country— (cheers)—to declare that the organ and voice of a free people is to be found in the elected representatives of the nation. (Loud Ministerial cheers.) '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>MR. BALFOUR'S SOPHISTRIES..</head>
        <p>MR. BALFOUR'S SOPHISTRIES..</p>
        <p>Mr. Balfour's reply was weak, ineffective, and insincere. He professes to be a House of Commons man, and yet he actually com- plained that the Government had not accepted the offer of the House of Lords to help in mitigating the financial embarrass- ments that the Lords have created. Loud cries of" No ! no" met his statement that only "their own pet vanities" prevent Ministers from accepting the Lords' offer. He proceeded to defend the action of the Lords as constitutional, and flung at the head of his old colleagues Mr. Bryce's axiom that" a second chamber is a political necessity." What have the Lords done over the Budget? Only referred it to the people. Can you persuade the people that they are gping to suffer a great wrong because they are being asked their opinion on the Budget? Such casuistry' on a great occasion is characteristic of Mr. Balfour. A vigorous speech from Mr. Arthur Henderson showed that the Labour party are wholeheartedly with the Government hi this campaign. Then came the division, showing the sub- stantial majority of 215 for the Government. None of the Nationalists voted in the division. There were eighteen Nationalists present, but they deliberately abstained. Has Ireland no concern with the liberties of a nation?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>WARRINGTON LIBERALS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON LIBERALS.</p>
        <p>President's Rousing Appeal.</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles E. Parker, president of the Warrington Liberal Association, has written the following manifesto to members of the Ward Committees :—" The House of Lords, by the practical rejection of the big, brave, Budget, to which the Government and the House of Commons have devoted months of strenuous toil and carried by large and undiminished majorities, has precipitated a national crisis, the issue of which will affect the people's rights for years .to come. 43y the step just taker' the Upper House seeks to take from the people, acting through their representative body, the historic right and power to control finance. Besides this unwarranted interference with the control of taxation the Lords now arrogate to themselves the right to destroy any Liberal Government by rejecting their measures, thus forcing an appeal to the country whenever they think fit. Whilst the Conservatives are in Power the Upper House does nothing, and does it well. But when Liberals are in office every measure touching privilege, wealth, and landed pro- perty is so altered that it has to be dropped altogether or accepted in some mutilated form. It is true that the House of Lords contains a small minority of able men, but for the most part it is composed of persons of untrained, superficial and mediocre intelligence, entirely swayed by class preju- dice and aristocratic environment. The challenge that has been thus wantonly thrown down must be unflinchingly taken up by the whole people. The revolution precipitated by the Peers must be so directed as to make it impossible that this irrespon- sible hereditary thamber shall ever again claim the right to thwart the will of the people's representatives. Let Warrington play its part, and let every lover of demo- cratic rule help in this, momentous crisis."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>WHAT EVERY CVOMAN SHOULD KNOW.</head>
        <p>WHAT EVERY CVOMAN SHOULD KNOW.</p>
        <p>Every woman who values health and appearance or who from a sedentary life—which trany are compelled to live—suffers from headaches, indigestion, chronic constipation and many similar complaints, should use Kelenfold Spa Water " Arabella " a natural remedy and preventative from such ailments., Leading doctors of the day recommend and prescribe it with the best results. ." Arabella "mineral water should be obtained in every town and village. It is absolutely invaluable. Once used it is invariably adopted. " Arabella " should be kept in every home. In small doses it is a perfect aperient for children. Write to Christy and Sons, Old Swan Lane, E.C., for" Hints on Health "a valuable booklet, free.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>VOTE FOR SUICIDE. THE LORDS REJECT THE BUDGET</head>
        <p>A VOTE FOR SUICIDE. THE LORDS REJECT THE BUDGET</p>
        <p>and HAND THEMSELVES OVER TO THE PEOPLE.</p>
        <p>B3ckwoodsmen Bump up to the Majority.</p>
        <p>The Budget is Dead Long live the Budget The black deed has been done. On Tuesday night the House of Lords proved themselves as good as their threats. For the first time in the history of England a Budget providing the means for all the financial needs of the year has been destroyed by the House of Lords. Lord Lansdowne's amendment was carried by the Peers at midnight as follows: 350</p>
        <p>For Against</p>
        <p>Majority for 275</p>
        <p>Liberals and Progressives of al 'kinds are full of rejoicing, for the Budget is not really dead but gone sb'efore, and the Lords have delivered -themselves into the hands of the 'people. The fear was that they might shirk the issue, and only ihalf-do the deed for which they lad been primed, but Lord Lans- downe and his frignds realised they had gone too fat to draw back, and so made a very dramatic end of -themselves politically. The vote need leave no one in any doubt as what the Tissue is. It is not Tariff Reform or .Free Trade thi,s time, but the sacred 'right of the people to govern them- selves--a right which they have held for 300 years and which is now wantonly usurped by a House of hereditary legislators inspired by selfish tinterests. The people's representatives 'say "the land must be taxed." The Lords,who own most of the land, refuse to sanction the tax, which they would rather see spread over the necessaries of life and thus be borne by the poor- est of the poor. This is the situa- tion in its simplicity,, though there are other considerations which have weighed with their Lordships :for 'the' same reason—that they 'touch the lordly pocket. There is -the super-tax on large incomes and the increased death-duties on big fortunes, both very obnoxious to the men who have to pay, though they have escaped for centuries from the responsibilities which were morally and equitably theirs. Then there is the licensed drink trade—nowhere more powerfully repre- sented than in the House of Lords. It too has dodged the tax collector with uncommon skill for a long number of years. Now, 'the" voice of the people has made itself heard through the elected House of Commons, and The Trade is mulcted in dues which are not only just dues but dues over-due. Their Lordships are concerned for The 'Trade. It is an ally of theirs, and a good proportion of its profits find their way into the pockets of the Peers. These are some of the reasons why -the Budget, " the social welfare budget" has died the death in the Gilded Chamber, where national interests are of small occount when put in the balance with the cherished privileges of men who have inherited wealth, title, and a mistaken notion of ,supreme dictatorship.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>But the last word is not with the Lords. They have defied the nation, and it is Snow for the nation to speak. THE LORDS ARE WARNED. 3at Rush to Their Doom.</head>
        <p>But the last word is not with the Lords. They have defied the nation, and it is Snow for the nation to speak. THE LORDS ARE WARNED. 3at Rush to Their Doom.</p>
        <p>'LORD MORLEY'S WEIGHTY WORDS.</p>
        <p>The fifth day of the Lords' Debate was entered upon on Monday when Lord Morley —Honest John of Blackburn—treated a crowded house to a masterly vindication of the rights of the Commons. It was a clear, cold statement of constitutional realities, free from rhetoric and devoid of partisanship. He began by declaring that the Lansdowne resolution involved no fewer than five points, to wit :</p>
        <p>It arrogates to the House of Lords the control of the taxing power; It assumes the power of forcing a penal dissolution by refusing supplies;</p>
        <p>It demands a new Parlia-meni when the sitting Parliament has the misfortune to displease the House of Lords; It proposes to change representative supremacy into oligarchical and non- representative supremacy;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <p>•</p>
        <p>Mr. Chamberlain and the Lords.</p>
        <p>THE PEOPLE WILL NOT SUBMIT."</p>
        <p>It is said the Peers will not give way.. Then I say neither will the people submit.</p>
        <p>We grudge the Lords nothing that rightly belongs to them, nothing they can enjoy without injury to others; their rank and title, their stars and garters; but they claim to dictate the laws which we shall make, the way in which we shall govern ourselves, to spoil, to delay, even eject, measures demanded by the popular voice, passed after discussion by a majority of the people's House, and receiving the sanction and confirmation of popular assemblies such as this —a claim contrary to reason, opposed to justice, and which we shall resist to the death, —Mr. ehamberlain at Hanley, October, ISM.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>Tne Hereditary Principle.</head>
        <p>Tne Hereditary Principle.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. R. Sims, in the " Referee " about 20 years ago,cleverly 4atirised the hereditary principle operating in the House of Lords, His amusing verses have just as much point to-day, as they had then, and will perhaps be read with even greater appreciation.</p>
        <p>THE HEREDITARY PRINCIPLE.</p>
        <p>There was an ocean pilot, and his eldest son wa blind And deaf and dumb from childhood, likewise vacant</p>
        <p>_ - • in. his mind ; _ But of course he was a pilot when his daddy's course</p>
        <p>was run, And he navigated vessels as his father's eldest son,</p>
        <p>There was d clever surgeon, who could cut off legs and arms. And invest an operation in a thousand nameless charms ; He'd an eldest boy who'd never seen an operation done, . But succeeded to the practice as his father's eldest son.</p>
        <p>There was a pious parson who, when folks to danger strolled, . Would perform the part of shepherd and restore t 1 em to the fold; He'd a son, an unbeliever, but when Heaven that parson won, There succeeded to his pulpit his agnostic eldest son.</p>
        <p>There was a judge who ordered wicked criminals to</p>
        <p>, gal; He'd an didest son—a forger—who absconded from his bail: When that judge above was summoned through a</p>
        <p>tintack in, a bun, His vacant place was taken by his outlawed eldest</p>
        <p>The pilot and the parson and the, surgeon and the judge • Were all declared imposters, but they all refused to budge; What mattered lack of knowledge or the evil they had done, While each claimed his proud position as his father's eldest son? „</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Lords' Vet threatens the Social Welfare of the whole Nation!</head>
        <p>The Lords' Vet threatens the Social Welfare of the whole Nation!</p>
        <p>LECTION</p>
        <p>PAGE.</p>
