      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <p>Mr. ASQUITH'S counter- LIBERTY IN stroke is splendid. The PERIL. motion might have gone further, of course. It might have said, for instance,that the action of the House of Lords is not only a breach ,of the Constitution and a usurpation of the powers of the House of Commons, but -that it is also a claim to exercise the privi- leges of the Crown. Mr. QUILLER-COUCH made this clear at Fowey the other night. The Lords assail the rights of the people on one hand, and those of the KING on the other. The PRIME MINISTER, however, has to proceed according to recognised forms. Some things that he might say, and will say no doubt at a later date, cannot be said in a formal resolution. We cannot speak of the King in connection with these matters. He is under- -stood to be outside all party consideration. Nevertheless, the course taken by the House of Lords strikes as much at the King as ,at the Commons. Lord LANSDOWNE'S resolu- tion abolishes the two chief parts of the Con- stitution. For "the King, the Lords temporal and spiritual, and the House of Commons," it substitutes the House of Lords alone. This is intolerable. It would be as reason- able to abolish our present methods of government and substitute a committee .elected by the inhabitants of Colney Hatch. We have no desire to speak of the present situation in anything but moderate language. We desire to appeal in particular to moderate men. It is the moderate mdn who, after all must decide the case. An appeal is being made to the men who never vote. Well, let us leave the issue to them. If they do vote on 'this occasion, they will surely vote in favour of popular control of public affairs. No man in his senses can vote in favour of depriving the House of Commons of its con- trol over the power of taxation, and the King of his right to supplies, which mean not merely the maintenance of his palaces, but -the sustenance of his army and his fleet.</p>
        <p>Let us look straight in the face what has actually occurred. The House of Lords claims to be the supreme power in the country. It not only puts the House of Commons aside, but it puts the King aside? Is this tolerable on any terms? Is it tolerable on any possible grounds? There is only one answer to those questions. Is it not tolerable. Any man who -votes in favour of the position taken up by the House of Lords, is a traitor to his country and an enemy to his own liberty. It is preposterous that we should be entering on a fight for public freedom in the early years of the Twentieth _Century against the aristocrats, plutocrats, brewers, and distillers, in what is sometimes called " The Upper Chamber." Why should we be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>_AL/ • _ .</head>
        <p>_AL/ • _ .</p>
        <p>involved in this conflict Why should the whole country be thrown into turmoil, and its finances into disorder, because these new rich men do not desire to pay their due share of the national taxation? The whole thing is preposterous. It is that and something more. These rich men demand exemption from any addition of taxation because they wish to put the neNv taxes on the poor. We welcome the manifesto issued by the National Liberal Federation. It is some- what too long, perhaps. It is somewhat too grave. It is not in any sense an appeal to what may be described as "popular feeling." It is a very serious document, for it deals with a very really serious situation. The concluding sentence about the average British voter is one that we believe in, and that we feel bound to quote. It is in these terms: "He will vote in the coming election for the right of self-government, for the House of Commons, for untaxed food. He will vote against privilege, against the Peers, and against Protection,"</p>
        <p>There is nothing more TARIFF REFORM certain than the fact IMAGINATION, that if the Conserva- tive party can stimu- late the public imagination to a sufficient extent they will be able to stuff Tariff Reform nostrums down the public throat ad lib. But, fortunately, there are constantly recurring hard facts which cannot be dis- posed of without due mastication, and it behoves the great mass of Free Trade adherents to keep these facts well to the front. A striking instance of the effect of imagination is afforded by the meeting held this week in the Hippodrome by the local branch of the Women's Unionist and Tariff Reform League. Our neutral contemporary gives a very sympathetic report of the pro- ceedings, but unfortunately its imagination does not appear to have been equal to the occasion. In Wednesday's issue we are-told that " the building was crowded, the audi- ence being estimated at from 2,500 to 3,000." Now, being "crowded," we should not have thought the building could hold any more, but this is where imagination comes in and supplies any deficiency, whether mental or statistical. The same report tells us that at the close of the meeting the following telegram was despatched to the Right Hon. Joseph Chamberlain, M.P. :—"Four thousand people at a meeting organized by the Women's Unionist and Tariff Reform Asso- ciation in Warrington, send you their best wish es," etc. Kindly note here the effect of a couple of hours' careful stimulating of the imagination-2,500 becomes 4,000. The Tariff Reformers could almost, if not quite, see double!</p>
        <p>Sir W. J. Crossley, SIR W. J. CROSSLEY'S Bar t., M.P., the CAMPAIGN. respected member for</p>
        <p>the Altrincham Divi- sion, has been making a tour of his constitu- ency, and on Tuesday he received a cordial welcome at Lymm. The large and enthusi- astic ,meeting was a happy augury for the campaign which will soon be in full swing, and afforded unquestionable indications that the progressives of Lymm will rally unitedly to Sir William's support in ,the great struggle for the people's rights. Natur- ally, oppmtunity was taken to congratulate the member upon the well-deserved honour which the King has bestowed upon him, for Sir William's many public-spirited and beneficent acts have gained the esteem of political friends and foes alike. In a homely and very convincing speech, Sir William put the gravity of the present crisis before the meeting. He not only justified the most important proposals of the Budget up to the hilt, but showed that the action of the Lords in refusing the necessary supplies to the King and forcing a general election, is absolutely unconstitutional and a gross abuse of their privileged position. In Sir William Crossley the people of Altrincham division have a sturdy champion of democracy who is follow- ing whole-heartedly the splendid lead of the Premier in resisting the Lords' attempted usurpation, and maintaining inviolate the supremacy of the Commons in matters of finance. We believe they mean to give him a solid backing and rep9at the memorable triumph of 1906.</p>
        <p>At last, and not before</p>
        <p>WAR ON time, some action is IMPROPER BOOKS. being taken with a view to stemming the tide of poisonous literature which has been the object of severe criticism from pulpit, press, and platform for so long. The cry has been mainly against the " problem " novel, and it is 'certainly in this form that most of the unwholesome literature which has become all to common of late has seen the light. But there has been creeping into other classes of books a tone which, to say the very least, is not calculated to elevate, and, altogether, there is little doubt that the action which has been taken by ten of the principal London circulating libraries is justified. These institutions have addressed to the publishing trade a letter stating that they have determined in future that they will not place in circula- tion any books which, by reason of the personally scandalous, libellous, immoral, or otherwise disagreeable nature of their con- tents may prove offensive to any considerable section of their subscribers. The libraries in question further request the publishers to submit all books to them for consideration a week before publication. This is undodbtedly I a strong action, and it may go far towards I solving a very difficult problem. Of course, there is always the danger that such a literary censorship, for it really amounts to that,may go too far. A book which is in the opinion of one person immoral may in the opinion .of another be quite harmless. But, after all, and while keeping this in view, there is undoubtedly room for a great deal of prun- ing. There exists, unfortunately, a large public which seizes eagerly every " spicy " book that appears, and consequently, there is a large staff of writers ready to supply the demand for such matter. But there is an even larger publics which demands healthy literature of all kinds, and it is for this pub- lic that the libraries ought to cater. Their move towards killing the circulation of the poisonous stuff, which is doing such incon- ceivable harm is one in the right direction, and, if the publishers and the public will only do their duty, there is no reason why the plague should not disappear.</p>
