        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>• Extensive alterations to the Corporation baths are advertised, including the provision of two new swimming baths. Lady bathers .will live in hope once again. - Theirs has been a miserable lot; so far,and if any persons had grounds for despair with regard to muni- cipal enterprise the frequenters of the dingy ladies' tank in Legh-street must be the very individuals. • * * *</p>
        <p>The heavy rains of the past three days have caused much discomfort to all but the web-footed denizens of the land, but they have not been without their practical value. It is a long time since the streets, and the sewers, and the river had such a cleansing. The triver was, all yesterday, bank-full of water which was scouring seawards at a great rate.</p>
        <p>A remarkable instance of devotion to duty on the part of a Warrington boy scout has come to light. On a recent Saturday after- noon the local troops were manceuvring and a promising scout was placed on duty near the Grammar School, with instructions to remain there until he was relieved—which should have been a few hours later. By some mischance np one thought of him until mid- night when one of filo scout masters hurried to the scene and found the youngster still Sticking manfully to his post. * * *</p>
        <p>In view, of the Warrington Town Council's intention to erect a ferro-concrete bridge in place of the existing Warrington Bridge, it is interesting to note that the largest ferro- concrete bridge in the world is now in'course of construction in Grafton, New Zealand. A striking illustration of the bridge appears in the "Weekly Graphic and New Zealand Mail" of September 29th of the present year. The foot-note to the picture reads: "We give an illustration of the progress of Grafton. The huge arch of the bridge, spanning a dis- tance of 320 feet, was completed on Saturday last week. The span is the largest in the world in ferro-concrete. It is anticipated that• the Bridge will be completed in February next." * 11,</p>
        <p>The street-lighting of the town is like the curate's egg, only good in parts. We are glad to note that the Street Improvement and Lighting committee is not satisfied with the • general illumination, and has sternly informed the Electricity Committee that unless there is a distinct improvement dur- ing the next two months electric lighting may be discontinued. The ultimatum may not perturb Alderman Smethurst and his Committee very much, but it will give unbounded pleasure in the Gas Department. where the feeling still prevails that electric lighting is an intruder. The general public will be somewhat puzzled to decide which is the. worse : the electric street lighting, or the house gas lighting. Both are open to con- siderable improvement,* _ _</p>
        <p>Time was when the Mersey was a beautiful stream and old Warringtonians longingly recall the days when anglers plied the line in its pellucid waters. With commercial pro- gress has come pollution generally sufficient to scare away all but a few very daring finny visitants. This week, however, the tides have been so high that immense shoals of fish—mostly smelts or sparlings and lampreys have been carried up the river. On Tuesday after the tide had receded some of the workmen at the Mersey Mills found about a score of lampreys clinging to the rocks. The fish were taken to the Town Hall and placed in a large tank. Strarige to say, they still survive their unpleasant adventure, but, truth to tell, some of them look rather sickly! The Ship Canal has been similarly honoured and the men employed at Latchford Locks have, we hear, been break- fasting on smelts caught in large numbers by means of improvised nets. * *</p>
        <p>The Lords are asking for trouble, and indications point to a rare kettleful. The issue, of the coming struggle is the greatest since the Civil War. Then the issue was whether England should rule itself or be ruled by a despotic Monarch. Now the issue is whether it shall rule itself or be ruled by despotic Peers. One despot or six hundred—what is the difference? We do not think that the people who refuse to submit to one are going to submit to the six hundred. * * * _ _</p>
        <p>They came out eipecting to find this battle a sort of Chevy Chase, in which the nobles in their armour should ride across the plain and hunt down the miserable churls of the House of Commons. They found it instead a second Austerlitz, in which they were faced by the guns of the enemy on the one side and a frozen lake of humiliation on the other. They have preferred the guns, and now the guns are beginning to go off. * *</p>
        <p>But as we recall the name of the great Napoleon we must imitate the great Napoleon in not underrating our enemies. The reason why Napoleon won battles like Austerlitz was because he neglected no single detail that would make for a victory, and looked after every button on the gaiters of his army. It is the same spirit that the Liberals must show in the coming campaign. They must leave nothing to chance. For they must remember that, great as is their cause, they have great interests and great forces against them. What are those forces? * * *</p>
        <p>In the first place, they have offended the prejudices and interests of ten thousand powerful men who have acquired by long habit the feeling that they have a right to possess and dominate the land of England. That feeling has now becpme so strong that these ten thousand are prepared to fight very hard before they are going to -be beaten. They are powerful men, and have great influence, dnd it Will require the help of every Liberal in the shires to defeat them.</p>
        <p>That part of the fight must be fought hard, but it must not be fought in a manner to exclude from grace those who wish to take a: sensible and manly course. Whatever these men may say, no one really wishes to deprive them of their land. All that the Budget proposes is that certain mild taxes should be placed 'upon certain land values, produced by the growing of society. There are large land- lords who are willing to pay those dues and who recognise their justice, and in this fight we must welcome the assistance of such land- lords. We make it deaf that the battle is not against a class, but is only against those members of a class who wish to shirk their proper obligations. * * In this fight the big landlords who resist the land taxes will have two great allies. First there will be the brewing interest. That interest has firmly got it into its mind that it has aright to enjoy its monopoly and yet escape all further taxation. The liquor taxes of the Budget are not excessive. There is no new tax on beer. The higher licence duties are only such as have been agreed upon as jiist by. partisans of both sides during the last thirty years, and were even recom- mended by a Qominittee of the House of Lords in theiearly seventies.</p>
        <p>Here again we must disinguish. There are many small publicans who recognise the justice of these taxes, and who will pro- bably help us in our campaign. They ought to be encouraged. It is not against the publicans that the Liberal Party has any fight. On the contrary, they have a. profound pity for the unfortunate publicans who are being squeezed- out and harried by great brewing companies, and there are provisions in this Bill which will prevent any fresh burden falling upon the tenants of the public- houses. But the Budget claims, and properly claims, a fair contribution from the liquor monopoly towards the expenses of the nation.</p>
        <p>The second great ally of the Peers in their fight against the people will be the Tariff. Reform Party. That Party has now come out into the open. It has proclaimed through the voices of • Mr. Chaplin, •Mr. Austen Chamberlain, and Lord Milner— three men who are quite powerful enough to dominate any. Tory Ministry that is returned to power—its intention to tax food. Mr. Waiter Long, and by his silence Mr. Balfour, have both followed in the wake of these leaders, and so the people of England know quite clearly from this moment that Tariff Reform" means firstly and principally the policy of taxing food.</p>
        <p>* * * What will happen if this policy is carried out'? The landlords may perhaps obtain bigger rents, though even that is doubtful. But every other result will be thoroughly bad. The farmers will have to pay over any bigger profits they obtain to their landlords, and the agricultural labourers will have to pay more for their, food without getting higher wages. Meanwhile, in the towns the workman also will have to pay more for his food, and the result will be not a rise of wages but a depression, because the greater penury that will prevail will intensify the competition, and will make men weaker in the presence of their employer. The poorer that the poor become, as we now know, the less chance have they of fighting against their poverty.</p>
        <p>* * * If the people of England. are really going to submit to this sort of treatment they are unworthy of their history and of their liber- ties. This victory of the Lords would mean the greatest shock to human liberty that the world has seen in modern times, but it will not happen. The lists are set; the challenge has-been given, and the issue is now clear. We have confidence in our champions, and we cry as they enter\ the lists, "God defend the right."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>MR. WINSTON CHURCHILL, M.P. TO SPEAK AT WARRINGTON, DECEMBER 18th.</head>
        <p>MR. WINSTON CHURCHILL, M.P. TO SPEAK AT WARRINGTON, DECEMBER 18th.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Liberal Association are indeed fortunate in securing the promise of a visit from the Right Hon. Winston Churchill, M.P. (President Board of Trade). Mr. Churchill has consented to speak at War- rington on Saturday afternoon, December 18th. The request for tickets has already been, so large that if a tithe of the applicants are to be accommodated, it will be necessary to arrange an overflow meeting, as the Parr Hall will be far too small for this occasion. We understand'that Mr. Churchill will in all probability be willing to attend an over- flow meeting and we would strongly advise all our readers who have not already done so to make immediate application for tickets to Howes, jun., at the Liberal Offices, Bewsey-street. gull particulars will be given next week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>ROLLER SKATING.</head>
        <p>ROLLER SKATING.</p>
        <p>EMPIRE RINK.</p>
        <p>The Empire Rink has been catering for the public in an excellent manner this week for Monday, Tneiday, and Thursday were special nights. On Monday Crosfield's Band was in attendance under the conductorship of Mr. E. Smith and the large number of skaters fully enjoyed skating to the splendid music ,rendered. On Tuesday evening the Empire Hockey Club played Chorlton H.:l. The home team troubled. the visiting goal keeper cons- sider,ably but were only successful in getting one goal which was scored by Berry during the first half. Result Empire H.C. 1 ; Chorlton H.C. 2. On Thursday evening a grand march and musical chairs were held. The march was led by Mr. F. Kinsey and Miss Elsie Chalmers and it was really a fine sight to see about 300 skaters in the procession. The musical chairs caused considerable amusement and the prize Was awarded to Naster Stanley Blackstoek. On Wednesday evening the Empire H.C. played the White City H.C. away and drew, the score being one each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>NEXT WEEK.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening next a whist drive and social are to take place when four valuable prizes are to be competed for. The prices are as tmial.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>WHO WAS THE OWNER?</head>
        <p>WHO WAS THE OWNER?</p>
        <p>Interesting Point of Law in a Warrington Case.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court, on Thursday, before his Honour Judge Shiress Will, K.C, Mrs. Mary Brareton, Normanhnrst, Fairfield-road, Latchford With- out, brought an action against Messrs. G. and J. Weaver, furnishers and upholsterers, Bridge-street. for the return of certain articles of furniture, value £lO, alleged to have been wrongfully taken under an execution levied against her husband, Randall Brereton, contractor.—Mr. W. B. Forshaw appeared for the plaintiff, and Mr. A. Browne for the defendants. Mr. Forshaw said the case involved the well-known principle that where one person purchased property and another provided the money, the property belonged to the person who provided the money. He submitted that if he proved that his client bought the furniture, she was entitled to the verdict. Mr. and Mrs. Brereton were married ,on February 22nd, 1907. Their engagement had met with home opposition and thinking that the opposition might be likely to separate them they decided to he married. The husband, however, had no money to commence a home with, and for a time they lived with their respec- tive parents. The wife, however, had £56 in the Post Office Savings Bank and she ultimately suggested to her husband that they should have a home of their own and she would buy the furniture. At her request Mr. Brereton went to Messrs. W,iyiver*s shop and selected furniture to the value of .C 27 which was paid for with his wife's money. He told Mr. Weaver that it was his wife who wanted the furniture. Later a second lot of goods was ordered by Mr. Brereton on his own aeconnt and it was for that debt that execution was levied. Plaintiff did not sue for the return of any of the Roods included in the second purchase; her claim was simply for the furniture which she had personally paid for. Evidence was given by the plaintiff and her husband. it was admitted that the accounts were made out in Mr. Brereton's name but he said he told Mr. Weaver that his wife was buying the furniture.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne said t defence was that the firm never recognised Mrs. Brereton from beginning to end of the transaction. Her name was not mentioned to them. If bargains of this kind wen to be allowed, where would the tradesmen be? He did not accept the proposition that the person who provided the purchase money must necessarily be the owner of the goods. It depended upon what pasied between the ,tradesman and the purchaser. Mr. Brereton did nrut disclose his agency and therefore he was the owner. He was the tenant of the house. George We,aver, James Weaver, and William Henry Baker gave evidence to the effect that Mrs. Brereton was not mentioned in connection with the transaction and the defendants were under the impression that they were dealing solely with the husband. "</p>
        <p>His Honour said be was perfectly satisfied that Mrs. Brereton's money paid for the purchase. The question was not what Messrs. Weaver a.nd their employees wrote in their accounts end ledgers, but.to whom did the furniture elong ? The person who paid for it was presumably the owner. There was no evidence that the payment was a loan from the wife to the husband. He gave judgment for the plaintiff with costs..</p>
        <p>COAGULINE, TENASITINE.' Cements for mending all things. Gd. each.—Adtt..</p>
        <p>Personal. - Warringtonians will be interested to hear. that Mr. A. J. Ashton. K.C., who has been appointed the Com- missioner to,hold the inquiry respecting the conduct of the Liverpool police in dealing with the recent disturbance of the peace, is the eldest son of the,late Mr. Walter Ashton, manager for many. years of the Manchester and Liverpool District Bank, Sankey- Street, Warrington. Mr. Ashton was born at War- rington in 1855, and was first educated at the Boteler Grammar School.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. L. Blease who has been as chosen Liberal candidate for Chorley in opposition to Lord Balcarres is the son of Mr. Walter Blease of Messrs. Blease and Son, Liverpool who are auditors to Messrs. Pearson and Knowles.</p>
        <p>At the recent examination held in Liverpool in connection with the Victoria College of Music, Miss Lily Taylor, 14, Enville-street, Latchford, a pupil of Miss M. Hewitt, A.V.O M., passed the intermediate grade, first class pass with honours. Election Expenses. _</p>
        <p>The following additional returns of election expenses have been received at the Town Hall :—Fairfield Ward: Mr. G. N. Vernon. £2B 13s. 9d. • Orford: Councillor Elijah Cumiington, £l4 12g.; Latchford: Mr. Thomas Hewitt, 110.125. •,‘Belvsey : Councillor Owen Toole, 12s. 6d.; St. John's: Mr. Jams Shaw £l3 4s. 3d.</p>
        <p>Warrington Women's Circle Bridge-street. Mrs. Clatworthy presided on Sunday, and a unique address on the sigh of Christ in restoring to the man his power of speech was given by Mrs. Gladding. A recitation by Miss Prince was enjoyed. On Friday evening the Juniors trained by Mrs. Letheren gave Christmas choruses maid of their stall. Small prizes for the hest dressed dolls were awarded to Elsie Brown, Eleanor Monks and Annie Eustance. Recita- tions and action songs were added. "HERALD" KITCHEN RANGES Rive 50 per cent of fuel.—Agent J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, 51 to 55 Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. Mayoral "At Home." The Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. W. J. Forshaw), held an "At Home" at the Town Hall on Thursday afternoon. The weather was most unfavourable, rain falling heavily during the greater part of the afternoooa, and consequently the attend- ence was not as large as usual. The entrance hall had been most tastefully decorated with choice chrysanthemums by Mr. R. Whitfield, the head gardener, and presented a bright and inviting appear- ance. The guests were received in the Council Chamber, the Mayor and Mayoress being accompanied by Mr. W. J. Forshaw and the Misses Forshaw. Light refreshments were served, Mr. and Mrs. Powis baying charge of the catering. An orchestra was in attend- ance and played a varied and pleasing Programme. Hospital Sunday.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Hospital Sunday Com- mittee was held at the Infirmary yesterday. Mr. John Fairclough presided and there were also present : Councillor H. Roberts, Mr. A. J.Joiley (hon.s‘ecretary) and Mr. J. H. J.Hampson (secretary of the Infirmary). The hon. secretary reported that four places of worship which contributed to•the fund in 1908 failed to do so this year. Two commenced or ranewed their contributions. On balance there was a loss of £6 Is. 4d. under those heads. The total receipts were £293 15s. 10d. an increase as com- pared with last year of 8 13s. (id. Of this amount £lBl 18s. 6d. was collected within and 1111 ]7s. 4d. outside the County Borough. The expenses (printing, adveriising and postages) amounted to £lB 10s. 4d. as against £l7 16s. 10d. last year.—The report was adopted, the chairman remarking upon the fact that in proportion to population the places of worship out- side .the borough seemed to contribute more than those in the town.—Mr. Jolley, who ha S held the secretaryship of the Committee for fifteen years, was cordially thanked for his past services and re-elected. Hospital Sunday was fixed for March 6th, 1910. " PEVERIL " FIRE GRATES still unsurpassed, Inspection invited.—.T. S. GARLAND, Kitchen Range and Stove Warehouse, 51 to 55, Bridge at., Warring- ton.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Latchford Wesleyan Church Bazaar.</head>
        <p>Latchford Wesleyan Church Bazaar.</p>
        <p>As will be seen from an advertisement in another column, it is intended to hold a grand bazaar in con- nection with the above church in the schoolroom, Knutsford-road, on Wednesday, Thursday and Friday next. It will be opened on Wednesday at 2.30 by Mrs. A. H. Crosfield, the chair being taken by Mr. James Dodds; on Thursday at 2.20 Mrs. Chsrles E. Parker will open the ,bazaar, Mr. J. G. Withinshaw presiding; whilst on Friday, at 3 o'clock. Mrs. Gunnell will declare it open, Mr. George Weaver taking the chair.—An excellent orchestra is to be in attendance and play selections during the afternoon and evening. There will be the usual variety of stalls, provided with numerous articles to suit all tastes, at all prices. Concerts and entertainments have been arranged for each evening, and a collectiori of interesting curios will be exhibited. The proceeds of the bazaar are to be applied to the liquidation of the debt of £450 ou the new Sunday school building Widow's Compensation Money. An application was made to Judge Shiress Will, KC., at the Warrington County Court, on Thursday by Mr. H. J. Longton, solicitor, on behalf of Sarah Jane Sykes, widow of Charles Sykes, Corporation electrician, who was killed through falling from a ladder whilst trimming an electric street lamp, for the disposal of the sum of 1235, cornpensatinn paid into nourt by the Warrington Corporation. Mr. Longton asked for the money to be allocated as follows :- 113 12s. Gd. for funeral expenses; £5 lls, for costs of removal from Warrington to Huddersfield; the balance of the money to be paid out for investment in two cottages to be purchased in Huddersfield, on the advice of applicant's father and brother, who were connected with the, building trade. The widow was the sole dependent—His Honour said it was an unAsual thing to pay out the whole of the amount at once, but he was satisfied that the applicant would be guided by the advice of her father and brother. and he would therefore grant the application. TABLIE AND HANGING LAMPS, new new designs showing.—.l. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. Musical Service at the Parish Church.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Warrington Parish Church Past and Present Choristers' Association a musical festival will be held in the Parish Church next Thursday evening when the F rester part of Mendels- Sohn's oratorio St. Paul" will be performed by local soloists and a choir of upwards of eighty voices. It is interesting to note that the association, which is the only one of its kind in the cauntry,was formed through the instrumentality of Mr. Row-ley, who preceded 31i. W. S. Woods as organist at the church. Its primary object is to keep in touch with all members who have passed through the choir, and to take an interest in their subsequent welfare. Other objects include the formation of a fund to be devoted entirely to the choir boys of the Parish Church, grants being made yearly for that purpose ; assistance being rendered to any deserving case of a member in distress, while the interest of the invested fund is devoted to aid any boy who shows exceptional ability in music. By much effort and self-sacrifice this fund has now reached £9O, and it is the earnest desire of the• association that it may this year be raised to at least £l2O. With this object in view, the special service is being held. The soloists will be Miss E. Taylor (soprano), Miss J. Armstrong (contralto), Mr. J. H. Draper (tenor), and Mr. J. B. Poole(bass).</p>
        <p>Bankrupt and His Accounts. BenjaminN Ginsburg; fancy goods dealer in the Warrington and other markets and living in GOlhorne- street, appeared for his further examination at the Warrington Bankruptcy Court before Registrar Ridgway. Mr. Johnson, assistant official receiver, mentioned that the debtor had filed certain accounts in obedience to an Order of the Court, but he thought that the registrar would agree that the account was not sufficient. They wanted if possible to trace the debtor's transaCtions. In his cash account he had simply named a rump sum of £5OO as his estimated takings at the vaiious markets. It was quite impossible for them to investigate or look into, an account of that kind and it must be obvious that it was perfectly useless as it was:—Mr. Browne who appeared for the debtor said that if Mr. Johnson would suggest how a better account Could be-rendered they 'would do it, but he contended that it was physically impossible.—Mr. Johnson thought that they were not asking for anything unreasonable. What they wanted were debts and individual accounts of the same. There were yery good reasons why those accounts should be pressed for and he earnestly asked the Registrar to make a further order.—Mr. Browne urged that it was impossible to provide a more detailed account than had already been • put in, but the Registrar ultiMately ordered - a further account tobe filed befOre the 31st December.:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Infirmary.</head>
        <p>The Infirmary.</p>
        <p>The Hon. Treasurer begs to acknowledge, with thanks,the sum of £5 from the Warrington Caledonian Association, being proceeds from their recent Scottish concert.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>" Y " Branch N.B.W.T.A.</head>
        <p>" Y " Branch N.B.W.T.A.</p>
        <p>The udual meeting was held on Wednesday at Silver-street Girls Club. In the absence of a speaker the president °Coupled the chair. . Mr. T. IL Elliott and Miss Lloyd Jones rendered songs which were greatly enjoyed by the girls.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Young People's Society. • Mr. C. Eccles presided at the weekly meeting. of this society which was held on Monday evening in the Memorial Buildings. Miss N. Womersley read a paper on "Women and girls as inventors and discoverers," and it proved to be very interesting and enjoyable.</p>
        <p>XMAS PRESENTS. Large selection in silver and geld. Jewellery, Electro-plated and Silver Articles.. —MILLER Co., Sankey st.—Advt. A Young WarrlngtOn</p>
        <p>At a Chamber Con—cert held at the Memorial Hall, Manchester,on Monday, Master Edward S.Roy, 13 years of age, of 25, Cairo-street, played (a)Romance in F (J. Richardson), (b) Minuet O.P. 19 (W. H. Squire) before a crowded audience, and was heartily applauded for his solos ; his quality of tone and style receiving well- earned compliments from a number of friends present, who wished him a successful future. Local Musical Successes.</p>
        <p>At the recent examination held at Liverpool November 22nd and following days in connection with the Victoria College of Music, London, the following pupils of Mrs. G. Mayhall A.V.C.M. London 148, Knutsford-rd, Warrington, passed very, success- fully. Names and grades as follows. Associate: Mr. George Martin, Hall Street, Warrington, Ist cPass pass. this entitles him to affix the letters A.V.C.11, to his name, and also to wear the Cap and Gown of the college. Intermediate.: Miss Bessie Newton, Knuts- ford Road, Warrington, Ist class pass with honours. Miss Beatrice Dora Moorfield, Hewitt-st, Warrington, Ist class pass with honours. _ MILLER &amp; Co. (opposite Trinity Church), for Whist Drive Prizes. Large Stock.--Advt. Lectures on Tennyson. • - - - - - -</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. L. Sch-roeder delivered the last of the course of University Extension Lectures on "Tennyson : his art and personality" in the Con- servative Club Assembly Room on Wednesday even- ing. The special subject for consideration was Tennyson's dramatic work and the lecturer dealt in a most interesting way with the trilogy of historical plays—" Harold," " Becket " and "Queen Mary."— At the close a hearty vote of thanks was passed to the lecturer on the proposition of the Rev. C. Harvey Cook seconded by Airs. Stansfield. Penny Popular Concert. . .</p>
        <p>An -excellent entertainment was given before a large audience on Saturday evening by the Veronas Concert Party, Liverpool. The programme was nicely varied and included songs, duets, and a humorous dialogue. A humorous duet," The lady palmist," by Miss Lil Kellett and Mr. Fred Carlton caused great merriment. The party gave a concerted item, "Come to St. George's" with pleasing effect. A humorous duet, "It must be me,' by Messrs. W. Hickey and Bert Kaye, was well received and Mr. Fred Canton sang" Send for a policeman" in a creditable manner. " Mr. Robson's Dilemma," a humorous dailogue, by Miss Lil Kellett and Fred Carlton, was a popular feature. Mr. Carlton appeared as Mr. Robson (a pro- fessor of music) and Miss Kellett as Mrs. Robson his wife. During .the interval the Rev. Thomas Miller gave a short address, in which .he spoke about the evil of betting and gambling at sports.</p>
        <p>FOR WHIST DRIVE PRIZES you cannot do better than see MILLER &amp; CO's selection.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Rylands' Recreation Club. A grand miscellaneous concert took place in the Gorsey-lane, on Wednesday evening. Miss Carrie Hastings sang " Bid me -discourse " and " Walz Song " very tastefully. Miss H. Bancroft's singing of "Flight of Ages" and "Tit for Tat" was highly appreciated by the audience. Mr. J. Straw, solo , concertina, was well received for his selection from 'Maritana," and had to respond. Mr. A. Allinson, solo s'Oprano (cornet), in his renderings of "0 lovely night" and " Star of Bethlehem" capti- vated the ears of all. Mr. J. Willet sang " Ailsa Mine" and " Evensong" in his usual fine style. Mr. T. Humphreys sang "Guiding Light" and "Bedouin Love Song" with effect. Mr. Farrington, juvenile comedian, had a hearty reception. Mr. Alf Grounds, humorist, who gave two humorous musical sketches, created roars of laughter and had to respond repeatedly. Mr. T. Taylor accompanied in his usual manner and finish.—The whole concert was a huge success, and was only marred by the inclement weather.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Funeral of Mr. John B. Caldwell.</head>
        <p>Funeral of Mr. John B. Caldwell.</p>
        <p>The funeral of the late Mr. John Bernard Caldwell, who died at his residence on November 24th, took place last Saturday. In the morning a solemn Requiem Mass was celebrated in St.. Alban's Church. The range was for the most part Gregorian. Mr. J. akersley presided at the organ and the celebrant was the. Rev. Father Whittle and the Cantors the Rev. Fathers Primavesi and L. Buggins. The male voice choir was in attendance. A large crowd gathered at the Cemetery to pay their tribute of respect to the deceased gentleman. The Rev. L. Buggins performed the last rites.. The mourners were as follows: Masters Bernard and John Caldwell (sons), Messrs. Edmund and Peter Caldwell (brothers), W. and C. Unsworth, T. R. Knowles (brothers-in•law). Mr. W. H. Payton was also *sent. The choir boys of St. Alban's were at the graveside. Wreaths were sent by the follow- ing Family," Mr. and Mrs. W. Unsworth, Mr. and Mrs. C. Unsworth, Mr. and Mrs. T. R. Knowles, Dr. and Mrs. Geary, St. Alban's Choir, employees of Caldwell's, Ltd., Mrs. Brierley, Mr. and -Mrs. E. Byerley, Mr. and Mrs. Warren, Miss Webb,. Mr. and Mrs. E. Connor, Mr. and Mrs. Partridge, and Mr. and Mrs. W. C. Brooks.</p>
        <p>MILLER &amp; CO., 10, Sankey at., Jewellers. Xmas Presents, great variety, from ls.—Advt. Boy Scouts' Display. If the grand display of the Warrington end District boy scouts which was held in Crosfield's Dining Hall on Thursday evening had done nothing else but bring before the townspeople the objects of the movement it would have gone a long way to satisfying its pro- moters. However, notwithstanding the inclement weather there was a fairly large attendance pre- sided over by Captain Crosfield, and there- fore the affair should be a success financially. The objects of the concert were to provide funds for organisation, camping materials and a club room. The number of scouts in Warrington and district is about 00, and 50 Of these took part in the display. Girls are also included. They are indeed, a smart set of lade and lasses and a fine testimony to the movement. The sc'arts gave a picturesque display, both individ- ually and collectively, and the programme was also varied by musical items from Miss Silcock and Mr. W. Dutton, and members of the corp. A short sketch, "How the Ist Loamshire troop distinguished them selves,' written by Mr.J. B. Starkey,was well performed Much credit is due to Messrs. J. B. Starkey, 11. L Bairsto and R. Prince for the training of the scouts and also to Messrs. R. Ronald and H. Hampton, ambulance instructors. Captain Crosfield and Major Appleton were thanked at the cicse for the interest they \were taking in the movement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>SOME RECENT "HOWLERS."</head>
        <p>SOME RECENT "HOWLERS."</p>
        <p>As the result of a recent examination we have been favoured with the following state- ments of historical fact: Hawkins and Frobisher discovered the district which goes by the name of Westward Ho!</p>
        <p>Sir W. Raleigh sailed round the world and founded potatoes and tobacco. In the reign of Elizabeth Rhodesia was discovered by Cecil Rhodes. It is also called Virginia, after the Vigin Queen. The Pilgrimage of Grace consisted of a band of men who by their wives and children were called Pilgrim Fathers. In this reign (Ed. VI.) people abolished the Pope and Roman Catholicism. When Augustine landed in England and reformed the people by teaching Christianity,this was the first stage of the Reformation. No more was heard of it until Edward VI.</p>
        <p>Mary firmly believed in the Act of "do heredito comprehendo." Mary's disposition was soured, so she become very religious and burnt many people. Henry VII. was crowned on the field of) Bosworth) by Dean Stanley,</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND' with warm water is an excel-. lent gargle for sore throat, Colds, Coughs, &amp;c.,—Atlrt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>GAMING WITH COINS.</head>
        <p>GAMING WITH COINS.</p>
        <p>Thomas Stringer, 17, Manley-street, was' charged with gaming with coins for money in a public passage at the rear of Cartwright-atreet on November 27th.— Sergeant Stuart said that in consequence of complaints about gambling, he proceeded to Cartwright-street in company with Detective Clough aud they secreted themselves in an empty house near by. At a quarter past twelve, prison( r, with about eight others com- menced to play "pitch and toss." Soon afterwards they changed the game to "head it." After a while, prisoner ceased to play and started betting on the money. Witness left his hiding place and took prisoner into custody. The others ran asLay. There were also a number of lads about 15 years of age standing round. —Prisoner was tinedlOs. and costs 16s. 6d. or fourteen days.</p>
