      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>GOLF PAVILION TBIEVES.</head>
        <p>GOLF PAVILION TBIEVES.</p>
        <p>Two young men named James Newell and William Lofthouse, said to belong to Patricroft, were at the Altrincham Petty Sessions en Saturday, committed for trial at quarter sessions on charges breaking and enter- ing the golf pavilion at Dunham Massey on two occasions, and the golfhouse of the Warrington Club at Appleton, and stealing therefrom a number of articles. The value of the articles alleged to have been stolen from the Warrington clubhouse was given as £2 Is. Bd. Inspector Owen announced that there was another charge arzainst the prisoners in respect of the golf pavilion at Bazhe, near Chester, but it was not intended to go into that case then. 'lt was intended to mention it to the chairman at quarter sessions, with a view to his taking it into consideration when sentencing! the prisoners there.—lnspector Sharp stated that Newell had informed him that morning where he was likely to find the lady's gold watch.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>HEAVY FINE FOR STREET BETTING.</head>
        <p>HEAVY FINE FOR STREET BETTING.</p>
        <p>John Brown, a young man of 14, Wellington-street, appeared at the Borough Police Court on Monday on a charge of loitering at the reir of Ellesmere-street and Wellington-street on November 27th for the purpose of receiving bets. He pleaded guilty.—P.C. Woollacott said that acting on instructions from the Chief Con- stable, he proceeded to the neighbourhood of Ellesmere- street and Wellington-street and there saw the prisoner receive betting slips, including football coupons, and some cash from a man. Witness went up to prisoner and offered him a betting slip and then arrested him. Prisoner said "It is a fair cop. I could not tell you in that guise." He was brought to the Police Station and on being charged said "That is right." On being searched be had 31 betting slips relating to horses running in the Manchester November Handicap, and 18 football coupcns, with £3 ss. 6d. in cash corresponding with the slips.—The Chief Constable said prisoner bad defied the police with impunity. He had been caught by Sergeant Stewart before, but .he said he would tee his solicitor and all that sort of thing, and the matter dropped. He had been taking bets, &amp;c., and all that kind of thing for months past. There was nothing to be said in his favour with regard to the betting at all. He did not think he was a "master's man." Prisoner was fined £lO and costs and the slips were forfeited.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED EXTENSIVE RAILWAY ROBBERIES At the Borough Police Court on Wednesday William Warburton, of Friars Green, was charged with steal- ing from a warehouse of the London and North-Western Railway on November 27th, three parcels, each con- taining 18 shirts, of a total value of £2 ss.—The Chief Constable said that the prisoner was arrested the previous day and it was only early that morning that they were able to.establish a case against him. Prisoner had made several admissions and it was essential that he should ask for an adjournment of eight days in order that the Railway Company might make the necessary enquiriss. On Saturday the prisoner with other men who were at pres3ot in custody with charges against them were seen by Detective Reddington and detective officers of the London and North-Western Railway to leave the warehouse carrying parcels. At one of the men's houses 31 pawn tickets and a large number of shirts were found; which probably were stolen last Saturday.—A remand was granted for eight days. At the Police Court, on Thursday, Edwin Hamer, Thomas Maines, and Edward Maines' residing at 32, Howard-street ; Joseph Tracey, 28, Catherine.street ; John Horsfall, 48, Gladstone-street, were charged 'with stealing a number of shirts and other clothing from the goods warehouse of the London and North-Western Railway.—lt was stated by the Chief Constable that an adjournment of the case was necessary, as in all proba- bility other serious charges would be preferred against the defendants. Another man named Warburton was in custody in connection with the robberies. The police had visited the , respective homes and found a number of handkerchiefs, shirts, quilts, &amp;c.—The magis- trates adjourned the case for a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>THREE INCORRIGIBLE YOUTHS. SMART PENALTIES.</head>
        <p>THREE INCORRIGIBLE YOUTHS. SMART PENALTIES.</p>
        <p>Three youths, named James Hirri., Bank-street, Joseph Cullen, Bank-street, and William Worsley Scotland-road were brought up on remand at the Borough Police Court on Wednesday. Harris and Cullen were charged with stealing from 61, Winwick- street, a clock, 0 packets of Woodbine cigarettes and a quantity of tobacco the property of Sarah Jane Knight on November 22nd and they were also charged with stealing from a shop in Sectland-road 14 packets of Woodbine cigarettes value le. 2d. the property of F. Wisedale. All three prisoners were further charged with stealing from tbe cash draw in the shop of 60 Legh-street 3s in cash the property of John W lliam Walters on November 10th.—The Chief Constable said that Mrs. Knight was a grocer carrying on business at 60 Winwick-street. On the day in question the prisoner Harris went into the shop and asked Mrs. Knight if she had any playing cards for sale. She replied that she had only toy packets at id. each, Prisoner left the shop but returned at half-past five and took the articles the subject of the charge. The clock was sold to 'a Mrs. Brindley for is. Bd. The police were informed and P.C. Holland made inquiries and recovered the clock and some of the cigarettes. With regard to the second charge the prisoners took the cigarettes from the counter. Inquiries were made and it was found that Harris and Cullen had been selling the packets of cigarettes at two packets for lid. On Wednesday 10th November, Harris went into the chip potato shop, 60, Legh-street, and stole 33. from the till there. Tne other prisoner were outside and they divided the money. Continuing the Chief Constable said there were no fewer than eight charges against the prisoner Harris who was no MOTO than a lad. Harris had been eight times previously before the Court, four times for steal- ing. Cullen had four previous convictions awl Worsley ten times previously for different offences. Harris was sentenced to nine months hard labour, Cullen six months hard labour,and Worsley three months hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>A striking performance at the Palace and Hippo- drome this week is that of Addie Donna, in the amusing sketch "Dr. Spot's Patients." The lady impersonates five successive patients Who call upon the doctor in the space of a few minutes, and are remarkably different in characterand appearance. At no time is Addie Donna off the stage for more than a minute and her changes of Tnake-up and costume are remarkably expert. In addition she has a talent for oomedy and keeps the house in roars of laughter. The Leotard Brothers specialise as hand balancers and hat-throwers, and their skill is undoubted. The turn would be improved if the performers 'did not keep up a continuous stream of silly jokes in baby talk. Their jugglery is good enough to stand bY itself. A- lady ventriloquist is a- decided novelty, and Florence Raymond makes a decided hit in the role. Her patter lacks originality which a, little study might furnish. Tom Moore is a decidedly droll comedian. His quiet style is a pleasant change from so much of the burlesque which we see in the halls, and his humour is infectious. Kioto gives a marvellous exhibition of balancing and anatomical suppleness. To see him mount a pile of small bricks, and then bend over backwards till his hands and feet rest on the same small surface, and change. from one support to the other, is a spectacle not readily forgotton. The Jap I's a little wonder, and bets an example few will be inclined to emulate. Nellie Lunelle is a capital dancer, and she ought to realise that her fortune is in her feet and not in her voice. Rayburn; and Dix present an amusing " due " entitled" Skits on the phone "and Alunroe's Comedy team of boys combine singing and foolery in a more or less pleasing style - The Palace pictures this week record an adventure in Paris by the great Houdini and the orchestra plays consistently well.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
