      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>METAL DEALER HEAVILY FINED.</head>
        <p>METAL DEALER HEAVILY FINED.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court, on Thursday morning, Joseph Matthews, marine store dealer, 14thgoes-lane was charged with acting as a marine store dealer without the necessary licence. Five other charges of buying metal under the required weight were also preferre(lagainst him.. .</p>
        <p>The, Chief Constable explained that on various dates in October and November. the defendant purchased various quantities of scrap metal. In a police court case last week when three men were sent to gaol for stealing lead and copper, defendant admitted in evidence that he carried on the business of a marine store dealer. The defendant had not been registered as a marine store dealer during the past twelve months. Chief Inspector Williamson provtd that the defen- dant had not been registered as a dealer for the past twelve months.</p>
        <p>- In reply t) Mr. Steel witness admitted there were many otheil dealers carrying on business who were not registered. For the defence Mr. Steel said his client bad carried on business for the past five years, and was unaware tlpit a licence was necessary. The police knowing 'he was unlicensed, did not give him the slightest warning. The defendant was not desirous of avoiding the law. If it were necessary for him to takeout a licence he would do so, and it his application were refused he would then give up the business. The Chief Constable said Matthews bad been eight times before the court. He had also been convicted of a similar offence at Oldham.</p>
        <p>The magistrates imposeda fine of £5 and costs or one month.</p>
        <p>In reference to the other five charges of purchasing metal under weight, as required by the Prevention of Crimes Act, the Chief Constable stated that the police found from an examination of the defendant's disry that a number of small purchases of brass and other metal had been made. One one occasion the defendant wilfully bought fill). of brass whilst a detective was on the premises making inquiries with regard toe robbery. The police for many years had the greatest difficulty in dealing with that class of offence. Small robberies would continue to take place when there were facilities for disposing of the proceeds. Mr. Steel contended that the police were piling on the agony against the defendent. It, was whilst the police had in their posss ssion the diary that the informa- tion was got for the support of those charges. The result of such a procedure would he that dealers would not be willing to help the police in tracing metal robberies.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said the defendant bad never willingly assisted the police. In regard to the case last week the police had practically to drag out the informa- tion from him.</p>
        <p>The magistrates imposed a fine of £5 and costs in the first case, and 40s. and costs in the remaining four charges. The fines in all cases totalled £2l 9s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Warrington Borough Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before the Mayor (Chairman) and Mama W. Roberts S Wright W Furness, H. Thornton, r</p>
        <p>Locker and G. Pilling. ARREARS OF MAI NTENANCE.—JOhn Clarke of Littons Row, was brought up on warrant for non- payment of arrears of reformatory maintenance to the amount of El ls.—Prisoner was ordered to pay the money or go to prison for a month in default.—Henry Humphreys of Philip Street was charged with a similar offence his arrears amounting to £1 6s. 6d. The money was paid and defendant was discharged .</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Councillor H. Roberts (in the chair), C. Jackson Holmes, Alderman Bolton and Dr. Fox. "Rip ME,UP WITH A KNlFE."—Samuel Collins, a young man of Deakin's-yard, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Deakin's-yard on Saturday night, and also with assaultine James Mannion.—James Mannion said that prisoner bit him on the cheek for no apparent reason. He was drunk at the time.— Detective Clough said that at 11.30 on Saturday night he saw prisoner in a drunken state. Be asked him to go home several times and took him within 20 yards of the door. Prisoner threatened to " rip me up with his knife."—Prisoner who bad been before the Court 10 times previously, as fined 10s. and costs or 14 days' imprisonment. FRlDAY.—Before Mr. H. Thornton (in the chair), Alderman Pierpoint, 'Messrs. A. J. Jolley, G. Pilling, and T. Locker.</p>
        <p>"COMMERCIAL" FINED FOR TRAVELLING WITHOUT A TICKET.—Robert Hoult, commercial traveller, 140 Fox-street, Edgeley, Stockport, was summoned for travelling on the L. and N.-W. Railway on September 27th and 28th, without having a ticket. Defendant did not appear.—Mr. H. Tait, solicitor, Easton, prosecuted for the Company and said that on September 27th ticket collector Dawson vi as on duty at Bank Quay Station when the defendant came off the plattoTm When asked for his ticket he replied" Contract" and produced a monthly contract season ticket between Warrington and St. Helens, issned in the name of J. Williams. From inquiries ticket collector Blinstou challenged him the following dty when defendant gave as his excuse that he bad left his contract at home. On the following morning defendant booked a third-class ticket to Earlestown and informed the collector that he had sent his season ticket to Manchester.—The magis- trates imposed a fine of 20s. and costs in each case, or one month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington and D:strict Rural Council was held in the Berey Chambers on Wednesday afternoon, when Mr. R. Forshaw presided. There were also presfent :—Messrs. J. Backhonse (vice- chairman), W. Houghton, W. Stanier, A. Brooks, J. Ball, W. Furness, W. Mather J. C. Sutton (clerk), B. S. Newcombe (surveyor), H. H. Btirnage (assistant surveyor), Dr. Sephton (medical officer), and F. Kerfoot (inspector).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>SPITTLE BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>SPITTLE BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>The CLERK said the General Purposes and Finance Committee advised payment to Messrs. Robert Davies and Co., of 18s. for purchase of land bought from Mallinsons. Messrs. Robert Davies and Co.. agreed to accept transfer from the widow and thus the absurdly expensive resort to compulsory powers bad been avoided. The professional services of Messrs. Rdbere Davies and Co. bad extended from 1908 to present date and the particulars of such services were detailed in the 78 entries on the nine pages of bill paper before the Council. He (the Clerk) had perused the items and found them to be the ordinary professional duties, the customary fees for which would have totalled to a large sum. The following is a letter received from Messrs. Davies and Co.: "re Mallinson."—As desired we enclose our bill from which you will see what work has been involved in this small matter. We have, however, made a nominal charge of £5 55." The Council authorised cheques for £l2 18s., purchase money £5 55., law costs and payments £1 ls. The CLERK continuing said: In the matter of pur- chase from Mr. J. W. Houghton which included a dwelling house ard'63s square yards of land the purchase money totalled to £147 12s. 6d. The bill of Mr. Ridgway, Mrs. Houghton's solicitor, amounted to £7 9s. and 15s. payments. Mr. Ridgway had, however, under the circumstances voluntarily reduced his claim to £5 ss. and 15s. payments. Cheques were sanctioned by the Council for those sums.</p>
        <p>''</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, December 4, 1909 THE WEEK'S HOBBY.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 4, 1909 THE WEEK'S HOBBY.</p>
        <p>A LETTER RACK.</p>
        <p>This letter rack is very simple in construc- tion, and may be made of almost any kind of wood, but either teak or mahogany is the best, being capable of taking a good polish, and having a nice appearance when finished. Get two pieces of wood each twelve inches long, three inches wide, quarter of an inch thick. One of these must be cut into three pieces four inches long to form the shelves of the rack, the edges being bevelled or cut te</p>
        <p>an angle to screw on to the other piece at an angle of about fifty degrees, as shown in Fig. 2. A hole must next be bored in the piece forming the back board to suspend the .rack. _ _</p>
        <p>Fig. 1 :s a front elevation of the rack when fitted together, all the necessary dimen- sions being shown in these two figures. Fig. 3 is a sketch of the finished rack, but it may be further ornamented by cutting a fretwork pattern in the shelves. A coat of French polish or varnish will now be needed to complete it.</p>
        <p>* * *</p>
        <p>A NEAT BOOKCASE.</p>
        <p>The following is a simple and effective way of making a neat and handy bookcase: Materials required are: Two pieces of wood, 16in. by 7Sin. by Sin. for, shelves</p>
        <p>(Fig. I); two pieces of wood, 18in. by Sin. by fin. for sides (Fig 2) ; one piece of wood 16in. by 10in, by for back. Cut the wood to the shapes indicated. Shelves, about in. deep, must be let in at the</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>4.••. -</p>
        <p>FIG. 2</p>
        <p>sides. The fixing will be kind quite easy; and wedges (Fig 3) will be required to keep the sides in position. Glass doors may be made for a front, and the wood may be polished or painted.</p>
