      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>LEGAL FIRST AID.</head>
        <p>LEGAL FIRST AID.</p>
        <p>THE PUBLIC TRUSTEE.</p>
        <p>By an Act passed in 1906, which came into operation last year, a new departure has been made by the appointment of a Public Trustee. The officer created by the statute is a corporation sole—that is, he, as an officer, exists quite independently of his per- sonality; and in speaking or writing of him you would not describe Min as Mr. Stewart, but as "the Public Trustee"; just as, officially, you do not speak of Mr. Winning- ton Ingram, but of the Bishop of London. Of course, the Public Trustee is head of a department, with clerks and offices; and, probably, a large quantity of red tape, quite new.</p>
        <p>MAYS AND MAY NOTS. This Public Trustee may be appointed by any member of the English public (he has nothing to do with Scotland or 'lreland) to be the trustee of a will, or of a settlement. He may also be appointed by the Court to be a judicial trustee; or he may be appointed trustee of a convict's property while that convict is in durance vile. He may be ap- pointed and may act either alone or jointly with other trustees. Before enlarging upon the Public Trustee's special functions, men- tion may be made of one or two things he may not do; and one or two trusts he may not accept. In the first place, he may not accept the position of trustee of a will, etc., if that would involve the carrying-on or management of a business; bwause, clearly, being a mere official of the superior clerk itype, a civil servant would not be equal to such a task. Nor can he be trustee of a deed for the benefit of creditors; nor must he accept the administration of an estate known by him to be insolvent. Purely charitable trusts are, too, outside his sphere of activity.</p>
        <p>* * *</p>
        <p>ABSOLUTELY SAFE.</p>
        <p>The Public Trustee may refuse any trust, apart from those mentioned in the last paragraph; but he must not refuse merely on the ground that the property is small. The real object in view by the promoters of the statute was to enable paterfamilias to leave his property in the hands of somebody absolutely safe. The Act, therefore, provides for the Public Trustee to be appointed trustee of a will or settlement in one of two capacities. He may be an ordinary trustee (re a custodian trustee. As an ordinary trustee, he is appointed, either solely, or jointly with 'a private trustee; and in just the same manner as any other. His business, then, is to carry out the trusts of the will or settlement according to the terms thereof.</p>
        <p>* * * A CUSTODIAN TRUSTEE</p>
        <p>As a custodian trustee his position is quite different, and here his services may be utilised very largely. He may be so ap- pointed either by the will (etc.) by which the trust is created, or he may afterwards be appointed by the Court, or by any person who has the power to appoint new trustees. And when he is so appointed he does not manage the trust estate, or do any of the ordinary .acts required from a trustee. His sole business is to take charge of the capital of the trust, and to see that it is neither frittered away nor stolen. All the stocks, mortgages, lands, money, and other pro- perty is transferred into his name. He takes possession a all deeds, certificates, scrip, and other documents representing the trust pro- perty, and all rents, dividends, interest, and other ineame is paid to him. He then pays it out to the person entitled; but he may; on a proper request being made, allow income to be paid into a banking account, or to the managing trustees.</p>
        <p>* * *</p>
        <p>HIS LIABILITY.</p>
        <p>It has been *said that the Public Trustee: was absolutely safe—a statement which re- quires just a modicum of qualification. He is liable just as a private trustee would be, except where the liability is one to which neither he nor his staff have contributed, nor could have averted by reasonable diligence. If, however, the Public Trustee. should be held liable for any loss of trust property, the persons entitled are pretty sure to get their money, because the British Funds • are to be liable to make it good. Unlike the private trustee, the Public Trustee is to be paid. If you think of using his services, write a line to him—" The Public Trustee, London," will find Jim—and he will send you his scale of fem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>TALKS ON HEALTH.</head>
        <p>TALKS ON HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Br A FAMILY DOCTOR.</p>
        <p>OVERDOING IT. am always lecturing people and telling them to think about their health, but some of my friends overstep the reasonable limit and think about their health too much. Every virtue becomes a .vice when it is overdone; thrift becomes miserliness, generosity be- comes extravagance, bravery foolhardiness, and so on. There are few objects more miserable and more deserving of pity than the valetudinarian—the man who can think of nothing but his health. No sooner is he awake than he rushes to the glass to look at his tongue; then, with an anxious mien, he feels his own pulse; he becomes worried because his nose tickles, and wants to know if it is a sign of cancer. He goes down to breakfast, and is in a fever of excitement lest the cook may have boiled the egg for ten seconds too long, and thus have ruined his digestion for all time. He pokes his nose out- side the door and imftediately goes upstairs to change his underclothes because it is colder than he thought it was. He is never happy unless he is making himself miserable. (No, I am not Irish). He loves bottles of medicine, and the cook makes a small fortune by selling the empty bottles; it does not much matter what the medicine is so long as it smells nasty, looks nasty, and tastes nasty. He goes from one doctor to another; they all tell him there is nothing the matter, and that if he will give up thinking about his health, lead an ordinary healthy life, taking as much fresh air and exercise as possible, he will soon be quite well.</p>
        <p>:o : - PATIENTS WHO NEED PATIENCE.</p>
        <p>Nothing could be more foolish and un- reasonable than to laugh at such patients; they need very careful and judicious hand- ling; they must be treated with kindness, firmness, and tact, and, above all, with patience. In some cases they must be re- moved from their surroundings, taken away from their work, and sent right away to some health resort. The worst patients, who have worried themselves into a nervous state and are really ill, must be put in bed and fed up, being placed under the care of an experienced nurse. Others can be influenced by good advice, and by exercising their own will- power can throw off their nervous feelings. In most cases the finest treatment is to send them away for a thorough change.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>WHERE THERE'S A WILL.</head>
        <p>WHERE THERE'S A WILL.</p>
        <p>Many people are ill because they have such weak wills; the will-power can be developed just -as much as the muscles of the arm can be developed. A blacksmith has a fine arm be- cause, he is always exercising it; the quill- driver at the desk has a weak arm because he never uses his arm for heavy work. If the q`uill-driver were asked to make a great effort with his arm he would break down under the 'task. Herein lies the importance of training children to govern themselves. The child who is taught to do things which it does not want to do is being trained for a future emer- .gency ; the child who is never instructed to perform uncongenial tasks, but to weakly , in- dulge its own laziness is being ruined for the struggle in life; its will-power is in the con- dition of the weak and flabby arm, and when it is called upon to make an effort it will break down. I get so many letters from patients Who obviously have nothing really the matter with them, but they have not the strength of mind to throw off their symptoms.</p>
        <p>: o : LAZY PATIENTS.</p>
        <p>It is rather unfair on the doctor when he is asked not only to prescribe but to carry out the treatment. Suppose a patient. were to come to me with dirty hands and ask me what is the best thing to do, I should tell him to go home and get some warm water and soap and a brush and make them clean for himself. But the patient. 'may be lazy and want me to do everything. He says,. "is there no medicine I can take that will make -my hands clean, without all the bother of getting water, soap and brush?" I can only reply that there is no such medicine. I have told him what to do, and if he chooses to negltet my advice his hands will have to re- main dirty, that's all. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>CHANGE OF THOUGHT.</head>
        <p>A CHANGE OF THOUGHT.</p>
        <p>If a patient comes .to me with some imagi- nary symptom, I can examine him and say that he is mistaken in thinking there is any- thing wrong with him, and instruct him to exercise his own will-polier and determina- tion and throw off his morbid ideas. I can- not use his will-power for him, he must use i# for himself—he must get the soap and water for himself. He wants me to give him medicine, but there is no medicine which will remove a symptom that never existed in real fact. Let him make up his mind to occupy his thoughts with other things, and the cure will be effected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>– MARVELLOUS CURE</head>
        <p>A - MARVELLOUS CURE</p>
        <p>It sometimes takes a tremendous stimulus to make a person use his or her will. A good example is the case of a young woman who thought she was paralysed; her doctor told her she was mistaken and that if only she would make up her mind to try and walk she would undoubtedly succeed. Well, nothing would persuade her to make the effort, and she lay in bed for months until one blessed day the house caught fire, and she was in danger of being burnt in her bed. This so frightened her that she jumped out of bed and ran downstairs. It needed something more than the poor old doctor's •remon- strances to stir her up.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>PUTTING ON WEIGHT.</head>
        <p>PUTTING ON WEIGHT.</p>
        <p>The great majority of cases of obesity can be cured by eating less; theibfew cases need special care and advice. Suppose a man eats one ounce a day more than is necessary; that ounce of food is not thrown out of the body, but is stored up in the form of fat or adipose tissue. Now, it my arithmetic is correct, one ounce a day mounts up to sixteen ounces, or one pound, in sixteen days, or call it a , fort- night. In fourteen fortnights, or seven months, the amount is one stone.. Now, one ounce a day too much is riot perceptible; the man will stoutly deny that he eats too muck, and his statement is almost true, but not quite; you .see, that little ounee mounts up to one stone in seven months. Our friend weighs himself in January, and notes his weight as ten stonc ; in July, when he is on his summer holiday, he finds his weight ' eleven stone, and 'mother had to put a piece in the back of his waistcoat.</p>
        <p>--....- ; 0 ; -.--.-.</p>
        <p>EAT LESS OR WALE MORE</p>
        <p>There are two methods whereby the weight may be.. reduced in a case of this sort. The first method is by knocking off the extra ounce of food. Do not take a second helping to pudding. The other method is by taking aa little more exercise, so that the extra ounce is not stored up in the body as fat, but is used up in performing some muscular exer- cise. The ways of taking exercise are legien ; I will only mention one here. If you are ' bottled up in an office and do not get much chance of daily exercise, get out of the train one station, before your nearest station and walk home every , fine evening. Of course, stations are not always so conveniently situ- ated for this to be suitable, but it is often a good plan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>SOME PRESCRIPTIONS.</head>
        <p>SOME PRESCRIPTIONS.</p>
        <p>A few useful prescriptions to keep by you: For Chilblains.—Resorcin, five grains; tannic acid, five grains; ichthyol, ten grains; vaselinc, 0110 ounce.</p>
        <p>For Chilblains When They Are Broken.— Ammoniated mercury, five grains; ichthyol, ten grains; starch powder, two teaspoonfuls, rine oxide, two teaspoonfuls; vaseline, half in ounce.</p>
        <p>A Shampoo Wash.—Soft green soap, two ounces; rectified spirit, two ounces; solution If ammonia, half an ounce; eau-de-Cologne io make up eight ounces. A. Gargle.—Potassium permanganate, five .cins, water, ten ounces.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>UNITED MISSION.</head>
        <p>UNITED MISSION.</p>
        <p>MR. GEORGE CLARKE ON ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Mr. George Clarke commenced his second week's mission on Sunday, when three successful meetings were held. In the afternoon he delivered to men only a lecture entitled "The Christian Athlete." Mr. James Dodds presided over a crowded audience, composed for the most part of athletes connected with the various clnbs in Warrington and district. The lecture was extremely interesting, with many humorous touches, and was much appreciated by all his hearers.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said at it was well over 30 years since he first came to Warrington. Duribg that time he bad been closely identified with the industrial pursuits and industrial life of the town. During all the time he had been the bon. treasurer of a sick and provident society in connection with the largest works in the town and had taken a deep interest in the struggles, hopes, fears, joys and sorrows of the toilers. (Hear, bear). Last but not least his sympathies had always been with, and he had tried to support as well as he could, the manly, healthy and innocent games in which the youth and manhood of Warrington bad , engaged. (Nur, bear.) He had never found anywhere such a love of fair play and justice, and such a determination to stand by the right and true as he had found in the workingmen of Warrington. As a class he did not hesitate to say that he would rather trust the workingmen of Warrington than any other class on any moral questions. (Applause.) They bad their religious and political differences, but when a great moral issue had been put before them they had risen above their religious and party proclivities and social preference and had stood loyally by the right. And then they had endeared themselves to him in a way and extent that he should never forget by their fairplay, and be therefore knew that they would listen with atten- tion and sympathy to Mr. Clarke while be sought to impress upon them that there sere higher ideals and to awaken in them the highest and noblest conception of their destiny. (Applause.) Mr. CLARKE began his lecture by proving that athletes could be Christians ana Christians could be athletes. He said that some years ago when he was in a mission conducted by Mr. Moody the Christians were asked to go and have a prayer meeting in another room so as to make way for others who could not get in. Eleven, including the speaker, went and alter the big meeting a steward came into the room in which they held the prayer meeting and said "1 geess that you chaps could beat anybody at. Cricket." "Aye," was the reply. It happened that nine out of the eleven had been members out of the eleven of the English team who went out and played against Australia. He was one of the two who had not been out, but he had played for his county. The other man was an excellent wicket keeper and would have I een chosent only the man who want was a better man than be. One of the men was Charlie Studd, who became a missionary in China. (Applause). Continuing, Mr. Clarke said that at the moment that he was conducting that service, Tom Hayward, the Surrey cricketer, was conducting a Bible class in London. The speaker referring to his own powers at cricket said that on his 52nd birthday he made 100 runs. He also took a great interest in boxing and if there were a fellow there about his own age who would like to have three rounds he would take him on. (Laughter). He said that the day when the football and running fields became a haunt and a custom for betting and gambling, Christian men would have to stay away and then it would be a mighty bad day for the sons and daughters of our beloved land. Therefore, whenever he got a chance to say to athletic men " Try to keep your football clean," he did so. The leaders of the clubs and the committee men were trying to do it, but it was the rank and file that it depended upon. The men of our land would have a great deal more sympathy with preachers if, instead ef holding up their bands and tnruing up their eyes and using some sort of skrieking sentence about the wrong of doing that, they woula mix with them in their games. He was sure they would have a greater love for them and a greeter faith in them. He would even go so far as to say that if the minister played cricket or golf they would preach a better sermon on the Sunday.(Laughter).Exactly the same train- ing that he had to undergo as a runner had to be practised in the Christian life. When a man was converted—and he hoped lots of them would be in that mission, as it would make men of them—God did not only forgive him the past but He put into him a new life and ikew nature. It took exactly ihe same kind of training to make them strong manly Christians as to make them athletes. Continumg,he said that they must excuse him, but that day behad to speak about himself. An athlete was a man who made up his mind that be was going to be the best man physically be could be. If there was a fellow he could not stand it was be who played for his team and then did not get his body into as good a condition as possible so that he could do all be could for the success of the team. He liked whole- heartedness. A man in the same way could never become a Christian worthy of the name unless he became a Christian whole heartedly. What made him_ (the speaker) an athlete? When be was young he went out to America with the idea of making a fortune. He did make money there, but it was not every Englishman that could say he had robbed the Yankees. (Laughter). Continuing he aid that while there he joined the Manhattan Running Club and had the proud right to wear the club colours. At this point in his address he alluded to Y.M.C.A.'s and said that be wanted them to have one in Warrington as it was a disgrace to a town of this size to be without such an institution. He also had the advice of the club trainer, the club and the use of the club grounds. What made a man a Christian? No doubt many of them would say that afternoon" I ought to be a Christie n," and then they. would say" I must be a Christian." Perhaps in that very ball they would give themselves to Chriet and then they would go out of the ball with their heads up and their shoulders back and with the privileges of a Christian. That day he would like them all to become" Christian athletes and then they would have four privileges, viz.: 1, the proud right to wear the club colours; 2, Christian companionship; 3, advice of the trainers—the ministers; 4, the Club House—the Church. Directly be became a member of the Manhattan Club he tried to be the best he could. Charlie his trainer did his best for him when he raw that he (the speaker) was doing the best he could. The first thing he had to be particular about was the things that be wore when he ran. He had seen a great many people start the Christian race well, giving promises of running a splendid race, but but because they would not take care of the things they wore, such as intemperance they failed. He bad seen men start a race with top bats on, or to apply it to the Christian race, "with pride of intellectuality"—fellows that said they had intellectual difficulties about the Christian faith. The intellects, like the top hats, were empty. Helalso mentioned about men starting a. race with their breeches" on and he applied that to those who bad got a lot of money and would not use it for the Kingdom of God. It was not easy to run the Christian race and they had got to train themselves. Charlie, his trainer was particular about the food he ate The diet he recommended was as follows: breakfast, underdone beef steak, or mutton chop, dinner and tea the same and the supper, to vary it, mutton chop or underdone beef steak. (Laughter.) When a man was in training he did not want anything but his under- done beef steak. They as athletes knew when a man had got, stale—when he did not fuel hungry after eating l lbs. of beef steak. (Laughter.) Put it the other way and if they were going to take care of their spiritual life they must feed upon the Bible which was —he hoped he said it reverently—their beefsteak. (Applause.) The mutton chops were good books. Charlie was very particular about the atmosphere that he breathed. Ask an athlete to go to a theatre, music ball or billiard room and he would say "No thank you; lam in hard training." A man had nothing to be more careful about than the atmosphere in which he moved. He knew preachers who told them it was wrong to go to theatres and musks balls. That was the wrong way to put it. Christianity was no policeman who marched at the heels of a man and said: "You shall not go to this place, or you shall not go to that." A Christian man saic : "No thank you, not in my line. I do not care for it. I like to walk upon the mountain tops. The atmosphere of those places is bad for me." The speaker said he never said it was wrong to go to theatres or music halls, he always raid to young Chrislians " If you give yourselves" to Christ you won't want to go to these places." (Applause.) When he was converted Christ took away the desire for thore things and now be walked on the hilly. Charlie wag very particular that be §liould not take any rotice of what people said. When he was Ifinniug some said" Take it easy" and others "Shove it in, If he had listened to what they said he would never have ran at all. Men were bigger cowards than women, because if it were not for the fact that somebody would see them, many would take sides with Christ, Women were not afraid: they did not care who saw them. If they were going to be first class men, they should not mind what anybody said. They should find out from Jesus Christ bow to run and God helping them they would win. Charlie was very particular about getting a start. There was nothing in the wide world in the Christian life more important than learning to start well. When he was converted 31 years ago Christ gave him the power to start and he did. Mr. Moody asked him if he would serve him and he (the speaker) answered "1 would be a cad if I didnt." (Applause). If they would come to Christ it wonld be the best day's work they ever did. He made money in America and lost it ; he got Jesus Christ for his personal Saviour in America and he had never lost Him. (Applause). Continuing he said that every athletic field had its offices and there was on the notice board : "The , right to refuse entries is reserved." He most have seen more than a hundred thousand people come to Christ and had never seen any refused yet. When a race was to be run there was always a notice that" Entries close on so and so," birt they could enter the Christian race where they sat. Mr. Clarke concluded with an elaborate description , of his last big race. He said he did not know ' anything like a race for a keen sense of enjoyment. The big race took place in the Borough grounds New York City and was witnessed by 30,000 people. Several clubs of America competed for the championship flag, and the Manhattan Club entered. He had never been more fit ie his whole life for a race and , as his club and another were on a level in points every- thing depended on him winning. It was a mile race A</p>
        <p>and he was only ehalfer" and a "quarter" but they had a better' quartee"so he had to run the mile? He only won the race by .throwing himself down on the tape and for a little time he did not know where he was, a minute or two after a lady pinned a medal on his breast and he knew he had won. Mr. Clarke con- cluded his interesting lecture by drawing the parallel of the Christian life and told hisaudience that Christ would pin the medal on them when they won. (Applause).</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening the hall was packed, people even being in the corridors and several were converted. The weather has militated against the attendance on the week nights, but still, the services have been successful.</p>
        <p>, To-morrow services will be held at 9 a m., 3 p.m. (when Mr. Clarke will deliver a lecture on the "Lord's Supper"---what it means and what it teaches) end at 8 p.m. Also during next week from Monday to Friday at 7.30 p.m.</p>
        <p>Fold-street Wesleyan Band of Hope. A meeting of the above was held on Wednesday even- ing and was conducted by Mr. Firth, tbe police court missioner. He gave a bright and interesting address to the children telling them that character was their best capital and if they wanted to build trustworty characters the best foundation was to be total abstainers. He warned them that "fires begin with small sparks" and they must light with all their might against the little sparks of temptation lest they become a grEat flame that could not he put out. "Strong drink," he said was one of the devil's baits to lure and entice people away from God to death. They must always resist this bait and never touch alcohol. He asked the children not to be stiff and starchy but to make theaaelves useful in the Band of Hope. Miss Edith Ball gave a recitation 6` Waiting for tea" and Miss Eva Jagger sang a solo. Mr. John White was the accompanist.</p>
        <p>Rai Thomas Miller on Survival after Death,</p>
        <p>The Rev. Thomas Miller preached to a good congre- gation on Sunday evening in Bold-street Church. He took as his text "if a man die shall he live again?" Job xiv, 14.)He said that there was little doubt that the patriarch believed in personal immortality. The great- tst hope came to him from the contemplation of the reality of spiritual fellowship, of the inconceivability of death destroying the links which bound the sold to God. His life with God gave Job his assurance. Immortality was the corollary of religion. From our nature, especially the distinctness between the material and spiritual parts, we might conceive the possibility of the survival of personality after death. Psychology would not identity brain with mind and thought. The grey matter might be necessary for thought for man as at present constituted, hut it did not follow that they were inseparable. if thought were not identified with the brain, then after the separation had taken place the thinking thing or being might be living and acting whilst the matter which formed the brain was rotting in the grave. We had made the mistake which Socrates condemned of identifying man with his body. "I believe" said Fiske "in the immortality of the soul not. in the sense which I accept the demonstrable views of science, but as a supreme act of faith in the reasonable- ness of God's work.' Concluding Mr. Miller said that a spiritual argument was provided by spiritual experi- ence. If our spiritual life were real then the fellowship we bad with god would surviNe death. It had little relation to the material world.</p>
