      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>WARRINGTON CHAPTERS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CHAPTERS.</p>
        <p>WILLIAM EYRES, THE ILLUSTRIOUS</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON PRINTER.</p>
        <p>The roll of Warrington worthies must always contain the name of William Eyres, and the place assigned to him is by common consent a high one. Yet there is perhaps no famous Warringtonian of whose personal life we have less knowledge. We know that he was born in Warrington early in 1734, and that he died here a century ago and lies buried in the Churchyard. For the rest we turn to his work, to the long series of volumes bearing the simple imprint: " Warrington : Printed by W. Eyres," which by their surpassing quality have con- ferred fame not only on the man and his press but upon the town in which he wrought. It is from the scanty information to be gleaned from the works he printed that we must draw our knowledge of the man and his successes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>AN EARLY WARRINGTON BOOKSELLER.</head>
        <p>AN EARLY WARRINGTON BOOKSELLER.</p>
        <p>William Eyres was not the first of the name to be associated with Warrington printing Or book selling. As early as 1704 a book was issued bearing the intimation "to</p>
        <p>and one can find in them in embryo "Everybody's Letter Writer," the "Ready Reckoner," the Farm and Nursery book and countless Almanacs of later years. They mark a period in history and give us a clue to the intelligence of the eighteenth century. And, coming to the subject of our article, they made a small fortune for William Eyres. They now figure as " rarities " in the book lists and when they come into the market command a respectable figure.</p>
      </div>
