      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>EYRES' WARRINGTON PRESS.</head>
        <p>EYRES' WARRINGTON PRESS.</p>
        <p>be sold by H. Eires, Bookseller in Warring- I ton." This Henry Eyres, or Eires, was the I grandfather of William. The tract bearingl the name was Peter Slynehead's "Spiritual wickedness in High Places," a bitter attack upon the Ecclesiastical Courts of Justice. Two others written by the same unpacified gentleman followed in 1706 and 1709,and both bear the same itnprint ".Sold by H. Eires." It is supposed that Henry Eyres was the local printer, and that two centuries ago he met such demands as the town made for printed matter. These demands would be very slight, and would hardly go beyond a small poster, a broadside, or effort of a local poet. Henry Eyres was suc- ceeded by John Eyres and to</p>
        <p>Slum OF him were born two sons _ WILLIAM EYRES Thomas and William. It Born 1734. Died 1809. was when these two sons grew to maturity that the name of the firm began to travel beyond the confines of the town. THE ADVENT OF THE CHAP BOOK. Possibly it was to. their, more enterprising brains that the countryside owed the exquisite enjoyment which came in the form of the Chap Book. Since only a small number of our readers have made the acquaintance of a real chap book we had better offer a word of explana- tion. The books were so called because they were sold by chapmen or hawkers. The sale of literature was a common means of liveli- hood before the days of the penny post and chapmen (" Travelling Stationers" was the more dignified title they applied to themselves) were fairly numerous. .The chap book was almost the only kind of book which reached the villages a century and a half ago; it was certainly the only kind of book they could afford, and the modest penny (though a penny was a bigger thing in those days) would bring from the pack of the pedlar either a history, a fiction, or a romance.</p>
        <p>The ancient building in which Eyres used to practise the printer's art. It stood on the east side of Horsemarket-street, somewhere between the shop of Mr. Prince, and Pig-hill.</p>
        <p>printer in Prescot and he disappears from our view. William remained in Warrington and a happy turn of circumstance gave him the opportunity of showing what he could do.</p>
        <p>The circumstance was the founding of the Warrington Academy and the coming of a company of learned and literary men to the town. The young printer met the occasion. He was twenty-five years old when his period of prosperity began and his hand presses and type were sheltered in a picturesque timbered building that stood in the centre of the town. Beginning with a sermon or two he secured the printing in 1760, of Dr. Priestley's "English Grammar" the first of a series of text books published by that fine scientist and scholar. These appear to have been the only books of any consequence issued until 1767. Then came the first great effort in which William Eyres showed himself to be a master of his craft. Priestley had completed his great work "The History of Electricity" and he entrusted it ,to "Eyre's Press." The volume ran to 744 pages quarto and is as a fine specimen of printing as the period can show.</p>
        <p>Three more years and Ere s Press was famous. Important works succeeded each other rapidly. Dr. Forster's " Travels "in three volumes appeared, and Dr. Aikni— destined to be Warrington's greatest book compiler—sent forth his first efforts. Early in 1773 Miss Anna Letitia Aikin took the literary world by storm by her quarto volume of poems—a volume printed as poems had never been printed before in magnificent quarto pages, and Dr. Enfield's "History of Liverpool" made a worthy successor during the same year. Next year came Enfield's " Speaker ' which held its place for nearly a century and ran through countless editions, and Thomas Pennant, the fine antiquary and scientist, brought the manuscript of his "Tour in Scotland," and afterwards his four- volume "Zoology."</p>
        <p>, ETTP.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>TYPICAL CHAP BOOKS.</head>
        <p>TYPICAL CHAP BOOKS.</p>
        <p>The chap; -book .was the ancestor of the penny dreadful (the truth had better be told), as it was of the novel, the book of ballads and "Old Moore's " Almanac. We have two specimens of Eyre's chap books in our possession and a description of them will serve to illustrate the fine variety of fare which the travelling booksellers had to offer. The first is" The history of Wat Tyler and Jack Straw." It has four shockingly bad wood-cut illustrations to adorn its twenty- four pages. Considering the temptations which the theme offered and considering that chap books were "made to sell," the book is a very sober bit of history and even in the closing murder scenes he does not unduly make the flesh creep. The second is " The Capitivities of Jerusalem lamented; or a description of Jerusalem from Joshua's time to this year of Christ." In a similarly small compass the writer compresses this vast history and manages to find room for five wood cuts and " Publius Lentulus's Epistle to Rome Con- cerning Christ," a bit of literature that would appeal mightily to the religious villagers of the day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>K ALEIDESCOPIC FARE.</head>
        <p>K ALEIDESCOPIC FARE.</p>
        <p>But religion and history were far from exhausting the scope of the chap books. There was the big field of romance and the possessor of a nimble penny could exchange it either for Guy of Warwick or the Seven Champions of Christendom. If his tastes rose higher he could have King Arthnr and the Round Table, or if his soul ' went out into the invisible" he eould have "Faustus and his Satanic Companion and large doses of the witch, fairy or hobgoblin. A glance through the titles of the col- lected chap books reminds one irresistibly of the Wax Work Exhibition of the days of onr youth in which the Chamber of Horrors loomed very large. Criminals and freaks, ghosts and monstrosities hold a big place, and of course the prophet—the prototype of Old Moore—has his place. The ancestor of the "Dream Book" was among them, but in addition to giving the sure clue to dreams it gave the unerring interpretation of every mole that adorned the human body. And let no one suppose that the heart strings remained unpulled. Those who failed to weep over the sorrows of the real wives of Henry VIII could bestow their tears upon imaginary maidens of every unhappy type. And if fiction did not go far enough there was always " The Murder of Maria of the Red Barn," which everybody knew was true in every detail, to wring the heart of tbe most callous and stir the deepest fountain of tears. We linger over the Chap Books for several reasons. They were the pioneers of the modern novel and handbook,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>SOME FAMOUS BOOKS.</head>
        <p>SOME FAMOUS BOOKS.</p>
        <p>The year 1777 saw John Howard's "State of Prisons" passing through the press and history was made thereby. Admirable as Howard's books are as specimens of typography they are excelled by the superb product, hailed by every critic as William Eyres masterpiece, "The Earls of Warren and Surrey." This book in two quarto volumes has been declared to rank among the finest and most finished specimens of the printer's art ever issued from the press. A mere list of the great works printed at Market Gate would weary the reader. To those who would know how skilful a crafts- man William Eyres was we give some counsel. Let them enquire at our admirable Reference Library for a sample of Eyre's best work. With the work before them let them remember till slow and difficult processes of a century and a half ago, remember that blocks and plates were cut by hand, that each sheet was placed separately under the press and its perfect and equal finish the result of a skilled arm and eye. Note the quiet dignity of the title-page, the easily read type, the perfect spacing and paging.</p>
        <p>- Except by the far-famed Foulis and Baskerville it is doubtful whether, he was even approached—it is certain he was not equalled—by any printer of his age. In 1803 in failing health and after fifty years of toil he sold his business to Mr. J. Haddock.</p>
        <p>Six years later William Eyres passed away. "Life's work well done" is a fitting epitaph, and his books are a legacy which his fellow townsmen are likely to prize more and more as the years roll by. A.M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>WARRINGTON RESIDENT WITHDRAWS NOTHING. " What I have said I have said."</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RESIDENT WITH- DRAWS NOTHING. " What I have said I have said."</p>
        <p>Many of our readers will call to mind the statement made some considerable time since by Mr. Price of Queen-street. Such interest has it awakened that we have commissioned a reporter to interview Mr. Price who withdrew nothing from his statement, but on the contrary made a most important addition. Mr. J. Price, of 46, Queen-street, Warrington, says : "Four years ago I had an awful attack of lumbago. I got up one morning with a terrible pain in my back, and I could scarcely dress myself. I was so bad at work that I had to go home. I had no use in the lower part of my body, my legs being powerless. The kidney secretions were stringy, and scalded. I had to get about with sticks. A neighbour advised me to try Doan's backache kidney pills, and after I bad taken a few doses I was able to do without the sticks. Very soon I was able to start work again. (Signed) Joseph Price."</p>
        <p>A year later Mr. Price said :—" Thero has been no sign of old trouble returning, and I have never lost a day's work since I told of my cure by Doan's pills." Doan's backache kidney pills are two shillings and ninepence per box, or six boxes for thirteen shillings and ninepence. Of all chemists and stores, or post free direct from the Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W. Be sure you get the same kind of pills as Mr. Price had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>v. 1111111111111111111111111111111111.</head>
        <p>v. 1111111111111111111111111111111111.</p>
        <p>Yr.." 211 WICLIN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
