      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>FIRST WARRINGTON NEWSPAPER.</head>
        <p>THE FIRST WARRINGTON NEWSPAPER.</p>
        <p>' While the Eyres brothers did not disdain to offer the humble fare of the chap books, they had ambitions of their own and one of them took shape in the launching of " Ey'res' Weekly Journal or Warrington Advertiser." The prominence given to the family name suggests that the Eyres had a "good con- ceit," and no Small belief in the name, but there were difficulties in the way of launch- ing and maintaining a newspaper in those days and neither the name nor the energy of the Eyres' could keep the" Advertiser" alive. There was the -paper tax, and there were - legal restrictions ; news was scarce and purchasers far from numerous. Born in March 1856 the paper only lived a few months and the brothers (then 24 and 21 years old respectively) had to face their first big disappointment.</p>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON ACADEMY.</p>
        <p>Soon after this failure Thomas Eyres appears to have established himself as a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>– FR-OM – LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</head>
        <p>- FR-OM - LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</p>
        <p>By Philip Snowden, rd.P.</p>
        <p>THE LABOUR PARTY CONFERENCE. The Annual Conference of the Labour Party has been fixed for January 19th, 20th and 21st, at Newport, Mon. Present indica- tions seem to point to an alteration of the date of the Conference. The General Elec-, tion will probably be in full swing on the dates mentioned, so the holding of the Conference would be at that time an impossi- bility. _ .</p>
        <p>Ai the time of the last General Election an alteration had to be made in the place and date of the Conference. But on this occasion there are circumstances connected with the Election which may cause both the date and the character of the Labour Conference to be changed. There appears to be such a strong and widespread feeling in the country in favour of some understanding by which the handing over of seats to supporters of the House of Lords, through a division of the anti-Lords' vote between two candidates, that the Labour Party will have to come to some decision on the matter. The Chairman of the Parliamentary Labour Party has announced that the question will be con- sidered at a special meeting of the Executive of the Labour Party this week. That meet- ing will be held about the time these lines appear in print.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>WITHIN THE CONSTITUTION.</head>
        <p>WITHIN THE CONSTITUTION.</p>
        <p>The Executive of the Labour Party can do nothing in such a matter except in accord- ance with the constitution of the Party. The constitution requires that the candidates and members of Parliament connected with the Labour Party, shall strictly abstain from identifying themselves with any other political party. Anything of the nature of a bargain or an arrangement with the Liberals by which Labour condidates would be withdrawn is clearly impossible under the constitution of the Party. Nothing of the sort has ever been done, and in ordinary circumstances such a thing would never be for one moment considered. The Executive of the Labour Party cannot make any such bargain. The Labour candidates are pro- moted by the Labour and Socialist bodies in the respective constituencies, and the final voice in any question of arrangement or withdrawal would rest with them.</p>
        <p>THE EXCEPTIONAL CIRCUMSTANCES.</p>
        <p>Only the most exceptional circumstances could justify the Executive of the Labour Party in taking any steps with a view of bringing about an understanding to avoid the regrettable result which sometimes comes from a three-cornered contest. But the most extreme stickler for rigid independence will admit that the circumstances of the coming election are exceptional and unprecedented. There will be only one issue before the electors ; and that will be whether the House of Lords or the electors are to govern the country. The importance of that issue to every democrat, nobody will deny and at a moment when the long-desired opportunity has come for striking an effective blow at the hereditary enemy of the people, it would be suicidal to allow that enemy to escape the punishment it deserves through a division of the forces which are eqnally anxious to curb its power.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>JANUARY CONFERENCE.</head>
        <p>THE JANUARY CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>The Executive of the Labour Party, as I have said, can take no definite action. But it can give the Party the opportunity to consider the present situation, and to decide upon what action it thinks it would be best to take in the circumstances. There area number of courses open. The Conference which was to have been• held in January might if possible be brought forward to a date anterior to the General Election—l should think there would be very great difficulties in the way of doing that. The financial year of the party does not close until the end of December, and amendments to the resolutions can be sent in until near Christmas. The ordinary Conference could not, I think, be brought forward into December. I do not think it is desirable that the ordinary Conference should be held before the General Election. That Conference deals with all manner of questions, including the internal business of the party. Clearly at a, time like this the delegates would be inter- ested in one subject only; and the country, too, would not have the least interest in the business of the Conference apart from the discussion of its election policy. So the alternative to bringing forward the ordinary Conference to a date before the election seems to be the holding of a special Con- ference solely for the discussion of election policy. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>SPECIAL CONFERENCE.</head>
        <p>A SPECIAL CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>This could no doubt be done, and it would give the party the opportunity of expressing its mind on the present political situation and the policy of the party in the circum- stances. It would be rather hasty work to get such a Conference called together before Christmas, for the machinery of such a big movement works rather slowly. But some- thing similar has been done before by other parties. On the Eve of the General Election of 1895 the Parliamentary Committee of the Trades Union Congress called a special Con- ference to discuss its election policy. The constitution of the I.L.P. provides that the party must hold such a special conference when Parliament is dissolved. So that there are no insuperable difficulties in the way of calling a special Labour Party Conference to discuss election policy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>PRACTICAL DIFFICULTIES.</head>
        <p>PRACTICAL DIFFICULTIES.</p>
        <p>The real difficulties will be those which the Conference will have to face. The election arrangements of the Labour Party have been made in the natural expectation that when- ever the next election_ came it would be of the usual character, and would be fought on the usual issues. Labour candidates have been brought out in something like 70 con- stituencies. A vast amount of work has been done, and much money has been spent, and it is hardly human nature to step down now on the eve of the fight. But there are occa- sions when discretion is the, better part of valour, and when it is wiser to run away to live to fight another day._</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>IN- OTHER CON STITUENCES.</head>
        <p>IN- OTHER CON STITUENCES.</p>
        <p>If the special Conference to which I have referred is held, it will not only have to con- sider the question of three-cornered fights, but it will have to take into consideration what the policy of the Labour Party must be in those constituencies where there will be no Labour candidate. That will be a much less difficult matter to settle. No Labour man certainly will dream of giving a vote to a supporter of the House of Lords; and in view of the importance of obtaining the largest possible vote against them, the disposal of the Labour vote will present no difficulties.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <p>There are many brands of Margarine but none of them are equal to "GOLDEN FLEECE" MARGARINE, which only costs Bd. per pound.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>WITHOUT PREJUDICE.</head>
        <p>WITHOUT PREJUDICE.</p>
        <p>If a Conference of the Labour Party should decide thatsit is desirable to come to some understanding for this election only on the question of tringular contests this would not in any way prejudice the future action or policy of the Labour Party. No arrange- ment would be thought of for a single moment which in any way interfered with the freedom of action of the Labour Party. The Labour Party could enter into no understanding except as the equal of the party with which it treated,and any decision would be but a temporary understanding, made in very exceptional circumstances, for the benefit of a cause the Labour Party was willing to help. The Labour Party, beyond all parties, cannot afford to let the forces of privilege, as represented by the House of Lords, triumph by its action. The Labour Party may not love the Liberals, but it likes the Peers a good deal less, and it would be the duty of the Labour Party to accept the assistance of any party, or any individuals, who were sincerely fighting against an undemocratic institution. THE STRENGTH OF INDEPENDENCE. There was a time when the very mention of anything but absolute isolation for the Labour Party would have been regarded as rank treason, but the Party is now in a position of such strength and security that it can afford in its own interests or in the interests of some national question, to make temporary terms without any risk of its independence being impaired. Isolation is the policy of weakness; independence, of strength. Nothing can be done or will be done which will interfere with the freedom of the Labour Party at any time to take its own course according to its own ideas. The Labour Party will settle this question and it will do it, I am sure, in a way which will be for the present and future advantage of the movement for the independent representa- tion of Labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>OUR MISCELLANY.</head>
        <p>OUR MISCELLANY.</p>
        <p>Our Summer Pilgrimages were received with most kindly interest by our readers and the comments made thereon were on the whole of a very flattering kind. There comes, however, a comment from a reader in Middlewich which is pitched in the minor key. Although, as he correctly surmises,we must have passed through Middlewich a number of times we have not even mentioned its point of crowning interest and chief glory —its "pubs." The inns of the town are at once its ornaments and landmarks. The dweller in the salt town would never think of naming a street—so we are assured—but instructs the stranger to keep on past the 'Bull' till he comes to the ' Bear ' and then turn down by the • Eagle.' "</p>
        <p>We plead -guilty to -flaying missed a point of interest, but Cheshire is full of such points, and not even another full summer will exhaust its stories of fact and lore. •</p>
        <p>** * *</p>
        <p>Curiously enough we have just discovered among our ancient sheets a poem written and printed in the days when Middlewich along with the rest of the country was living in dread of a French invasion. This elabo- rate recital of the Middlewich Inns must surely gratify our correspondent, as we hope the sentiment may.</p>
        <p>PATRIOTIC VERSES ON INN SIGNS AT MIDDLEWICH. When folks meet together dissension to sow, And by breeding divisions encourage the foe ; When false motives like colours they hold to our view; Its a sign they might find something better to do,</p>
        <p>If ever the French should attempt to come here To eat up our beef, and to drink our strong beer, Of both they'd fall short, but if fighting they wished, At each sign of Middlewich they would be dished.</p>
        <p>First the Lion called" Golden "would make them to quake,. And the" Talbot" I doubt not would give them a shake; At the sign of the " Wolf " would they venture to rap They'd find, though too late, they'd run into a trap.</p>
        <p>By our Bears White and Black they'd be put to the rout, And a thrashing they'd get at the " Wheat Sheaf " no doubt, From " Lord Hood" a broadside they'd meet, to their cost, And at the "Bull's Head" they'd be savagely tos't.</p>
        <p>At the" White and Red Lion "they'd find, to their shame, Whether Black, White, or Blue, British lions are game; At the " Bridge Foot " they'd stop, perhaps call for a " whet " ; And they'd get it—that is a good ducking they'd get.</p>
        <p>II they call at the " White Horse " they'll treat them so laud, With a horse shoe, that more kicks than halfpence they'll find ; Should they venturo to peep at the " George and Dragon" They'd see to their cost, they'd nothing to brag on.</p>
        <p>Next at the "Seven Stars," they'd soon show them the door, At the " Oak " a good drubbing they'd get and no</p>
        <p>more; Should these "Sans-culottes" dare with our " Crown" interpose, They'd prick their French fingers well under the "Roe."</p>
        <p>A t the "Nag's Head" with bites and cuffs they would be treated. At the "Ring o' Bells" next with an empty house greeted ; The sign of the " Eagle" would raise fresh alarms,. And they'd run like soup maigre to es-cape the "King's Arms."</p>
        <p>May the sign of the " King " ever meet with respect, And our good constitution each Briton protect; May he who ,prst caused all the troubles of France Be high hung on a sign, on nothing to dance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>FIERCE SKIN ERUPTION.</head>
        <p>FIERCE SKIN ERUPTION.</p>
        <p>" I was affected on my legs and lower part of the body, also between my fingers, with a very bad itching. When I was in bed I felt as though I could tear myself to pieces, also when at work. I was always losing sleep through it. I tried some blood medicines from the chemist, and also two doctors and a herbalist but to no effect, Then I read of the Cuticura Remedies, and so I thought I would give them a trial. I am pleased to say that one set of the Cuticura Remedies made me as well as I ever was, and I can recommend the Cuticura treatment as the one that cured me. I must mention that the spots on my body and legs were red and full of matter and the pain was awful for a full three months before I used the Cuticura Remedies. Thomas Burns, 11, St. Aidans Terrace, Deaf Hill, Trimdon Colliery, Durham, England, December 14, 1908."</p>
        <p>TREATING THE HORSE AS A GIRAFFE.—AIan seems to have largely exercised his ingenity in reversing the conditions under which the horse would live in a state of Nature. We compel him to stand in a narrow stall, with his head up-hill, to his manifest discomfort, and the detriment of his feet and legs, when, if consulted, says " Farm and Home," he would reverse the position entirely, and in many other ways we do violence to his natural instincts. Being naturally a ground feeder, and especially provided with an elastic arrangement to enable him to obtain his food easily in this position,we treat him as though built like a giraffe.</p>
        <p>For Cooking or Pastry "GOLDEN FLEECE" MA RG A RINE is delightful. Quite RS good Its Butter, in fact.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>OUR SERIAL STORY.</head>
        <p>OUR SERIAL STORY.</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.]</p>
        <p>‘-v%#.-1•i</p>
        <p>replied the other, sinking back again into his former position. "There seems to be a sort of Fate working for us—or against us— brother. By the look of thie place, I take it that we're not likely to be found—for a time at least; aro when we are found, it will be a toss-up wructi or us is declared to be John Baxendale and which of us that arch-rogue, Rufus Cridge. Also, by the fact that I'm here at all, after a very deuce of a night, wandering about, scorched and torn and half dead, I take it that I am in your debt to that extent. Well—brother--I thank you." "You've nothing to thank me for," replied John Baxendale, curtly. "It looks as though this were the end of things, for one or both of us; it's a question of the sins of the fathers, you know. My home is gone; my name is a by-word among men; I am branded wrongfully for something that you did—" "All very true," broke in the other, carq- lessly; "but you must remember that you't had your safe and easy twenty-five years. It couldn't go on for ever. Nor, if it comes to that, can I be expected to take upon myself the onus of a crime for which you're accused —even if the accusation is a blunder. How- ever, don't let's talk about it now. We're both in the same boat, in a way, and there's no reason why we should fly at each other's throats. What do you want to do?" "I haven't made up my mind," said John Baxendale. "Heaven knows, Rufus, I _ .</p>
        <p>treated you fairly and squarely so soon as I knew the truth; I was willing to share every- '4;ug with you at once." Yes—on conditions-and the conditions did not suit me," retorted the other. "I'll own it was a mistake to kill the girl; but she's made you the scapegoat. That wasn't my doing. It's a 'bit of- a tangle, and I don't quite see how we're going to get out of it. But, I warn you, they shan't hang me if by good luck I can put the rope round your neck." "That's honest, at all events," replied Baxendale. It seemed, indeed, as though the two men kept guard over each other during that long day, each fearing to let the other out of his sight. Now John Baxendale would find him- self dropping to sleep from sheer weariness after the excitement and the exhaustion of the night and- the day through •which he had passed, and would sta,rt awake to find his brother watching hiM. And now Rufus Cridge would fall asleep, and then would wake with a start, to find Baxendale gloomily pacing the floor of the hut, as though striving to make up his mind what to do. But the cunning of Cridge finally triumphed; for at last Baxendale, utterly exhausted, made up his mind that he must sleep in good earnest, and, with cetentation, he sat down on the- floor of the hut, with his back against the door, so that the other man could not pass out. And then he slept, assuring himself that ',a would wake at the slightest sound. But Rufus Cridge knew better. Darkness was falling when, quite noiselessly; Cridge opened the narrow window of the hut and looked out. Not a sound anywhere, save of a bird stirring in the bushes, or cheeping sleepily; only the stars and moon above him. The man with his back against the door slept heavily, his arms fallen to his sides; and Cridge drew himself through the window and dropped outside, and so was free. He looked back at ,the man sitting in the shadows of the hut, closed the window silently, and stole away. _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>MS FATHER'S SON;</head>
        <p>MS FATHER'S SON;</p>
        <p>BY TOM GALLON</p>
        <p>Author of " Tatterley," "Dicky Monti,," "Kiddy," "A Rogue n Love," "Fate's )3t3g. gar Maid," " The Charity Ghost," &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>CHAPTER IX.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER IX.</p>
        <p>Baxendale Hall blazed high ' that night, while the mob surged about it, clamouring more and more feebly for John Baxendale, and half afraid—simple country folk that they uere—to see the ruin before them, though it was not of their making. As the dawn came up in the sky, after some small attempt had been made to subdue the flames, "be people crept away to their homes, look- ing back at the smoking, blackened ruin that once had been Baxendale Hall. Some talked of the strange happenings of thexi days, and of how curiously John-Baxendale had changed in a moment from what they had known; others pointed to the wood where the body of the dead girl had lain, aid whis- pered of madness; remembered the wild, shrieking figure they had seen in the lurid light of the flames, and thanked Heaven, piously enough, that John Baxendale wae done for. Far better to pethh like that than to live to face the consequences of what he had done.</p>
        <p>Officialdom, as known in Deepington, had meanwhile taken charge of the business, and had removed the body of poor Bella Ogden to the little hotel in the town. More than that, a guard of one rustic policeman had been set over the tree; and wires had flashed back and forth to London, summoning aid. The newspapers would be full of the matter on the morrow; the name of Baxendale would ring from one end of the country to the other.</p>
        <p>But to-night the real John Baxendale, staggering away from the house through the darkness that was fitfully illumined by the blazing building, and hearing the shouts and cries of the people who surrounded the place, and who seemed to clamour most of all for even in the midst of shouts from the more sober-minded that the fire should be subdued, and the valuable things in Baxen- dale Hall saved, remembered only that he was a fugitive, and that another man, in his likeness, and supposed to be John Baxendale, had doubtless perished in his stead. In a sense, that lifted from him the onus of the business. John Baxendale, branded a mur- derer, would be supposed to be dead, , and so to have atoned for his crime. The further thought that it left this real John Baxendale homeless and nameless did not occur to the man at that time. He had escaped; there was time at least for him to think.</p>
        <p>He waited about a long time until after the people had cleared away, and until he could see the stark naked walls of Nihat had been his home standing up against the morn- ing sky. In a sense, the despair that had entered into his heart at that time brought mith it a new courage. So much had been lost, and so much of doubt and difficulty and danger faced him that he could sweep aside all minor considerations, and could face what had to be faced as a straight issue. And nick of all at ti..tt time he decided that he must take the matter into his own hands, and *fight this battle of life or death himself. . . .</p>
        <p>He -would be looked for at the cottage; clearly he must not go there; rior must he go to any usual haunts where he was known; until it could be discovered whether or not this other man had perished in the flames or was alive to carry on the work to which he had set his hand. John Baxendale must set away, and must watch and wait, depending only on himself and his own strength to prove his innocence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <p>With that determination he set off to walk by a circuitous route through the grounds to a place at which he could strike some dis- tance beyond Deepington before the day- light should fully have come. Walking like that, with his mind full of a new resolve, he beard quite near to him something moving in the bushes; and he stopped, fearing that someone might have dogged his footsteps and be waiting to seize him. And as he looked, the bushes were parted by a hand, and some- thing crawled out and caught at his clothes and rolled over at his feet. It was Rufus Cridge—and, yet, as it seemed, not Rufus Crielge at all.</p>
        <p>Fo-r this thing was blackened and scarred and burnt, and it moaned pitifully as it clutched at John Baxendale with scorched hands. Baxendale suddenly found himself kneeling beside the man and speaking his name, and striving to get some sense into him. But Rufus seemed utterly worn out, and could only gasp and point to his lips. With a gentleness he did not feel, Baxendale laid the man down on the grass at his feet and ran to look for water. He filled his cap at a little stream, and came back and mois- tened the man's lips and dashed some of the liquid into his face. The man got to his knees and swayed about for a moment and strove to laugh, and then rolled over in a dead faint. John Baxendale knelt there beside him in the cool summer morning and wondered what he should do. The man was absolutely at his mercy; and yet, even if at that moment he- could have given him up to justice, he would scarcely have furthered his own cause. For this was Rufus Cridge, and the man who knelt beside him was John Baxendale. Softie other way must be found; above all, the man could not be left to die here like a dog in a ditch.</p>
        <p>So it came at last to .the one brother pick- ing up the other, and so staggering with his burden in the direction of a secure hiding- place he knew at the furthest extremity of the grounds. This was an old ruined keeper's hut, long since abandoned, and partly over- grown with a tangle of weeds and brush- wood, and never likely to be visited by any- one now. John Baxendale staggered with his burden in at the door of the place and 'aid the man down. He began to see that with the water he had given him and the vs arm contact of his own body in carrying him the man was slowly getting some colour into his face.</p>
        <p>He began to understand that in some fash- ion Rufus Cridge had contrived to escape from the burning house, but had not been able to get further away than this. He began to wonder what he could do with the man; saw now, on calmer reflection, that to at- tempt to give him up—even had not humanity forbade it—he must draw attention to him- self as that John Baxendale for whom every- one would be searching, and must also point clearly to the likeness between himself and his brother. Yet he could not leave the man :there—to die of exhaustion or starvation— and he was equally powerless to take him elsewhere. He leant against the wall of the hut, looking at the man, and striving to solve this new problem that had been presented to him.</p>
        <p>The day came 430, and grew to high noon, and still Rufus Cridge lay there in a deep sleep, so getting back his strength. It was well on into the afternoon when he awoke and stretched himself and looked at his brother seated on the floor of the hut near him, and watching him. So fbr a minute or two the men so strangely brought together looked into each other's faces, as they had looked before.</p>
        <p>" Hullo !" exclaimed Cridge, at last, with a grin; "what's happened to us?" Oh !—you've managed to escape with your life out of the place you set fire to," replied John Baxendale. We met last night, you know, while the mob outside clamoured for your life—" "I beg your pardon—it was for your life," said the other, raising himself on one elbow. "It was Baxendale they wanted; I remember that, at least. Baxendale who killed the girl—Baxendale who set fire to the house and shrieked defianoe at the mob from the win- dows • Baxendale—always Baxendale!" •!4e won't discuss that now; we've some- thing else to talk of," replied John Baxen- dale. "Do you realise that you're in my bands—and in my power?" "Oh. yes—if you care trk Da it that, way."</p>
        <p>mow</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Unwimolli-m</head>
        <p>Unwimolli-m</p>
        <p>Ire was wise enough to know that the very means adopted for. his safety by Johanna Cridge might make for his undoing if he went to any of his Old haunts; that that story of the double life led by John Baxendale might point straight to a certain Rufus Cridge al- ready known to the law. For the present he would remain in hiding, relying on his own cunning to enable him, as he had boasted, to put a noose about the neck of his brother. So, for the moment, he disappears into that greater world beyond Deepington and the ruins of Baxendale Hall. John Baxendale awoke slowly, to find him- self in utter darkness, cramped, and stiff, end chilled. He struggled to his feet and strove to remember where he was; saw the faint light of the moon stealing in through the nar- row windows, and recognised his surround- ings. He groped his way to the corner of the hut, where Rufus Cridge had lain, stooped down to touch him, and found nothing. Then he called to the man sharply, and finally ran to the door and flung it open, letting in what light there was. And so found the men gone, and knew that all his work was to be done</p>
        <p>-He dare not remain there. He knew enough of Rufus Cridge to know that in some fashion or other he might contrive to betray thie brother of his into the hands of the law. Even now there might be men on the look-out for him, waiting for that John Baxendale who must surely be wanted by this time. After a little hesitation he crept out of the hut, and looked about him, striving to get his wits to- gether, and to realise how long he had been there, and what had happened since the burn- ing of the house. It was absolutely necessary that he should get away; he must not be taken unawares. The cool night air revived him, and he felt refreshed after his sleep. He presently set off at a great rate to find his way to free- dom. And most it seemed at that time that freedom spelt London; that there, in some fashion, he might hide until the false John Baxendale should be laid by the heels, or un- til something should have happened to eet the real one free. Learning a new caution !rom the dangers that seemed to beset him on every side, he avoided railway stations, for the present, at least, and set off in the direc- tion in which he knew London to be.</p>
        <p>He argued to himself as he went that the news of the crime would not have reached provincial places 60 rapidly as it would have reached London itself. There wt‘s in his mind an idea that he would adopt some disguise, until he could get into some place where he could lie hidden. But he found that that was not, after all, absolutely neeessary. He wore a rough tweed suit and a cap, and he knew that by the time he had covered a few miles of his journey the dust of the roads would have gone far to soil them. One change only he made. At a little town through which he passed he bought a rough muffler for his throat, and discarded at the earliest oppor- tunity the convontional collar and tie lie wore. So that when at last, having travelled by devious ways to reach London, he stepped into its streets, he looked but poorly clad, and would escape notice anywhere. Ile kept away from any place where he was likely to be known or recognised. He engaged a small room in a fifth-rate hotel, where it was necessary, having in mind the fact that he possessed no luggage, to pay the small rent in advance. But such men are known and expected in such quarters in London, and so that matter presented no difficulty. John Baxendale had dlsappeared, and was no more than a 'shabby man among many shabby men in the streets of London.</p>
        <p>But, strangely and awfully enough, those streets grew alive with his name. First. in a dreadful way, newspaper placards spoke of the murder of a young girl in a wood ; the newspapers themselves told the story of that dreadful writing on the tree. After that a note of interrogation in the flaring head- lines, "Where is •John Baxendale? "—a ques- tion not to be answered by anyone just. then. John Baxendale, trudging the streets for a couple of days, and sleeping in his humble room a couple of nights, watched the growing excitement, and resolved to wait still longer to sec what would happen. In a 62116f.`, it was a curious feeling for a man to sit, as he did more than once, in a puhhie garden or park in the heart of London and to rend about himself. Of the name he bore, and the long generations of former Baxendales before him ; of the burning of his home, and what had been apparently his own strange madness on that night after the murder of the girl. And always again the quest ion, never answered with any satisfac- tion, " Where is John Baxendale? '</p>
        <p>On the third day after that pers4itent in- quiry had begun, Mr. Felix Goode at in his room at the top of a queer old house in "Iloomsbury, dining, as was usual with him, 4lone. Outside in the fi tree te , through the ?en windows (for the night was warm and cioec) be could hear the mica a the wws,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <p>paper boys, and could see, if he chanced to turn his head, those boys, each with his sheaf of papers, and with the flaring placard flapping in front bf him. And always of John Baxen- dale and of nothing else. It. seemed as if the world had stopped for John Baxendale. The old man sighed, and went on with his dinner, with no very great appetite, pouring himself out a glass of wine, and even shaking his head over that, though the wine, like him- self, was old and good. The old servant who waited upon him. coming: into the room on some ordinary matter, startled him with a sentence that was by no means ordinary. "A person of the name of Cridge to see you, sir." said the man. The old lawyer almost dropped his glass. He looked round at the man sharply, and 'then, seeing the servant's perfectly grave face' told him, with a curious little stirring of his pulses, to shew the person in. "You need not come yourself, simply shew him in," he said. Mr. Felix Goode, in some agitation, got up from the table, and stood to receive his visi- tor. He had heard nothing of Baxendale since the night of his disappearance, and yet, the name had been dinned into his ears all day long, and had been before his eyes when- ever he walked the streets. Who was this man, coming now with slow steps towards the door of the room, and what would he know of John Baxendale? The door was opened, and the man came in. He fingered his cap in his hands, and he looked round sharply as the door was closed; seemed a little grateful that the light was dim, and so shuffled into the room and nodded at the lawyer. "Well, Mr. Rufus Cridge," said the old man, "what brings you here?" The man glanced quickly at the door, and then moved a step or two towards where the old man stood. "Don't you know me?" he asked. Felix Goode came at him almost with a run, caught his hands, and stared into his face. "Baxendale—good heavens!" he whispered. "So I deceived even you, did I" said John Baxendale, with a hollow laugh. "I thought it best, to give that name, I was BO sure of being admitted then. I see food and wine on the table, may I help myself?" He sat down, and the old man eagerly helped him to the food. Outside the boys were still calling, and that same eternal ques- tion, answered now for the first time, was being asked. Inside, the two men looked into each other's eyes, and asked perhaps another one:</p>
        <p>"What was to become of John Baxen- dale? "</p>
        <p>"Now, tell me," said the old man at last,</p>
        <p>"why this name, and why this shabby ap- pearance ? You have good reason, doubtless. Doubtless, also, you've had a good reason for disappearing. I need hardly tell you that my place has been watched—the office, I mean— and I rather fancy I am suspected of knowing something of your whereabouts. Fortunately, they don't seem to know my private address.'"</p>
        <p>"Well, I saw that there was no , earthly chance for me if I allowed myself to be taken. I determined that they should, if pos- sible, find the other man; it could be decided afterwards which of us called himself John Baxendale. As they have not found him, ap- parently, I have made up my mind to take the matter into my own hands. I have a plan which I will not tell even to you for getting at the root of the matter."</p>
        <p>r</p>
        <p>my_ purpose to tIC tne 'latter.</p>
        <p>she demanded.</p>
        <p>"Because John Baxendale is badly wanted for a crime committed by Rufus Cndge, and Rufus Cridge might as well suffer his own just punishment. We'll adjust the iselleei bet- ter afterwards, whe» the rightful man has the handcuffs on his wrists. For the present, I'm Cridge—Cridge, who has lived here for years and whom everyone knows."</p>
