        <p>•A'lluoitto.ti -`pa-p-pu ens „‘aiippo aninu •JN—plasaad alp ao; aapti °utopia&amp; aa‘noA -sputni Jno Jo no si ?sum; ato laadxa •,Cum. aatmo atonal Januut Luup „ .7q2noti; pilau V Jour 'pm) oqs „`ogioais .zaiato u Sjt puu 4utut pieq V aa,nox ;he table. In the moet natural way, as it leered, she provided him with food, and then ;at fmlinly near him, watching him from time ;o time but saying nothing. Preeently, ac was to be expected, exhaustion overcame aim, and his drowsy eyelids closed, opened again to take in the quiet figure of the woman and the sordid room, and then closed again. The woman, never taking her eyes ;rout him for a moment, got up quietly an atolo across to the door, and opened it an went out.</p>
        <p>She crossed the landing to another dour, which she opened softly, and went in. Mr. Saul Tuttle was stretched in an ungraceful attitude upon a frowsy bed there, fully iressed, and snoring heavily. She woke him to consciousness with a vigorous cuff at tilt, tide of his head. He sat up, gurgling and muttering, and blinking at her. By dint of repeating the one statement over and over again she got him at last to understand what *lad happened. "John Baxendale has come here; he's in )ur hands at the present moment!" said the woman. "Now, do you think you could lay hands on Rufus to-night?" "I know w'ere 'e is, if that's wet you mean," said Mr. Tuttle. "Down at-" _</p>
        <p>She clapped a hand over his mouth. "1 ,lon't want to hear," she broke in. "When you have done what I want you to do, you must go and find Rufus, end send him to 30111 C other place—anywhere out of the way or the present. But before you do that, go ind fetch the constable on duty at the end of the Rents."</p>
        <p>"Not me," said Alf. Tuttle, turning pale. "I never speak to a copper meself, not unless 'e speaks to me first." "Don't be a fool," said the woman, roughly. "Go to him, and simply say tha? you know where John Baxendale is, theb bring him straight to me. Say that he'4. better bring another with him, in case Baxen- dale makes a fight of Mr. Tuttle at last very reluctantly con- snlted to go, and surprised Middleton's Rents by walking down it a minute or two later with two stalwart policemen following him. He turned into the house, and went up the stairs, and so brought those strange com- panions of his into Mother Cridge's room, where John Baxendale slept heavily, in his 2hair.</p>
        <p>"That man," said Mother Cridge, pointing to the sleeper, "is John Baxendale, wanted for the murder of a girl named Bella Ogden in a wood near Deepington." "Funny thing," said one constable, in a low voice, and with a glance at his fellow. "I always thought his name was Cridge." John Baxendale had started awake. As he sprang to his feet, looking from the men to Johanna Cridge and back again, he realised ia a dim fashion what had happened. He de- termined to play his trump card. "My name Is Cridge—Rufus Cridge," he said, quickly. "You know me, constable?" "His name's Baxendale, and he has been known here as Cridge on and off for years past," said the woman. "The dead girl knew him as John Baxendale; here he was kaown as Crick°. He has led a double life." That night cal London went mad over fresh placards and fresh editions of newspapers. And the placards read "John Bmendale found I His double life I " (To be continued.)</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 4, 1909.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <p>"You'll run your neck into danger," said the old man, with a doleful shake of the head. "Tell me what you have been doing since the night of the fire. I suppose you know that everything was destroyed?" "Yes, I know that. But that seems a small matter somehow. I found Rufus Cridge, half dead, as it seemed. I did what I could for him, and he slipped .away while I was asleep. Like myself, I suspect he has lien in hid- ing— " And lie is better at the game than you are, remember," broke in Felix Goode. "Think, my dear John—you may walk out into that street "—he pointed through the window as he spoke—"and have a hand clapped on your shoulder in a moment. The sharpest eyes in the world are looking for you everywhere." "And if it comes to that, I don't mean to hide very much longer," said Baxendale. "As I've told you, I mean to get to the heart of the matter within the next few hours. Watch your newspaper placards, for you shall see startling news. I mean to force the other man to come forward.."</p>
        <p>"You mean Rufus Cridge7" John Baxendale nodded. "I mean that he shall betray himself; and my plan is a very simple one. There, don't .sorry, old friend," he added, seeing the concern on the old man's face, "nothing bad can happen to me. I've walked these streets for a couple of days, and lain hidden at night in a poor lodging; but the end of all that is in sight. I know myself that no harm can come to me, be- cause at the worst we must tell the truth from the beginning." The lawyer nodded. the truth," he answered.</p>
        <p>"Yee—we "must tell</p>
        <p>They talked for a long time in the gather- ing dusk of the room, looking out on the busy world below that clamoured for John Baxen- dale. They talked of possibilities, of schemes by which Baxendale might get out of this tangle into which he had been drawn, and all the while Baxendale knew in his own mind what he had to do. When at the last he shook hands with Felix Goode, and 'prepared to start, he voiced something of his purpose. "In a very short time you shall hear from me, and you must be prepared to speak, if necessary. Have no fear for me; the purpose I have is as clear in my own mind as though it was already accomplished. Good-bye." The old man took him down to the door of the house, and so with reluctance shut him out into the street. Going back to his own rooms, Felix Goode shook his head many times over the rashness of youth, and wished that he could have found his way into John Baxendale's confidence.</p>
        <p>Meanwhile, John Baxendale seemed almost to take upon himself another character as he trudged on through the streets. His hands sank deeper into his pockets. A listless shuffle, quite unlike his ordinary alert walk, seemed to belong to his legs and feet, as he moved along; his cap came further over his eyes. So, long after the lamps had been lighted in the streets, and while people in the poorer quarters still sat at their doors to catch a breath of air, John Baxendale shuffled into Middleton's Rents, and into the doorway of a certain house, and climbed the stairs. Coming to a door he remembered to have knocked at before on a certain memor- able occasion, he opened it now without cere- mony. and lurched in, nor did he take the trouble even to remove his cap. Johanna Cridge was standing at the win- dow of the room, looking out over the squalid streets below her, with her hands clasped be- hind her back. She turned her head quickly as Baxendale lurched into the room, scanned him for a moment, and then spoke. "So you've come back, Rufus?" she said, in a low tone. "What's in the wind now, and what wind drives you here?" _</p>
        <p>Watching her, the man crossed the room and sit down near the table, spread his arms upon it, and looked at her sullenly. She had not yet seen-him fully, or understood that it was Baxendale, and not his brother, that had come back to that place. " Yes, I've come back," said the man at last. "It isn't me they're looking for, you know, it's Baxendale. I'm safe enough. I've been a fool to remain away as long as I have. Come, aren't you going to say a word to me?"</p>
        <p>Baxendale stretched out a hand, and the woman drew near to the table and touched it for a moment with her own. In that moment Baxendale had caught her quickly by the wrist, and, springing to his feet, stared hard into her eyes. "Now, Mother Cridge, what do you think of your son? " he asked, in a fierce whisper. The woman looked at him for a moment incredulously, then sucked in her breath sharply. "Baxendale !" she breathed. "And what brings you here? Suppose I went out into the street now, and told anyone that the John Baxendale everyone is asking for was here in this place? " " You won't do that," he interrupted, quickly. 'The game is too risky. Besides, I'm not John Baxendale, I'm what you tried to make me a little time ago—l'm Rufus Cridge. The game's in my hands flow; you shall all dance to my piping. There are two men in whom you are deeply interested— John Baxendale and Rufus Cridge. It suit,</p>
        <p>The Peeresses.—The House of Lords makes ample provision for the presence of ladies—more than half the gallery space is reserved for them. and on great occasions, such as a debate and division on the Finance Bill, it is crowded with distinguished titled women. Their presence adds greatly to the brilliance of what is always a remarkable scene, although it has nowadays no political significance. That however, was not always the case and down to Victorian times, the Peeresses occasionally attempted t 3 influence the decision of the Legislature by appearing in force in the galleries of the House of Lords—Lord Lyndhurst once said that the place "had been turned into a casino, and was unfit for the transaction of business." The last occasion when an attempt was made to exert petticoat, influence, was in 1860, when Lady Palmerston and a large pirty of peeresses made a demonstration with the avowed object of inducing the Lords to reject the Bill for the repeal of the paper duties. A Royal Shooting frip. —The Duchess of Connaught, and Princess Patricia, are both familiar with British East Africa, whence the Duke and Prince Arthur are to proceed on a big game shooting expedition next month. The Duchess and Princess Patricia, who are again to accompany the party, went with the Duke when he last visited the Victoria Nyanza district, nearly four years ago. All the members of the Connaught family have been great travellers, and it is probable that the forthcoming expedition will follow closely on the lines of President Roosevelt's, although not so long a stay will be made in the country. According to present arrangements the Royal party is to leave on January 22nd, returning about the middle of May. Electric Appliances for the Home.—One of the London Electric Lighting Companies has fitted up a model villa at Catford as n permanent exhibit of what can be done with electric appliances by way of saving housework. So far, not much progress has been made in substituting electric methods, mainly owing to the uncertainty of cost, and the general belief that they are much more expensive than existing arrangemets. At this model house, everything that is possible is done by electricity. Hot water for the bedroom, early morning tea, and for the bath, is obtained by turning ona switch. The house is warmed throughout by electric heating radiators, and the cooking is done by electric) ovens, grills, self-contained heating cookers, and so on. Gas, coal, oil and candles, find no place in this dwelling, and it is easy to understand what on immense amount of housework this must save. The only thing is the question of cost upon which Company should give detailed information. In comparing this with present expenses there is not only the saving of gas, coal, and the wear and tear of furniture to consider, bat also the possibility of doing with fewer servants.</p>
        <p>The Servant Problem.—Lady St. Helier in her " Memories of Fifty Years " suggests that Mormonism which started in America in 1830, was really the out- come of the servant problem. She once visited Utah in company with Colonel Stanley, her first husband, and according to her, the pioneer settlers in the western states of America found that the only way to obtain and keep servants was to marry them, and a plurality of wives thus became a sort of necessity in the keeping up of a large establishment. Polygamy has long since been made illegal throughout the United States, but Lady St. Helier's remarks have given rise to the facetious suggestion that the social and domestic conditions of the Normon pioneers are reproducing themselves in this country, and that the only real solution of the servant problem is the one they adopted. That of course, is only a Gilbertian remedy, but another and at least a little more practical one, is suggested by a lady who puts her servants on a sort of sliding scale of good conduct pay." They have their fixed wages, as is usual, but over and above those they are offered some little extra pay and privileges, each week accord- ing to their having been more or less satisfactory in their work and duties. The Drawing-room.—The modern drawing-room with its dwarf chairs and rather dolls-house like furniture, always presents difficulties for people of more than ordinary height. If one happens to be tall, one cannot sit with comfort, or grace of bearing on a, chair that would be more suitable for the nursery and it is perhaps for reasons such as these that there is talk of going back to the mid-Victorian style of draw- ing-room. The girls of that period had not learnt how to grow tall, as the present athletic generation of women has, and the modern drawing-room would have been more suitable for them,than for the people of the present day. The very tall woman is often envied for her commanding height, but she has many discomforts to put up with, and often has a difficulty in preventing these inconveniences becoming apparent to other people, especially in the drawing-room. Modes at the Tailors.—Tho girl or woman who has found the task of walking in a skirt, closely resembling a bolster case, difficult, will rejoice at the promise of , better times to come. For all our best tailors are I introducing a little fulness into the hem of their walk- ing skirts, an idea infinitely more becoming aad graceful than the"lamp-post "notion, in which we have lately indulged to a considerable degree. It is very possible that with the fuller skirts may arrive fuller sleeves, therefore impecunious seekers after new tailor- made costumes, would do well to pause and consider, before deciding on the style. The best plan undoubtedly is to endeavour to steer a middle course, and neither be dowdy, run to extremes, and when an extra length of the fabric is available, it should be regarded as a treasure, and stored up for new sleeves, or other renovations, that will bring the costume up-to- date.</p>
        <p>No Turbans.—The combination of net and fur is well to the fore in the millinery world, and when the some- what heavy-looking fur turban is concerned, the net makes for lightness and is altogether desirable. The mode is an expensive one however, fur the net needs to be renewed so very frequently. The all-velvet turban is perhaps more generally becoming, the best creations being closely gathered. A flowing veil is. usually arranged about the crown, thrown back, aad allowed to float at will.</p>
        <p>Ninon.—For yokes and sleeves, also the little under bodices so modish just now, the shallow-pursed would do well to bear in mind the fact that ninon is much to be preferred to chiffon. It wears very much better. and looks almost as well, and in the matter of colour- ing, nothing is left to be desired. Very dainty after- noon blouses are just now being being built of ninon, much inlet with lace. These exactly match the skirt in tone, and taken altogether, are a huge success. About Mending Gloves.—Well-dressed women do not care to be seen in mended gloves. Comparatively few,, however, seem to realise that it is possible to accom- plish such a task in a manner absolutely impossible of detection. Human hair is employed, instead of silk or cotton, and this in all shades is obtainable, of course,of all hairdressers. The idea may seem a little weird, but the fact remains that suede and kid gloves mended with hair, usually come through such a process triumphantly. A Shortage of Eggs.—Housewives always expect to pay more for eggs at Christmastime but this year it seems there is almost a famine in them, and also in bacon, both of which threaten to be exceptionally dear. The bad summer has checked the supply, not only in Britain but also in Denmark, Italy, Austria, and other countries, which send us large quantities of eggs. It is said that there is a shortage of 200,000,000 eggs from Austria and Hungary alone, a deficiency which must inevitably make itself felt in our markets. That, no doubt, is good news for the home poultry farmer, but for the housewife it may mean paying 2s. a dozen for cooking eggs before Christmas.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>FORESTERS' NOTABLE ANNIVERSARY.</head>
        <p>FORESTERS' NOTABLE ANNIVERSARY.</p>
        <p>Replies to Clerical Criticism of Warrington.</p>
        <p>The seventy-fifth anniversary of the Warrington District of the Ancient Order of Foresters was cele- brated on Saturday evening, when a large gathering of members assembled at a tea and social held at the Lion Hotel. Complete success attended the function, the arrangements having been admirably carried out by the following committee : - Chairman, D.C.R. J. Grayson; vice-chairman, D.S.C.R. W. Bancroft; hon. secretary and treasurer, P.D.C.R. Peter Peacock ; district secre- tary, L. Davies ; P.D.C.R.'s J. Walsh, J. Chrimes, H. James, J. Hollins, R. Thornley, and W. R. Smith; P.C.R.'s J. Davies, J. Lockett, S. B. Healey, G. %V. R. Baines, and J. McCulley. After tea a capital musical programme was submitted and various toasts were honoured. Bro. PEACOCK said Bro. Robert Pierpoint regretted that a previous promise prevented him attending. Bro. A. H. Crosfield, M.P. accepted the invitation to attend but he wrote on Thursday stating that the doctor had informed him that he must take all the rest he could owing to the strain of the past few months and he was therefore regretfully obliged to forego the pleasure of meeting his brother Foresters. "It is a great disappointment to sue" he wrote " to miss a friendly evening with them, for I can assure you this Ancient Order of Foresters has no more sincere well wisher than yours faithfully A. H. Crosfield." Dr. Martin also expressed regret that he could not attend. P.D.C.R. CHRIMES proposed the toast of" The Army and Navy." Bro. Major APPLETON, in responding, said it was the duty of the nation to do all in its power to WO that our forces on land and sea were strong enough and that they were equipped with the best weapons. Politicians on both sides told us that all was well; be trusted it was so. A neighbouring country, which was now the second strongest naval power in the world, was, how- ever, spending this year £500,000 more than England was doing. The members of the Territorial Army were only too anxious to do their .best, but, considering the amount of training they received, was it right to expect them to meet those whom they might possibly have to meet—the highest trained troops in the world? He did not wish to be gloomy but the question was a very important one and he asked them, as sound.level- headed men, to think it over. Men like Lord Cromer and Lord Roberts, with no political axe to grind, told us that all was not well. The horrors of war were so dreadful that no one who had seen even as little of war as he (the speeker) had, would ever wish for war again. Therefore soldiers and sailors appealed to the nation to be strong, and make war impossible. (Applause). Bro. Alderman TINNION proposed "The Order and Warrington District. He said the Order was one of ih3 most important friendly societies in the United Kingdom and the details of organization of such a vast society filled a comparative outsider like himself with amazement. It was one of the most striking proofs of what could be done by combination, ability, strength and patience of the workingmen. The objects achieved by the Order—the relief and maintenance of members in sickness, infirmity and old-age, and the assistance of their wives and children—were far ahead of the times or of modern legislation—and they hoped that what- ever legislation might take place in the interests of the various classes of society, the Government would bear in mind that the present friendly societies were the only true foundation on which they could build. Their aim was self-help, not selfish help, and they wished to make men realize their brotherhood. Friendly societies tended to make men more thrifty and industrious and better citizens. They helped to mould and improve their characters, and that was the foundation of all brotherhood. That Order was managed by the brethren for the brethren; every member, no matter how humble, could take some part, according to his desire and ability. One of the grand features was that there were no proprietors except the members and no shareholders to take the plums. The society was founded on its present basis seventy-five years ago and the fact that it had existed so long showed it must have been built upon a sure foundation. He hoped they would all live to celebrate its centenary. The last balance sheet of the Order showed a membership of 912,815 and if they added members' wives who had an interest in the society, the grand total was 1,289,901. (Applause). The funds amounted to £9,151,171, the increase for the past year being £258,696. During the seventy-five years of its existence, £27,000,000 had been spent in relieving sickness, distress and infirmity and In paying sums at death. The average income was £1,200,000 aith an expenditure of nearly £l,ooo,oooper annum. They hid every reason to be proud of the position of the War- rington district, which had a membership of 1,200, comprising six Courts, with funds of nearly £7,000. The High Court was held at Warrington in 1841 and they would be exceedingly proud if the meetings could be held there again. Legislation would probably take place in the immediate future with reference to State insurance. He thought it would be admitted by all thinking men that the State should do something for those people whom the friendly societies</p>
        <p>did not touch—the blind, consumptive, aged and weak who did not got the full benefit of the friendly societies. He hoped that whatever was done in the direction of alleviating their distress would be done with the assistance and co-operation of the friendly Societies. Mr. Lloyd-George had given an intimation to the officials of the societies that anything that was attempted wonld be based on those lines. Alderman Tinnion concluded by observing that their Order had an illustrious past and they believed a still greater future awaited it. ..(Applause).. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <p>. _ • •</p>
        <p>Bro. L. DA VIES, District Secretary, in responding said he was pleased the district was in a very happy and prosperous condition and that every branch was able to pay what it had promised. Owing to slackness of trade in the town they had not been able to enroll as many new members as they desired, but they hoped the tide would turn next year. (Applause). P.C.R. BAINES proposed) "The town and) trade of Warrington." He said that despite the abominable attack of a certain clergyman, couched in the usual extravagant language experienced from that quarter, they were satisfied that the town, with all its faults, could hold its own with regard to manners, morals or any subject for comparison referred to. (Applause.) Bro. Alderman TINNION, in responding, said he was pleased that Bro. Baines had rebutted the statements recently made by a neighbouring clergyman. Even admitting that Warrington was as bad as he endeavoured to paint it, the rev, gentleman might have been loss brutal in his expressions than he was when he sent forth that miserable epistle. He (the speaker) denied the picture he painted in any way represented the social or moral condition of Warrington. He had had (some considerable experience ; lie had been in every town in Lancashire, and in many other towns in the North of England. He did not say that Warrington was perfect— far from it ; there were some blots which seemed inherent in all manufacturing towns—but it would compare more than favourably with any or most towns in Lancashire. What he would say to the rev, gentlemen was "Less of your talk. Come down from your high pedestal of perfection and purity and roll up your shirt sleeves, and if it is anything like as bad as you have painted it, it is your duty to come and help those who are endeavouring to uplift those who are not as happy and as favourably placed as many of us would wish." Continuing Alderman Tiunion referred to the increasing importance of the work of Town Councils and to the necessity for doing it con- scientiously and honestly. He was prepared to say the Warrington Town Council of to-day compared very favourably with the councillors and aldermen of the past, in their desire to do honest work, to improve the health and comfort of the town and the happiness of its inhabitants. There was a great deal of work yet to be done. They had a very conscientious medical officer of health who would leave the town—they hoped it would not be soon—in a much healthier, purer rind altogether better condition than it was to- day. Warrington as a manufacturing town stood high in the list in regard to the works of a productive character which contributed to the rates. The gas undertaking was exceedingly well managed, they had an excellent electricity and tramway service, a splendid supply of water, and the markets and many other under- takings which assisted the reduction of rates. Having alluded to the diversity of local industries, Alderman Tinnion spoke hopefully of the prospects of an early revival of trade, as evidenced by the fact that America was said to be looking forward to a great boom and that the harvests of the world were satisfactory. He concluded by expressing his satisfaction that Bro. Chrimes had been nominated for a seat on the Educa- tion Committee and wished him success. (Applause.) During the evening Bro. GEORGE MATBURY presented P.D.C.R. J. Walsh with a handsomely framed emblem. An interesting musical programme vvaa submitted, including sengs by Miss Annie Bancroft, Miss Sara Silcock, and Bro. J. Stockton, humorous songs by Bro. W. Jones, trombone solos by Mr. J. E. Hacking, and selections by the Apollo Quartette Party (Messrs. Critcbley, Cook, Gittens and Aukland. By permission of Mr. Eric Longdeb, manager of the Hippodrome. Mr. Harry Nelson (comedian) and Mr. Penstone (conjuror) also appeared and added considerably to the success of the entertainment. Mr. W. Pemberton was the accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>SIMPLE PRESCRIPTION.</head>
        <p>A SIMPLE PRESCRIPTION.</p>
        <p>SAID TO CURE RHEUMATISM, KIDNEY TROUBLE AND BLADDER TROUBLES.</p>
        <p>We know that the readers of this paper appreciate good advice, and we are glad, therefore, to be able to publish the following from a prominent physician. It is astonishing to knop, the prevalence of kidney trouble and other diseases, such as rheumatism, bladder and liver troubles, which are almost invariably caused by weak kidneys. It is said on good authority that one-third of the people in the United Kingdom who have reached the age of maturity are more or less afflicted with kidney trouble in some form. The most dangerous feature of this disease is the fact that many people have it without their knowledge, and conse- quently neglect it until it has reached a dangerous stage. The least symptom of kidney trouble, such as back- ache, must be given prompt attention, and a good reliable treatment should be taken, until every trace of the disease has been eliminated. We are glad to be able to give our readers a prescription which we have seldom known to fail in any case. It contains no harmful ingredients, is pleasant to take, not expensive, and can be supplied by any good chemist. It is as follows :</p>
        <p>Tincture Gentian Compound, one ounce; Syrup Rhubarb, one ounce ; Liquid Barkola Compound, one ounce ; Syrup of Ginger, five ounces. Take a teaspoonful after each meal, and one at bed- time.</p>
        <p>Wo are glad to publish this proscription, as we know it will help our afflicted readers who give it a fair trial. If taken continuously for a few weeks, it will be found to be an almost absolutely certain cure for Rheumatism, Kidney and Bladder Trouble, Backache, and all allied complaints. _</p>
        <p>NOte.—Other papers are privileged to copy the above.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
