      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>DISTRICT NEWS.</head>
        <p>DISTRICT NEWS.</p>
        <p>Whitley.</p>
        <p>CONCERT.—A successful concert was held in the C9uncil schools, the other evening, the vicar (Res. J. C. Trampleastue) presiding. Tbe programme included several pleasing item by Mr. H. Berrey's party from 'Warrington. Miss A. Moore (Whitley) received loud applause for her ftne rendering of the song," Swallows." The most popular feature of the evening was the play entitled "Mrs. Jenkins lodger," given by the Antrobus amateurs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>Culcheth.</head>
        <p>Culcheth.</p>
        <p>• INDEPENDENT METHODIST CHAPEL.—The annual congregational tea meeting and social in connection with this place was held on Saturday last in the school- room. About 60 members and friends sat down to tea after which a concert wi,s held, Mr. J. J. Hunt occupy- ing the chair. Mr. H. Griffiths of Manchester, who was the pianist, opened the concert with a piandotte solo from Wegner's Tanhauser " Act M. Miss Barton sang in a very pleasing manner" Willie's got another girl now" and Miss Kenyon was equally succe3sful in a song entitled " The Fairies' lullany." Miss Runcorn sang with much expression" Sunshine and butterflies" and The boys in scarlet and gold "and an excellent rendering of' The Gondolier " wa4 given by Miss Elsie Willocits. " Ttfe Kings Own" was ably rendered by Mr. W. J. Bate and" Oh I must go home to-night" and" A to Z "sung by Mr. J. R. Fletcher were much appreciated. 'Mr. W. Kenyon once more convulsed his audience by his humorous rendering of "We all jumped out of bed" and" All of a ditherum " and Mr. J. Sutton's song entitled" If I had been born a girl "was sung with befitting gusto. A humorous trio " Who'll shut the door "was sung by Miss Kenyon. Messrs. W. Kenyon and W. J. Bate who created much amusement, and a reading enticed" Owd Pinder " by Mr. E. Baxton added much to the interest of the evening. After the concert games &amp;c., were indulged in and refreshments were served at intervals. The evening throughout was very enjoyable and the prcceeds were in aid of the building ftnd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>Orford.</head>
        <p>Orford.</p>
        <p>DEDICATION OF NEW LFCTERN.—The old boys annual service at Orford Church on Sunday evening was rendered additionally interesting and impressive by the dedication, by the Rev. Canon Smithwrck, of Seaforth, of the lectern (presented by the Orford Old Boys', Association.) There was a large congregation. The members of tile Association assembled in Orford School at 5.30 and marched in procession to the church. The lectern, which has teen supplied by Messrs. R. Garnett and Scn to a design by Mr. A. 'Warburton, is of Dantzic oak to harmonize suth the general furniture of the church. The service VMS conducted by the Rev. G. Vale Owen, vicar. The first lesson was read by Mr. J. Cann (president of the associa- tion) and the second by Councillor Steel (bon. treasurer). The usual dedication ceremony was gone through in the early part of the service. In the course of his serer on Canon Smithwick said it was to be feared that the Bible was not as much read and studied to-day as it was by those who had gone before them, although, he was thankful to say, there were signs of a revival of Bible reading and of Bible study. It would be a had day for this country if we gave up reading the Bible. That beautiful lectern (on which the Bible would rest), the gift of loving and generous hearts, would testify to the valve the Church of England had always attached to the reading of God's word.—Special hymns were sung, and Mr. John Gibson presided at the organ. A collection was taken in aid of the building fund. Before separating the Te Deam was sang as a special act of thanksgiving.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>Padgate.</head>
        <p>Padgate.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN CHAPEL.—The annual foreign missionary sermons were preached in this place on s:unpay morn- ing last by the Rev. E. B. Keeling and in the evening by Mr. J. Upson, collections being taken at both the services in aid of the missionary fund. On Monday evening a lecture was given iri the chapel by the Rev. Simon C. Hall of West Africa. The annual report was read by the Rev. T. Gladding after which Alderman A. Bennett who occupied the chair spoke for a short time on the general condition of Africa. Tbe Rev. Simon C. Hall on rising to address the audience was received with appiamse and in referring }lick to some remarks of the chairman said that one can hardly help believing in the power of good government. There are many things done in West Africa which would cause English- men to blush and we in England have just cause for thankfulness, knowing that wherever the English fisg flies there is liberty of speech. The pro- gress of Christianity during the last nine years has been more marked than in any previous century. (Applause.) Iha question uppermost in our minds to-day is, how can we complete the ov2ngelization of the world ? The difficulties are very great, especially in Africa, when w,e considur what great forces are at work behind the people in this heathen country. Take their relig'on and you will find that in the whole range of West African deities von cannot discern one atom of gentleness, goodness, holiness or purity. All service to their gods is rendered by fear and all the people have to do is to keep "Bosom " in a good temper. Human blood is one of the most brutal means by which his wrath is appeased and thousands of people are put to death in order that their blood may Ia used as an atonement. Speaking of missionary work in general he detailed some of the foods which they had to eat, after which he went on to speak of circuit work. He said the circuits were very large and long distances had to be travelled every day without any trains or beasts of any kind to assist them in their travels. He had to preach on an average four times a day and In some of the villages the Christians were very few. The good work, however, was being carried on with untiring energy,and he appealed for help which was sorely needed to carry the message of Salvation to those in far distant Africa.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>Stockton Heath.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>GARDEN SOCIETY'S VISIT TO WALTON HALL.—At the invitation or Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., about fifty members of the Stokton Heath Garden Society paid another 'visit to Walton Hall on Sunday afternoon and spent a pleasant time iii inspecting the conserva- tories and the magnificent show of chrysanthemums. The party was conducted by Mr. Groves, the head gardener. HILL CLIFFn BAPTIST CHURCH.—The annual con- gregaiional tea and meeting in connection u ith the above church proved a highly successful gathering. At the public meeting the chair was occupied by Mr. Richard Billington, of Stretton, and speeches were delivered by Mr. J. W. Scholfield, the moderator of the Lancashire and Cheshire Baptists' Association, and Mr. T. Such. Solos were rendered by Mrs. J. Kenworthy and others and special anthems were given by the choir. Mr. T. Such gave an excellent recitation. TREE PLANTING COMMITTEE. ANNUAL MEETING. - -</p>
        <p>After a ycar's quiet but useful work beautifying the village the members of the Tree Planting Committee held their annual meeting on Wednesday evening at the Hall Cafe, for the purpose of receiving the Committee's report, statement of receipts, and expenditure, and appointing a committee, and officers for the coming sear. The chairman (Mr. C. Plinston) presiaed, Mr. A. Fawcett, (secretary) was also present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>CHAIRMAN'S itEMARKS.</head>
        <p>CHAIRMAN'S itEMARKS.</p>
        <p>At the commencement of the meeting the chairman reviewed the work of the past year and said there was a very different feeling in the aistrict, that night than there was twelve months ago. They were then to a certain extent under a cloud, as some people had got a false idea with regard to, their sale of work. However be thought that inert was dispelled and the sale of work proved a remarkable success. During the year the committee tad been busy keeeping the various beds of plants and shrubs in order and attending to the paint- ing of the remaining guards. Tbe beds on the canal bank were undoubtedly doing well and were very attractive, they were getting rather overcrowded and they had cossidered the advisability of transferring some of the shrubs, to other beds to replace dead ones. They were anxious, like a good many other people in Stockton Heath, that the hoardings at the corner of Ellesmere-roAd and Fairtield-road, should be removed and there were very good signs, that before very, long that might be accomplished, and the place instead of being an eye-sore, would be made into a beauty spot. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>COMMITTEE'S REPORT..</head>
        <p>COMMITTEE'S REPORT..</p>
        <p>The ecretary read the Committee's report, ahich said that the work of the past twelve months bad been, in comparison with previous years, light. They had left the trees and shrubs under their care, and had replaced the dead trees with new ones, where thought necessary. At the commencement. of the year they were in the state of indebtedness to the amount of £32. That, they were pleased to say, had been paid off, and they finished the year with a balance in hand of nearly £5O. (Applause). The most onerous work of they ear was the carrying out of the sale of work, which entailed much thought and devotion on the part of the Committee, and also the Ladies Committee. They owed a deep debt of gratitude to the Ladies' Committee, who had worked with untiring energy, and brought the sale to a successful issue. In conclusion, the report real : We trust we retain your confidence, and we thank you for your past kindness. Mr. W: El. Woodcock said he had great pleasure in proposing the adoption of the report. With regard to the past year's work, anybody, during the past summer, viewing the long vista of Ellesmere-road and Fairfield- road, would find a scene of beauty. Those who had experience of the planting of trees knew that it would take some years before they would grow to perfection and Stockton Heath become a garden city, but he believed that people who had not been in the district for about twelve months would realise what a great great improvement had been made. It was wonderful how much enthusiasm had been display the members and particularly by the ladies committee, whose hearts seemed to be so full of the good work. (Applause). Mr. Fletcher seconded and the report am passed. RECEIPTS AND EXPENDITURE.</p>
        <p>The Secretary read the b4ance sheet which showAd a</p>
        <p>Most Grocers of repute sell "GOLDEN FLEECE" MA.R.O ARM. Have you tried it ?—Advt.</p>
      </div>
