      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <p>r. . . , . , ,</p>
        <p>total income of £lO6 Bs. 6‘1., and a balance in hand of £49 Os. 3d. Mr. McDermid proposed and Mr. Leach seconded the adoption of the accounts, which was agreed to. ELECTIONS. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Lymm, step-son of the plaintiff, spoke to harrovving field on plaintiff's instructions. t Peter Leather, Broom Edge, said be did some plough- ing and was paid by Mr. Lord. William Winstanley, Dairy Farm, Lyinm, said he also ploughed for the plaintiff and received payment from him for the work and also for seed. By Mr. Acton : He bad done similar work for Whitelegg and had been paid by him. William Milliard, clerk to the Lymm Urban District Council, produced the poor rate book showing that Lord was rated for the land in question and had paid the rates. Mr. Acton submitted that there was no case for the defendant to answer. He produced the lease of the land which showed that Whitelegg was the lessee. His Honour: lam afraid I cannot say there is no case. William Henry Whitelegg was then called and denied that there was any arrangement that Lord should be the tenant for the fir6t year. The lease was read and understood by the defendant and it provided that the land should not be sub-let. V itness did most of the harrowing and he also mowed the crop of oats. While he was doing so Lord came up to him and told him he would kill him if he didn't get out of the field. Witness told him to get off the land as he did not want to talk to hint. Witness negotiated the sale with Davies who by way otf payment cleared off arrears of rent which witness was owing in respect of the land. By Mr. Jordan: Plaintiff did not take Raven Bank on witness's behalf, but simply for himself. Witness did the work there because it was supposed that Lord had taken it for him. The land bad now been taken by William Hinton but witness could not say whether the landlord had released him (Whitelegg) from the lease. Mr. Jordan: When you had.got the money did you take a ticket to emigrate to America ?—No, I swear I never booked my passage. Did you take steps to book your passage ?—I went to see a certain party. Didn't you take steps to emigrate?—No sir, I was talking of going at the time. His Honour said they had heard that the tenant of the land was prohibited from under-letting, but it was quite possible that there might have been some under- standing or agreement between Lord and Wbitelegg that the land should be managed in a certain Way. But the point before him was this : Here was a defendant, who upon the evidence, had honestly and bona MAI/ purchased the crops and had honestly paid for them, and action was brought against him for the price of that which he had already paid for. He must therefore look strictly into the facts of the case to see if such a claim was justified. After reviewing the evidence his Honour said he accepted Whiteleig's version as to certain arrangements between the parties and,he gave judgment for the defendant with costs on Scale C.</p>
        <p>The following committee was elected for the ensuing year :—Messrs. T. Baxter, H. Fletcher, 0. Hudson, T. Grant, J. Mynekyne, J. McDermid, Dr. Parker C. Plinston, W. Gorton, F. Timperley, W. A. Timperley, W. H. Woodcock, E: G. White, D. E. Woods, A. C. Allen. Mr. A. Fawcett was again elected secretary and Mr. •C. Plinston trvasurer. The Chairman said that last year he announced that the committee would have to maintain the trees for five years, on the expiration of which period they would be taken over by the local parish councils. The beds on the canal bank could not be placed in the care of the Council but the Garden Society would no doubt undertake to keep them in order after the five years had elapsed. •</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 4, 1909.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>THANKS.</head>
        <p>THANKS.</p>
        <p>It was decided to thank Mr. McDermid for the painting of the guard round the tree in the centre of the square, also to thank Mr. Bedell for replacing a dead tree with a new one in front of the house.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>Lymm.</head>
        <p>Lymm.</p>
        <p>SIR W. J. CROSSLEY AT LYMM.—A full report of Sir W. J. Crossley's meeting on Tuesday at the Drill Hall, will be found on page 2. CONGREGATIONAL GUILD.—The meeting of this society was held last evening. A lecture' was given by the Rev. A. Barrett, president of the Guild, dealing with the early history of Congregationalism and the struggle for religious liberty, which our forefathers had so bravely made in the past. The lecture was illustrated with lentern views. CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH.—A remnant sale of goods left, over from the bazaar of last January, was held on Friday last week, followed on the Saturday evening with a jumblesale. Both events were very satisfactory realizing the sum of manly £lB. During the evening of the remnant sale a short but exceedingly pleasing programme of musio was rendered by Misses Bradbury and Rogerson and Mr. John Moss. BOCKEY.—LYMM LADIES V. LYMM GRAMMAR SCHOOL.—Tbe atooye match was played on Saturday last, on the ground of the Ladies' Club,amidst a steady downpour of rain, and concluded in a draw, no goals being registered for either side. The play,was stubborn, the unpropitious state of the elements scarce entering into the consideration of the players, and on both sides at the close of telay, the opinion was freely . expressed that the geme had been of a most enjoyable diameter.</p>
        <p>SALE OF WORK.—A successful sale of work took place on Wednesday in the Wesleyan schoolroom. The room had been very beautifully decorated by the elder scholars and friends and looked quite a picture. Mrs. J. H. Brown, of Heswall opened the sale of work, and Mr. R. H. Meldrum, a late resident of Lyrum, presided. There was a very pretty Christmas tree; refreshment stall and fancy goons btatl ; and a quotatiop cake which caused much amusement. Mr. Hudson, as Father Christmas, caused a gi eat deal of fun for the children who thmoughly enjoyed themselves. The proceeds amounted to over £69, and were in aid of the church funds. WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.—A meeting of the Women's Libetal Association was held on Thursday evening in the Congregational School. Owing to the extremely rough night there was but a moderate attendance. Miss Welford ,occupied the chair and in a i few well chosen words welcomed n the name of the association, the speaker for the evening, Mr. Francis Milne of Bowdon, to whom she referred as one who was always welcome at Lymm and for whom there was a great feeling of respect and regard on the part of all who knew him. Mr. Milne took for his subject "The future of Liberalism." He dwelt , largely upon the present action of theHouseof Lords in rejecting the Budget for AS he rightly remarked,on the coming election depends, to an extent never before remembered, the future of Liberalism. He then went on to show the many social reforms that the Liberal party of the future untrammelled by the acts of an hereditary chamber,might inculcate and carry through. At the conclusion of Mr. Milne's address a vote of thanks to him and to Miss Welford for presiding was moved •by Mr. Jas. D. B. Smith, seconded by Mrs. Jones and cordially carried.</p>
        <p>ODDFELLOWS' DINNER AND PRESENTA HON. The Lodge dinner of' the "Loyal Trafford" Lodge of the Independent Order of Oddfellows (Manchester Unity), was held on Saturday evening at the Bridge- water Arms, and was followed by the presentation of a handsome gold watch to Past Prov. Grand Master James Whitelegg, who for 33 years has held the office of secretary. Bro. J. H. Copeland presided, and he was supported by P.G.M. G. F. Freeman, Deputy P.G.M. T. B. Forbes, P.G.M. Lot Brooks (Cadishead), Frank Webster, T. Rigby, John Norbury and William Holbrook (of the Warrington District), John Whitlow, F. Bolt, C. E. Ardern, Heahert Whitelegg (secretary of' the Lymm Lodae)—Various toasts were proposed.— P.G. Herbert Whitelegg, in responding on behalf of the I Lynam Lodge, said he had been looking up some ()M i I books recently and could give them some interesting I figures. Founded in 1833 they bad that year 20 members and at the end of the year a balance in hand of £5. In 1875 90 members, balance £365 and £65 paid out in sick pay. In 1895 106 members, balance £576 and £45 paid in sick pay, in 1900 130 members, £B2O in hand, in 1903 150 members, balance £l,OOO and £lO7 sick pay. This year the sick pay was more than usual, members evidently not enjoying such good health, but every lodge must. have its ups and downs. So far this year £134 had been paid out ind it was a fleure of which in one sense they were very proud—(hear, hea r)—as it showed that their lodge was doing a good work iii Lymm. (Applause ) Grand Master Freeman, as chief officer of the district, presented to Bro. James W hitelega a handsome gold watch, which was inscribed "Presented to P.P.G.M. James Whitelegg by the membert of the " Loy al Trafford" Lodge 1.0.0. F. (M.U.), on his retirement from the secretaryship after 33 years of office, November 27th, 1909." The !late secretary had done a tremendous amount of work for Oddfellowship in Lymm. When he took over the office there were 90 members with a capital of £360; aft against 175 members and £l.9Co•in hand now. P.P.G.M. Adam Webster. P.G. John Whitlow. and Bros. R. Brooks, John Forrester and S. Hinde added their testimony to the late secretary's worth. Mr.' Herbert Whitelegg said his father desired him to thank them all for their handsome present and kind word. Mr. 'Henry Bradburn proposed the "Visiting brethren," and songs were given by .lessrp. Lot Brooks,, Fred Guest, W. Holbrook, H. Whitelegg, William Webster. F., B. Mellor and George Holt. blr. Stanley, of the " Constitution " Lodge officiating as accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>DISPUTED SALE OF CROPS. LYMM CASE AT THE WARRINGTON COUNTY COURT.</head>
        <p>DISPUTED SALE OF CROPS. LYMM CASE AT THE WARRINGTON COUNTY COURT.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before his Honour Juige Shiress Will; K.C., Joseph Lord, insurance agent, Woodbank-road, Sanay-lane, Lymm, sued Samuel Davies, farmer, Heatley, for £7O 7s. made up as follows :-8 acres of oats, £5O; 8 tons of straw, £10; 4 tons 12 cwt. of hay, £lO 7s. Mr. Jordan barrister (instructed by Messrs. Higham and Co, Altrincham), appeared for the plainiiff, and Mr. Acton barrister (instructed by Mr. G. L. Watford, Manchester). defended. Mr. Jordan said the facts of the case were a little complicated. The plaintiff had a son-in-law named Whitelegg, wbn! had been a stoker in the employ of the ailway company and was working his way up to become an engine driver when it was found that he was colour blind which, of course, prevented that career. Plaintiff thereupon took for him a market garden at Raven Bank so that he could make a living. At the beginning of this year Whitelegg was tvishful te take another farm and wanted his fattier-is-law to advance him the money. Lord, having already taken a market garden for him, said he could not do that and finally a verbal arrange- ment was made that Whitelegg should take the farm on a five years lease and that for the first year plaintiff should take the farm from Whitelegg and work it for his own loss or profit, Plaintiff had to continue paying the rent and rates on the Raven Bank farm while • the son-in-law was to be responsible for the rent and rates on the seconi farm. In pursuance of the arrangement plaintiff ploughed and sowed the land, paying for the seed and labour. When the hay crop was petting ready plaintiff got in communication with Mr. Davies , and asked him if he would examine the hay with a view to purchasing it.Mr.Davies went to the plaintiff's honde where be saw Whitelegg and it was altervtards dis- covered that be made some arrangement with White- legg and paid him for the crop. Later when the crop of oats was ready Whitelegg proceeded to cut it. Plaintiff told Mr. Davies that although Whitelegg had done that,the crop belonged to plaintiff and he must not cieal with Whitelegg with regard to it, On September 14th plaintiff's solicitor wrote to the defendant inform- ing him that the oats he had removed from the Race Field, Bridle-road, were the property of their client and not of Whitelegg, and requiring him to pay the plaintiff for the same. It, after that notice, he paid Whitelegg, I the plaintiff would hold him responsible. Subsequently a claim was put in and Mr. Welford, defendant's solici- tor, wrote stating that be had seen the documents relat- ing to the matter, and was at a loss to understand the grounds on which plaintiff claimed to be the owner of the crop or to be Jaid the price thereof which had already been paid to Whitelegg. Mr. Jordan contended that assuming plaintiff could satisfy his Honour that the arrangement was made between him and Whitelegg and that this was brought to the notice of the defend- ant, if Mr. Davies took the oats and paid Whitelegg for them he''clid so with the knowledge that Whitelegg had no right to them. Plaintiff was called and bore out counsel's statement. In answer to Mr. Acton, he said there had been a family quarrel. He did not remember that there was a clause in the lease to the effect that" the lessee shall not assign or sub-let the premises or any pert thereof without the consent in writing of the lessors, unless such consent shall be reasonably withheld." Maria Lord, wife of the plaintiff, said defendant was told that the oats belonged to plaintiff and that if he' (Davies) bought them from anyone else he would be buying stolen property. Defenaant said he had seen the lease and he considered Whitelegg was entitled to sell the oats. George Arthur Dither, milk dealer, Bridge Foot,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Mr. John Littler (in the chhir) and Mr. Francis Wilmall. DUTCH COURAGE.—Job Jones a boatman, was summoned for loving been drunk and disorderly.— Police Constable Wynne said Job was making use of bad language towards his wife.—Mr. Littler said defendant had a big lot of courage when in drink. He would be fined ss. BROTHERS ROLLING IN THE IVlllD.—Thomas and Henry Woodflne, brothers, of Duckett's-court, were charged with being drunk and disorderly on Saturday night.—Deiective Davies said prisoners were fighting in King-street.. They were drunk and rolling in the mud Henry was covered with slutch from bead ta foot and you could not tell l'a-hat it was. (Laughter.) He separated them hut in a few minutes they returned to the conflict. Henry was beasty drunk.—Prisoners were fined ds. and 7s. costs or 14 days hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>TRAVELLED IN A HORSE-BOX WITHOUT A TICKET.</head>
        <p>TRAVELLED IN A HORSE-BOX WITHOUT A TICKET.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Police Court, on Monday, Richard Mockler, horsebreaker, 103, Church-street,was summoned for having travelled on the London and North-Western Railway without having previously paid his fare, and with intent to avoid payment. Mr. R. L. Mason, on behalf of the company. said that on September 20tb tbe defendant travelled in a horse-box with a horse from Wigan to Runcorn. On arriving at the latter place the defendant, when asked for his ticket, said be had not come by train, he said he had given his ticket up at Widnes or Ditton. He was then told that tickets were not collected at either place. Later he said he had a ticket, but no tickets were issued to Runcorn on the date in question. Ultimately the defendant said be was drunk, and did not know anything abOut it.—The defendant now said he had been to a trotting match at Wigan. He had too much drink, and did not remember anything about it. It was'pointed out that the defend- ant paid the fare for the horse. The bench imposed a tine of 20s. and costs.</p>
        <p>GIFTS FOR RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>MR. F. J. NORMAN'S MUNIFICENT OFFER.</p>
        <p>Runcorn Urban Council on Wednesday received trom Mr. Fred J. Norman a local chemical manufacturer, a munificent offer to finance th:ee schemes as a memorial to his father&amp;nd wife : 1. The building of a number of model semi-detached citizens' homes adjoining the Cotthge Hospital, no taint of charity to attach to the occupation of them, and no sectarian test to be imposed. 2. The furnishing of one or more houses where girls of the Balfour-road School could acquire practical housewifery. 3. The completing of the Cottage Hospital by erect- ing another ward. Mr. Norman in his letter wrote: "I pray you dis- pense with formalities, such as stone-layings and openings, and dismiss me from any prominence, bar the privilege of paying the cost and sharing the spadework. Trade is quiet, and any or all of the schemes may be proceeded with at once with advantage to the unem- ployed." Mr. Norman was thanked for his generous offer, and the matter was referred to a committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>WIDNES NEWSPAPER SUED FOR LIBEL.</head>
        <p>WIDNES NEWSPAPER SUED FOR LIBEL.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Assizes on Wednesday an action for libel was brought by Thomas Davies, con- sulting engineer, who was until recently the managing director of T. Davies, Limited, engineers and boiler- makers, of Widnes, against Joseph Swale, the editor and publisher of the "Widnes Weekly News." The alleged libel was contained in the report of a meeting of the creditors of T. Davies, Limited, which was published in the defendant's newspaper on April 23. The defence admitted that the words were defamatory, but denied that they were capable of bearing the mean- ing attributed to them by the plaintiff. Mr. Horridge, K.C., M.P., and Mr. Cuthbert Smith (instructed by Messrs. Collins, Robinson, Driffields, and Kusel) were counsel for the plaintiff, and Mr. J. Layton (instructed by Messrs. F. J. and C. Poole) represented 'the defendant.</p>
        <p>Plaintiff gave evidence in accord with his advocate's statement. The creditors' meeting, he said, was private and two or three members of the Press who applied for admission were turned away. For the defence no evidence was called. Mr. Layton contended that the report was fair and accurate, and was published in good faith and without malice. Defendant had never hesitated to publish the correspondence in which the suggestions against the plaintiff were withdrawn. The defendant did not justify the libel, and never had said one word which would tarnish the character of Mr. Davies, who was a man of high repute. The newspaper was one with a local circulation only, ankl the case was one which would be met with nominal damages The jury found for the plaintiff, awarding £lOO damages ; and judgment was entered accordingly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>COWARDLY HUSBAND PUNISHED.</p>
        <p>Thomas Duxbury, R collier, of Watery-lane, who was remanded on Saturday on a charge of aggravated assault on his wife, was dealt with at St. Helens on Monday. The case was adjourned to enable a Mrs. Roberts to give evidence confirming the statements of Mrs. Duxbury that her husband ran after her into Mrs. Roberts's house and nearly killed her. Complainant was suffering from extensive .bruises, and had two black eyes and a battered face. The assault was committed on Wednesday night last. The evidence was that the man regularly kept his wife and family short of food and proper clothing, and he was sent to gaol for two mouths' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>DEATH OF A ST. HELENS TRADESMAN.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF A ST. HELENS TRADESMAN.</p>
        <p>Great regret was occasioned at St. Helens on Saturday by the news of the death of Mr. Richard Sturgiess, tailor, of Bridge-street, and one of the first members of the St. Helens Cyclists's Club, of which he was vice-president. Mr. Sturgiess was sixty-four years of age. He was a native of Burscough and had carried on business in St. Helens for forty-six years. He was well known over a wide area, and his demise will be sincerely regretted. Death took place quite suddenly at one o'clock after about four days' illness. He was of a most genial and kindly disposition, a member of the 241 Lodge of Freemasons (Liverpool), director of.tbe Eccleston Bowling Club, and a member of the St. Helens Bowling Club. His favourite recreation, however, was cycling, and all through the past summer season he wag a regular participat in the Thursday club run. Mr. Sturgiess leaves one son and one daughter. The funeral took place in Burscough Cemetery on Tuesday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <p>When you ask for "GOLDEN FLEECE" MARGARINE see that you get it. Don't be put off with substi- tutes.—Adv t.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>GREAT MEET OF HARRIERS,</head>
        <p>GREAT MEET OF HARRIERS,</p>
        <p>Photo]</p>
        <p>;Tonge.</p>
        <p>3. PARSONAGE, WARRINGTON A.C. (Winner of' the 5 miles Team Race, and Silver Cup presented by Lady Greenall).</p>
        <p>position throughrut. J. Twiss, of the same cJub came in second; S. J. Hamilton, Warrington U.H., third: R. Ueswick, Warrington A.C., fourth ' • W. Stewart, War- rington fifth ; and W. Thorniley, Warrington, U.H., sixth. Warrington U.H. were the winners of the team race, the placings being ;—l, Stewart; 2, Thorniley ; 3, Shadwell • 4. Hunt, 5, W. Litberland ; 6, T.,RoWlinson. The unpfaced medal winners were: J. Twiss, Warrington .A.C., S. Taylor, St. Helens Cable A.C; and C. A. Harding Rylands' Workmen R.C.H. A fine struggle was witnessed in the six n.ile scratch race. C. Vose (Warrington U.H.) the winner, ran splendidly throughout, and easily obtained first place by about 300 yards. F. Antrobus, Warrington A.C., came in second, and A. Willcock. Warrington U.H.. third. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The arrangements for the meet were admirable and special praise is due to the hardworking and enter- prising hon. secretary, Mr.A.Bailie. '1 he other officials were :—Finance Committee: Mr. W. Peter Rylands, the Mayor (Alderman Forshaw), Alderman J.Smethurst, Mr. Henry Roberts : Stewards, Messrs. G. - Walker (chief), H. Hope, H. Collin, J. Fargher, F. Short, A. Booth, W. FL Smalley, W. BaMber, S. C. Price, W. Chalmers, and T.Locker ; Judges; Messrs.E. Pickavance, N.C.U., C. Berry, J. Alcock, J. Bell, H. Msddock, W. Webb, A.W. Dun. J. Potts ; Referee, Mr. W. Price; Timekeeper, Mr. H. N. Ellis (14. and D.C.C.A.); Hon. Starter. Mr. G. Eastwood; Competitors' and Field Stewards' Messrs, F. Berry (chief), B. Bate, W. Broad- hurst, J. Holden, W. W. Whittle; Clerks of the course, Messrs. A. Bailie (chief), T. Cotterill, W. Lattimer, T. Nonns, and W. W. Wildinc.</p>
        <p>_ Sir GILBERT and Lady GREENALL were present and at the close of the races Lady Greenall distributed the prizes which she had kindly given. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to her on the proposition of Air. G. Walker seconded by Councill.3l Steel. Lady GREENALL in reply referred to the value of sr:on and said she thought that if foreigners had come amongst them that afternoon and seen the pluck and endurance displayed they would not Le over anxious to make war upon our country. (Applause). Sir GILBERT GREENALL said the running had been splendid. (Applause). He did not think he could manage it—(laughter)--but he dared say he would have a good try. (Applause). During the afternoon a pleasing musical programme was given by the band of the 4th Battalion P.W.V, (South Lancashire Regiment), by the kind permission of Lieut-Colonel J. D. Pairolough and officers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>TOWN COUNCIL'S VISIT TO THE INFIRMARY.</head>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL'S VISIT TO THE INFIRMARY.</p>
        <p>At the invitation of the Board of Management of the Warrington Infirmary, the Mayor and members of the Corporation paid an official visit to that institution on Tuesday afternoon and were conducted through the various wards and departments. Owing to the fact that several committees were meeting at the Town Hall, the party was considerably smaller than would otherwise have been the case. There wen present :—The Mayor (Alderman Forshavv), the Deputy-Mayor (Alderman Smethurst, Aldermen Bennett and urton ; Councillors Birtles, Sankey, and Roberts, the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon \\little), and the assistant secretary to the gas department (Mr. W. S. Howard). They were met in the Board-room by Mr. W. Peter Rylands (chairman of the Board of Management),Rev. H. V. Pigot (vice-president) Mr. Charles White (vice-president), Dr. E. E. Bowden (hon. surgeon). Dr. Johnson (resident medical officer), and Mr. J. H. J. Hampson (secretary). Mr. RYLANDS, in welcoming the visitors, said It was within their knowledge that owing to the enlargement of tbe Infirmary, the cost of ,maintenance had very much increased, and one hope the Board had in askiug them to see what was being done, was that they must come to the conclusion that the town also might take a larger share in the responsibilities connected with the institution. Of course, the cost of management was something like £5.000 a year—a very large sum of money to find out of the pockets of individuals, and if the Corporation should come to the conclusion that they would like to take part in supporting the institu- tion, he could not help thinking that they would be wise in acting in a fair and liberal spirit. The MAYOR, in reply, sbid powers were being sought in a Bill which would come before the next Session of Parliament to enable the Corporation to pay an increased subscription to that institution. Letters of regret for their inability to attend were received from the Rev. Canon Stevenson, Councillor and Mrs. C. Jackson Holmes, and Mr. C. E. Parker. _ _ . .</p>
        <p>S.JA... • • • •••• ••• ••• The party was then conducted through the institu- tion by Mr. Rylands and Dr. Johnson. The dispensary the nurses' home, male and female wards, children's room. and operating theatre, were all visited and the members were much impressed with the general air of comfort and the up-to-date equipment of the buildings, A demonstration of the " X" ray treatment aroused considerable interest. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Dr. John- son on the proposition of the Mayor, seconded by Alderman Smethurst. Alderman BENNETT in proposing a vote of thanks to Mr. Rylands and those associated with him in the noble work of the institution said he was strongly of opinion that the Corporation did not do anything like what it ought to do for the Infirmary. Unless the town did a bit more the town might have to do it all. He would not regret it if that was so because at the present the burden fell mainly on a few individuals instead of on the whole of the inhabitants. . Alderman BURTON in seconding made a plea for more support from the various works. A vote of thanks having been passed, Mr. BYLANDS said the cost of the infirmary had suddenly doubled, and they could not expect that it would be followed by the duplication of the income at anything like the same speed. He, however, looked upon what had been done with great satisfaction. The ordinary subscriptions last sear amounted to only ,C6OO. This year they would Certeinly top £l,OOO, and he knew that next year there would be at least :£l*2oll The Secretary told him that a subsciiption list of £1,200 would compare very favourably with any other town of a corresponding size.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>Death of Mrs, John Piarpoint.</head>
        <p>Death of Mrs, John Piarpoint.</p>
        <p>f Many of our readers will regret to hear of the death of Mrs. John Pierpoint, widow of the late 'Alderman John Pierpoint, of Bewsey, which took place at her residence, 5, Palmyra-sqrrare, on Monday. The deceased lady caught a chill a few days ago which developed into pneumonia.. She enjoyed the respect of n large circle of friends and much sympathy is felt • with her son and two daughters i their bereavement. The funeral took place at the cemetery on Thursday afternoon.</p>
      </div>
