      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>WARRINGTON MALE VOICE CHOIR SOCIAL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MALE VOICE CHOIR SOCIAL.</p>
        <p>Presentation to Mr. Nesbitt.</p>
        <p>The annual social in connection with the Warrington Male Voice Choir took place last Monday evening in the Winmarleigh Cate, when, perhaps, the principal event of the evening was the presentation to Mr. and Mrs.Nesbitt. Mr. Nesbitt succeeded Mr.Hassall a little over half a dozen years ago and has led the choir from small beginnings into the first rank of male voice choirs in the district and, therefore, the presentation to him of a silver rose bowl was fully merited. Mr. SAMUEL HASSALL in making the presentation to Mr. Nesbitt, asked him to accept that little gift which had been spontaneously subscribed to by every member of the choir. They considered the progress made by the choir was entirely due to Mr. - Nesbitt's untiring efforts and distinct ability. When Mr. Nesbitt first made his appearance amongst them seven or eight years ago the, musical abilities of the choir were in a. very primitive state and it was owing to his instruc- tion that they had made such rapid strides. They looked forward to his continued association fot their further adcancement and pleasure. 'The concert of a week or two ago showed that they bad gained under Mr. Nesbitt's tuition. The present was not very valu- able but it would remind him of their appreciation of his services in years to come. 'They trusted that he and his wife would be long spared to come amongst them and that Mr. Nesbitt would continue to give them of his undoubted ability and talents. (Applause). Mr. NeselerT on rising to raspond was received with musical honours. He said that he little thought when he came there that night that he was to be the recipient of that token of their appreciation of his connection with them. He rather regretted that they felt it incumbent upon them to Show him in that tangible form their appreciation, because it was absolutely unnecessary. He came there as a duty and they bad crowned his efforts with success. He paid a tribute to Mr. North and the members of the choir who had ,generously supported his efforts.' Looking back on the seven years he said that they had been the most pleasurable he had had in any musical organization he had been connected with. (Applause). They knew very well that he had been connected with the Orpheus choir for 13 or 14 years. It was in a sense his baby—(laughter) —for he along with a few More started it. He was appointed hon. conductor and he had held that position up to the present time and he would not allow them to sack bim. (Laughter.) He could not dictate to the Warrington choir in those terms. (More laughter.) Mrs. Nesbitt was continually telling him that he must give something up. He was no younger than he was. He was getting on, and he felt that something would have to go, but it was not the Warrington choir. (Applause.) He thought that, seeing it was a little present for the household, Mrs. Nesbitt had better offer a word of thanks. On his oun behalf he thanked them- very sincerely for that rose bowl. It was very pretty. He did not look at that kind Of thing from the valua- tion standpoint. He was not like a politician who would pay £2,000,000 for valuation to get £20,000 or £30,000. He very much appreciated the spirit that had prompted that gift. and he hoped that the good feeling which existed, between them would continue. (Applause). Mrs. Nesbitt was about to utter a word of thanks,when Mr. TAYLOR rose holding a gold bangle, and saki that the choir were going to give Mrs. Neshitt that small present as a token of her sacrifice for their education and pleasure. Mr. Nesbitt was a very busy man, and a great deal of sacrifice must have been made by Mrs. Nesbitt when she allowed him to leave the household so often. The names of Mr. and Mrs. Nesbitt were inseparable to the achievements attained by the choir. (Applause). Mrs. Neserer responding said that she never grumbled when her husband was at Warrington, although she might 'do on other occasions. He bed hinted at getting together a mixed choir and then she would join. (Laughter and applause). _ During the evening a Whist Drive was held, the prize-winncrs being as follows :—Ladies : 1, Miss M. Hipkiss, booby, Miss Boscow ; Gentlemen : 1, Mr. W. Barnes, booby, Mr. Gillespie. After refreshments had been partaken of an excellent musical programme was gone through and dancing was engaged in to the accompaniment of Sir. Foster. The programme which was heartily enjoyed was as follows: Mr North, pianoforte solo : Miss Brooks' A Summer Night;" Mr. Dutton and Miss Silcock "In-the Spring- time ;" Miss Silcock, " Orpheus with his Lute and "Forget Me Fot '." Messrs. W. Dennett, J. Mather, John Gibson. rand A. Pearce, the youngest members of the choir, "Absence ;" Miss Currie Hastings "The Waltz Song " frcm 'Tom Jones ;" Mr. J.Wil lett "Ailsa Mine " ; and the Male Voice Choir"s 0 Peaceful Night" and "Beleagured."</p>
      </div>
