      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>HOUSING PROBLEM.</head>
        <p>THE HOUSING PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>Lecture at the Adult School.</p>
        <p>Councillor T. R. MARR, of Manchester, delivered a lecture on " Housing " at the Warrington Adult School, Academy-street, on 'Thursday evening. Mr. C. E. PARKER, who presided over a small attendance, expressed his indebtedness to Councillor Marr who bad rendered service to housing reform by the publication of an admirable book dealing with the conditions in Manchester. He divided the city into districts and showed that the death rate very closely followed the housing conditions. Where the houses were crowded together and might be described more or less as slum property, the rate was very high indeed. The figures ranged front about 36 per 1,000 in the congested areas to 13 per 1,000 in the more open and sanitary districts. It must be borne in mind that the poor and badly nourished people lived in the slums and therefore housing could not be the sole cause of this great and serious difference. Defective housing was, however, perhaps the most important condition that went to lessen vitality and the length of life. In his book Councillor Marr made a quotation from Dr.Ransome in which he showed by experiment that when the sputum from a consumptive patient was allowed to remain in slum conditions where there was not an abundance of light and air, it was virulent for many months, but when the doctor removed similar germs to his own house, where there was plenty of light and air, they died. Councillor MA RR said that bad as he knew slums could be in Manchester and other great towns, still, judging from the report on recent investigations, he doubted whether they in Warrington had any right to believe that their conditions were in any way superior to the conditions of some of the larger towns. Slum con- ditions were like active sores in the organic body. They could not be dealt with by ordinary general treatment of the system ; only a surgical operation was really effec- tive. The real slum area required cutting out altogether. Touching upon the problem of rent, Councillor Marr said the workingman who was constantly demanding cheap houses was the greatest opponent of good housing. It was that demand which bad largely led to the miles of jerry built streets which were to be found in large areas in nearly all the big industrial towns of England. If the workingman was well-advised he would aim at getting the best possible value for the amount he paid and seek to improve his wages and standard of comfort. Having dealt expansively with the powers possessed by municipalities to deal with defective, property and commended the garden suburbs scheme the lecturer said no scheme for improving housing in the great centres would be satisfactory unless they had in their municipalities men and women who were keen on the problem. It was a frequent occurrence— so frequent that it had become almost a commonplace— .to discover that the sanitary administration and hous.</p>
        <p>THE SPOT SHOP,</p>
        <p>(FISH MARKET)</p>
        <p>ing conditions of towns were looked after by a com- mittee which contained a large proportion of the very gentlemen who owned nr were agents for slum proper- ties. Such a state of affairs was a standing disgrace. Now that municipal powers had been extended, the time was ripe for urging on all citizens the importance of carefully studying the personnel of their municipal councils and seeing that they they were constituted of people who were prepared to take a wide view which regarded the welfare of the majority of citizens rather than their own private well-being. The CHAIRMAN said he believed the building of new houses ought to take place outside the towns. In most of the large centres the bulk of the land that was so far unoccupied would in future be required for recrea- tion grounds. Ire bad not much confidence in Town Councils. If they wanted a "soft job" let them seek it under a Corporation. There was not the same direct oversight and control that obtained in private concerns. The" model" byelaws of the Local Government Board had been a serious obstruction to many developments of housing, notably to the garden suburbs movement, as they imposed conditions which were not necessary or adaptable in the laying out of estates like those which the Garden Suburbs Company had acquired. Under the new Homing and Town Planning Act the conditions would be regulated more in accord- ance with common sense and the varying requirements. There was no doubt that the present housing conditions of England were a disgrace to the country. The new Act would enable ratepayers to apply pressure to slow- going Councils by appealing direct to the Local Government Board.</p>
        <p>An interesting discussion followed, one of the points raised being the difficulties which confronted the authorities in districts where the tenants received very low wages and had lost all idea of cleanliness, and where landlords had to press very hard to recover their rents.</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks was passed to Councillor Marr on the proposition of Mr. J. A. RANSOME.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
