      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>PENKETH ATHLETE-E. DARLINGTON.</head>
        <p>A PENKETH ATHLETE-E. DARLINGTON.</p>
        <p>Photo</p>
        <p>Penketh has added another of her worthy sons to the already long list of successful athletes who have been produced by the village. Ernest Darlington, who was born on September 3rd 1888, at Penketh, made- his first appearance on the track in the year 1906 at the Liverpool Pembroke Harriers' Spotts where he won his heat off 81 yards start in the 100 yards. The -next time was at St. Helens where he ran third. During the year 1907 he only ran four times, when he was successful at the Liverpool Workingmen's Conservative Sports winning the first prize in the 100 yards with 81 yards start, and aided with a slight wind he ran the 100 yards in 9 4-ssec. This was his last turn out in 1907. In 1908 he gained successes at Salford Harriers' Sports, Manchester and District Works League' Sports and Prescot Wire Works Sports. This year he has done well in numerous contests, meeting R. E. Walker in the Broughton Harriers' Sports at Fallowfield. Walker conceded 21 yards in the 100. and just beat Darlington on the tape, time 10 1-ssecs. At Royton he won the 226 yards handicap off 81 yards, and got second in the 100 yards. He was second also in the 220 at the N.C.U. Sports. As Darlington has youth on his side he should considerably enhance his reputation during ensuing seasons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>WARRINGTON BRIDGE. THE APPOINTiIiNT OF MR.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BRIDGE. THE APPOINTiIiNT OF MR.</p>
        <p>WEBSTER. -- Opposition from Councillor</p>
        <p>Pemberton.</p>
        <p>The following letter from Mr. J. J. Webster was read at a meeting of theGeneraliPurposes:Committee :- 39, Victoria Street, Westminster. S.W. • October 213th, 1900. "Dear Sir,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>"I am obliged by your letter:-Ciy-esterday's date informing me that the Council had appointed Me as Engineer to adjudicate on the designs and tenders received for the construction of the new bridge, also that I wal appointed as Engineer to superintend the carrying out of the work. I shall be pleased to undertake these duties, and to prevent any future misunderstanding, the following are the conditions upon which I understand the appoint- ment has been made:— (1) That I examine the plans, make all the necessary calculations for checking the various sections, &amp;c., make a full report upon the designs and tenders, and recommend the acceptance of such design and tender as I consjoder best fulfils the specified requirements. ' (2) That I prepare the final specification to be attached to the Contract, prepare such drawings as may be necessary for any modifications of the plans sub- mitted, which I might consider advisable, such modifica- tions to receive the approval of the Council. (3) That every detail drawing submitted by the con- tractor is to receive my approval before being worked to. (4) That I superintend the erection o I the work, making frequent visits to the site. (5) That a competent Clerk of the Works or resident engineer beappointed to see that my instructions are carried out, to be paid by the Council. (6) That I make a monthly report to the Ceitncil and make out the Certificate of payment to the Con- tractor. (7) 3 bat my remuneration for the above services be at the rate of 5 per cent. on the cost of the works, exclusive of travelling expenses. Subject to your approval an Agreement between the Council and myself might be drawn up upon the above lines. With reference to the adjudication of the plans, it would be much more convenitnt and satisfactury if the plans were sent to my office; there will be a tremendous amount of work in checking the calculations, etc., and I could do this more readily in my own office, and 1 should be available for my assistants who may wish to see me about other work in hand. I would commence the adjudication as soon as I received the plans, and send in my report as soon as possible. Yours faithfully, JOHN 3. WEBSTER.</p>
        <p>J. Lyon Whittle,Esq., Town Clerk, Warrington. Resolved, that the terms and conditions contained in the above letter be agreed to. The Borough Surveyor reported that he had received designs and tenders for the new bridge from 20 contractors. Resolved, that the designs, specifications, bills of quantities, and all papers other than the tenders received from tenderers be sent to Mr. J. J. Webster for his adjudication and report. Resolved, that the Mayor, Deputy-Mayor. with the Town Clerk and Borough Surveyor be appointed a Com- mittee to open the sealed packets, to extract the tenders, and to forward all the other papers to Mr. J. J. Webster, and that a list of the persons who had sent in tenders be sent to each member of the Council. COUNCILLOR PEMBERTON'S SPECIAL MOTION. At the monthly meetine, of the Town Council on Tuesday, Councillor Pemberton will move: 1. That Standing Order No. 3be suspended in order to enable the Council to consider the notice of motion standing to the name of Mr. Ccuncillor Pemberton, with reference to Warrington Bridge. 2. That so much of the following resolution passed by the Council on the 26th October, 1909, as relates to the appointment of Mr. J. J. Webster as engineer to superintend the construction of Warrington Bridge, be rescinded "Resolved, that on the recommendation of the borough surveyor, Mr. J. J. Webster be appointed engineer to adjudicate and report on the plans, specifications and bills of quantities, and also engineer to superintend the carrying out of the work." MR. WEBSTER'S ADJUDICATION. We are informed that Mr. Webster hopes to be able to send in his report and adjudication upon the plans which have been submitted to him on December 10th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>LYM M.</head>
        <p>LYM M.</p>
        <p>SHOP FIRE.—On Friday, the 26th November, whilst ' Mrs. Shepherd, tobacconist, of Bridgewater-street, was cleaning in the shop, a " maiden " full of clothes 'caught fire in the kitchen below. These were com- pletely destroyed, as well as a valuable parrot that she had kept for over twenty years. SUDDEN DEATH AT LYMM.—On Tuesday afternoon Richard Daniels, of Newfield, after returning home about half-past three, expired very suddenly. It seems that he went out of the house and shouted at a man for hitting his little boy, and the excitement appar- ently caused heart failure. He was weLl known throughout the district as a lamplighter.—An inquest was held at the Police Court on Wednesday afternoon, Mr. T. Ridgway being the coroner. The cause of death was returned as in " Natural causes." RE-OPENING OF THE LYMNI PRIMITIVE METHODIST SCHOOL—On Sunday last, the above school was opened, after having been enlarged very considerably, five new class rooms being nowayailable. The preacher was Mr. Barlow, of Hartley College, Manchester, who preached two sermons to very satisfactory congrega- tions. In the afternoon a children's service was held. Mr. G. L. Welford presided. Miss M. J. Appleton rendered a solo entitled "Tato Thy hands," and several other pieces were given by the small children. Tn the evening, Mrs. J. Warham rendered "Remember now thy Creator," and Miss Esther Appleton took the solo part of the anthem. On Monday evening a public tea was held in the schoolroom, and gas not having yet been installed, those who assembled had to be content with having the place lighted by candles and lamps. Mr. J. D. Jackson who should have given a lecture was indisposed so the Rev. W. B.Whiteway gave an address on"My adventures on the field of Waterloo." Mr. C. W. Brompton of Grimsby presided, and the lecture was thoroughly enjoyed by those present. The work of the enlarging of the schoolroom has been carried out by Mr. John H. Holt contractor and builder of Lymm. Collections were taken at the services in aid of the trust funds, ,</p>
        <p>SOCIAL AND DANCE.—A social and dance promoted by the evening continuation school was held in the Newchurch Schoolroom on Saturday evening last. Gramophone selections were given during the evening. and a pianoforte solo entitled "In Remembrance" was very ably rendered by Master George Thornley. Refreshments were provided and Mr. J. Orme was the accompanist.</p>
        <p>"GOLDEN FLEECE" MARGARINE, Bd. per pound, or 1 pounds for ls.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>"The Girl Who Lost Her Character."</p>
        <p>Mr. Walter Melville's drama "The Girl who lost her Character" is the attraction at the Royal Court Theatre this week, and the popular parts of the house have been largely patronized. The plot is sufficiently obvious to be followed without effort, and the many extraordinary, situations work up the excitement to a high pitch. Mr. Douglas Bruce's company gives a capable presentment, Miss Doris Brookes, in the title role, and Mr. iiichard Ferris as the unscrupulous Captain Geoffrey Caversham filling their exacting parts with conspicuous success. Mr. Wilson Gunning impersonates the Rev. Robert Everton with befitting earnestness, and Miss Maggie Hobart presents a clever study of Rose Seymour. One of the most sensational incidents of the evening is witnessed at the end of the second act, when thejilted woman gives vent to her frenzied despair by smashing the furniture in the Captain's London house. The *.cenery is on an elaborate scale and the orchestral selections are, as usual, bright and pleasing. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME, WARRINGTON DRAMATIC SOCIETY'S PERFORMANCES.</p>
        <p>Large audiences should be the rule next week when the Warringtoa Dramatic Society give their annual performances in aid of the Warrington Infirmary,Nursing Association,and St. John Ambulance Brigade. An unusually attractive programme, which does credit to the enterprise and ambition of the amateurs,will be presented. On the first three nights the society will produce Mr. Sydney Grandy's successful comedy. " A Pair of Spectacles," which will be preceded by the comedietta, "Mrs. Hilary Regrets." On Thursday, Friday and Saturday the boards will be occupied by Mr. Haddon Chambers' well-known play 'The Idler," which the society gave with cousiderable success in 1904.</p>
        <p>The cast for "A Pair of Spectacles " is as follows.:—</p>
        <p>Mr. Benjamin Goldfinch Mr. Sydney Wallhead Uncle Gregory Percy Mr. F. W. Richmond Dick Mr. W. H. Brown Lorimer Mr. R. P. Johnson Bartholomew Mr. Walter Young Joyce Mr. John Craik Mrs. Goldfinch Miss Dorothy Browne Lucy Lorimer Miss Bessie Pierpoint Charlotte</p>
        <p>In "Mrs. Hilary Regrets" the characters will be sustained by 4lr. Geoffrey Dodd, Miss Rose Wallhead, and Miss Mary Brundret. The following is the cast for" The Idler " : Mark Cross Mr. Charles H. Wood Sir John Harding, M.P Mr. Sidney Wellhead Simeon Strong Mr. H. G. Mosley General Merryweether Mr. Geoffrey Dodd Bennett, Mark Cross' servant lir. Walter Young Lady Harding Vrs. E. Redmayne Jones Mrs.Cross,Mark's mother... Miss Constance H.Broadbent Kate Merryweather Miss Bessie Pierpoint Mrs. Glynne-Stanmore Mrs. P. E. Griffiths Seats can now be booked at the Theatre.</p>
      </div>
