      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>HISTORY OF THE PARISH CHURCff: Lecture By the Rector.</head>
        <p>THE HISTORY OF THE PARISH CHURCff: Lecture By the Rector.</p>
        <p>, Under the auspices of the Warrington Church "Society,the Rector(Rev.Canon Willis)delivered a lecture on "The history of the Warrington Parish Church" in the Assembly Room at the Conservative Club, last evening. He said that St. Elphin, who lived here about the year .700, doubtless built the first church, for they found hi Domesday Book, according to the late Mr. Beamont, that he held a curucate of land free from all tax except' the gelt, which was the amount usually given for the erection of a church. Antiquarians suggested that the. building was probably constructed of mud and wattle. Five churches, he believed, had stood upon the present site. The first was a Saxon Church, the second a Norman building erected about 1150 by Mattho3us de Vilars, who granted the advowson in 1167 to the Prior of Thurgarton or Fiskerton-on-Trent, in Nottinghamshire. This church stood for about two hundred years, until 1350, the patronage being recon- veyed about 1250 to Sir William Fitz Almeric le Boteler, the seventh Baron, who had removed his seat from the Mote Hill to Bewsey. Of this church some stones were still to be seen in the Warrington Museum. It was taken down about 1350, and in its place was built the third church, in the decorated style of architecture, by Sir William Fitz William le Boteler, the 9th Baron, in 1354, of which the chancel still remains. The nave, after standing over four hundred years, was taken down in 1770. In that year the fourth nave was built by the Rev. Edward Owen, which lasted till 1859, but in 1833, soon after he came, Rector, Powys took down the south wall of the nave and built with the same stones the present aisle. In 1859 and onwards what was practically the fifth Church was built, that was to say, all that was now visible except the chancel and the south aisle. It was a. noble building, and though the nave, spire and chancel were all out of proportion, and the absence or clerestory windows darkened the interior, and the galleries were not ornamental, the general effect both externally and internally was very impressive and really wonderful considering the time at which it was done and the unecclesiastical feeling of the age. Having dealt in detail with the chancel, the Rector said the tower of the church of 1354 was shattered by Cromwell's cannon balls in the Civil War, when his army was encamped near the Church, and was taken down in 1690 by Rector Shaw and re-built by him in 1696. Of this second tower, a very heavy building, the greater part was taken down by Rector Quekett in 1860, a portion falling and bringing down with it the westernmost windows on either side of the chancel. The present tower, built by Mr. Quekett, in 1860, was a beautiful object, and so was the spire by which it was surmounted, the total height being 281 feet. The Rector also sketched the history of the Boteler or Lady Chapel, and described the various enrichments of the church of late years, the most recent beine the erection of a handsome memorial screen at the West end at a cost of £6oo.—The lecture was well illustrated with limelight views, the lantern being manipulated by the Rev. H. A. Lester, and proved most interesting.</p>
        <p>MARRIAGES. BLUNDELL—SAVAGE.—November 27th. at the Parish Church, Warrington, by the Rev..A. J. Bratt, George Artl34 Blundell to Ada Savage. BLAKEMORE—DARBYSHIRE.—November 28th, at the Parish Church, Warrington, by the Rev. A. J. Bratt, Thomas Blakemore to Sarah Ann Darbyshire. BAKER—HESKETH.—November 30th, at the Parish Church, Warrington, by the Rev. Canon Baker to Clara Hesketh.</p>
        <p>DEATHS. BORROWS—On November 30th, at 11, Tanners-lane, Richard Borrows, aged 69 years. JANE—On November 30th, at 11, Elaine-street, Warring- ton. Emily Ethel, the beloved daughter of the late William and Mrs. Jane, aged 29 years.—The family desire to tender their sincere thanks for the many expressions of sympathy in their bereavement. HOPE.—On December 2nd, at 68, Hood-lane, Sankey, Warrington, Marjorie Hope, aged 24 years.</p>
        <p>Warrington.—Printed and published by Jortti WALICEP dc Co.; (Warrington) Ltd., at The Examiner General Printing Works, Bewsey-street, awl published by them at the same place, and also at The Examiner Offices, Victoria-road, WIDNES' 32, George-street, ST. HELENS: 3, Market Street. EARLESTOWN; Church-street RUNCORN Dec. 4th,1902</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>_ , i – ' • =7,7'7 "7-</head>
        <p>_ , i - ' • =7,7'7 "7-</p>
        <p>CALEDONIAN ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Annual Dinner.</p>
        <p>Perhaps the greatest event of the year to the Scotsmen of Warrington, as to their kinsmen throughout the world, is the celebration of the anniversary of their patron saint (St. Andrew). The Warringtdn Caledonian Association always observe the Festival by bolding their annual dinner. This year the function—the twenty-first of its kind—was held on Tuesday at the Lion Hotel and about 60 gentlemen attended. Coun- cillor Fulton-Smith presided and amongst the guests were Councillor H. Roberts, Drs. Manson and Joseph, Messrs. E. Draper, E. J. Hall, F. W. Monks, and Lieut.-Colonel Fairclough. The menu was excellent and included "Haggis," which was served in Scottish fashion, a piper (Sergeant Daniel Murray, of Liverpool) heading the procession, followed by the chef and his attendants carrying the haggis and wine above their heads.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>KING AND THE CRISIS.</head>
        <p>THE KING AND THE CRISIS.</p>
        <p>Dinner over the president proposed the toast "The King "and said that he thought that in the present crisis the deepest sympathy should be with the King. The affairs of the nation must be a great source of concern to him at this time, but they were quite sure that his Majesty's usual tact and great sagacity would serve him in good stead.</p>
        <p>The toast w.;is drunk with musical honours. The toasts of the "" Queen ". and "The Duke and Duchess of Rothesay " were also honoured. NATIONAL DEFENCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. DAVENPORT proposed "The Navy, Army and Territorial Forces." He said they were proud of their defence forces of this country which after all formed the bed rock on which the Imperial structure was made. We must have these defensive forces so long as human nature was what it was. We had not yet reached the age of the millienium and we must recognise that self-preservation was the first law of nature. In these days of political controversy it could not be too strongly urged that this question must be outside the arena of party conflict. Our very existence as a nation depended upon our defending our interests in our Empire where they were in danger of attack. They had only to picture what would happen if the first-line of defence were broken—it was unthinkable. He thought that was an aspect that appealed to every politician what- ever party he belonged to, and that he would recognise the responsibilities of maintaining the service in the condition that we hoped to keep it. He coupled the name of Lieutenant-Colonel Fairclough with the toast. (Applause). Lieutenant Colonel FAIRCLOUGH responding said that in the rush of the present political conflict there was one thing of vital interest—that of national safety. It was not extravagant to build ships, but a necessity as it, was forced upon us by foreign empires and if we wished to meet the competition of those empires we must organise ourselves. The words of Nelson were as true to-day as when he said them that "the best negotiation in the councils of Europe was a fleet Of battleships." Battleships enabled us to get our rights and also provided employment for a great number of men. £4 out of every £6 that went to the building of battleships went to workingmen. Continu- ing the speaker referred to the Army and said that our regular army was the finest in the world. If they took the regular land battalion there was not a European regiment that could stand up to them. We had had over twelve months of Mr. Haldane's excellent scheme. It only wanted backing up by the citizens. There was one thing that they were short of and that was airships. Our Qovernment were building an airship now, but its progress was very slow. He thought that if they would only copy the continental nations and give a subsidy to a man who would invent an airship it would be a great thing. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>GREETINGS.</head>
        <p>GREETINGS.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN read a letter of apology for non- attendance from the Mayor (Alderman W. J. Forshaw), who was suffering from a cold on the chest, and Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., who wrote "that times were so critical that he must remain in London." Sir Peter C. Walker wrote apologising for non-attendance and wished them a good time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>" SCOTLAND."</head>
        <p>" SCOTLAND."</p>
        <p>Mr. 11. ROBERTS, in proposing the toast of " Scot- land " said there was no man in that room or out of it .L--who was not a Scotsman—who had a greater affection for Scotland or a more profound regard for the Scottish people and their character, for grit and perseverance and pluck, and determination to" get on," than himself. Entering into private life at a comparatively early age, the time at his disposal for purposes of travel had been extremely limited, which would at once explain the fact that it had only been hjs good fortune to " cross the border" on two occasions,—the first time for reasons of health in 1893, and the second, a few years later, as one of a deputation from the Corporation of Warrington to a Sanitary Science Congress. He mentioned that his second visit to Scotland brought him into close touch with a department of public life which, he said, had always had almost a fascination for him and to which he had devoted a siderahle amount of time and strength. He referred to municipal administration. The Sanitary Congress was held in the city of Glasgow and what he saw and heard from the leading men at head of the various civic undertakings in that great industrial and commercial centre, confirmed the opinion he had previously held. He believed then, and he believed still, that Glasgow had the finest and best and most complete all-round municipal administration of any city in the world. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Roberts then entered upon a comprehensive survey of the municipal enterprises of Glasgow, and paid a high tribute to the unselfish service and administrative capacity of Glasgow's leading men. In conclusion he appealed to his hearers, as Scotsmen who had made Warrington their home, to help in bringing to bear upon the municipal administration of this town that same foresight and public spirit which hive for many generations been so characteristic of their countrymen in the great northern city and which had made her at once the envy and admiration of the municipalities of the world. (Applause.) "WARRING FON CALEDONIAN ASSOCIATION."</p>
        <p>Mr. E. J. HALLO]) a witty speech, nroposed the toast " Wallington Caledonian Association" and coupled with it tbe name of Mr. Bowman. The toast having been drunk, Mr. Bowman replying said that the ?act that the society. bad existed for 21 years was perhaps more eloquent testimony to its vitality and usefulness than any words could express. Since its inception it had always taken a broad outlook and while it had preserved national characteristics it had not promulgated national prejudices. The objects of the society were two-fold, namely, benevolence and good fellowship. With regard to the former be was pleased to say that their charitable proclivities were not confined to their own nationality and it had been their pleasure to hand over to the Warrington Infirmary the proceeds of the recent concert. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>"TOWN AND TRADE OF iVARRINGTON."</head>
        <p>"TOWN AND TRADE OF iVARRINGTON."</p>
        <p>The toast" Warrington its Corporation and Trade" was proposed by Mr. F. W. Monks who said that there was no doubt that the Corporation of Warrington had an enormous amount of work to do. The shoulders of members of the Council were burdened and he thought that they would all agree with him that they carried out the work extremely well. He expressed the opinion that. corporate work might go on more smoothly and efficiently if we had a permanent Mayor —a GovernmeCnt official—as in Germany, for at present the Mayor had no sooner got into the work than he had to make way for his successor. Speaking of trade locally, he said he was more interested in business than in politics,' but if Tariff Reform were going to improve the trade in Warrington he would be trite ready to vote for it. ILaughter). He coupled wlthAbe toast the name of Councillor Steel. After the toast bad been drunk, Councillor STEEL responded,and alluded to the efforts .that were being made to secure better co-operation between Committees of the Corporation. He defended the town against the attacks which had been made upon it by "a most irresponsible person," and said he thought it would bear comparison with the adjoining towns. (Hear, bear.) They were doing all they could to eliminate the slums tvitcl improve the conditions of housing, and encourage better building. Mr. J. McDERIVID proposed "Our Patron," (Sir Peter Walker) which was musically honoured. The toasts" The Lassies,' proposed by Dr. J. P. Manson," Our Guests," by, Mr. W. Murray, and "The President," by Mr. W. Skinner were also honoured in the customary way. Au attractive programme of vocal items—songs, choruses, duets, etc.,—was submitted in capital style by the Minnesingers (Manchester), Messrs. IVill Rowley, Robert Fallows, Harry Hartley, and Will Pickard.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>Warrington Canine Society.</head>
        <p>Warrington Canine Society.</p>
        <p>1 The Warrington Canine Society held a series of matches at the Lion Hotel on Thursday evening. The rain, which fell in torrents, kept a good many would-be exhibitors away. Mr. Lloyd Lewis (Liver- pool) was judge, Mr. W. C. Baines, show manager, and Mr. Sanderson, secretary. Some exceedingly good dogs put in an appearance, especially. among the puppies. A splendid silver pewter for the best dog was won by Mr. Fair Turner with a bull terrier, Sankey Heroine, the reserve (a .silver medal)) going to Mr. Booth's cocker spaniel Handforth Pluegown, The puppy special, a silver medal was taken by Mr. White's retriever Wild Night. The following are the results of the matehes -:—Mr. White's Wild Night beat Mr. Tyrer's Emandator ; Mr. Turner's Sankey Heroine beat Mr. White's Dark Night ; Dr. Edwards (Wigan) Lady beat Mr. Sanderson's Rowton Judy; Mr. Booth's Handforth Bluegown beat Mr. Berry's Jolley Roger; Mr. Turner's Sankey Jimmy beat Mr. Townley's Togo • Mr. Turner's Sankey Fan a bye. Puppies: Dr. Edwards's (Wigan) Lady beat Mr. Tyrer's Gertrade ; Mr. White's Wild Night beat Mr. Townley's Julius.. •</p>
