        <p>under the advice of a Minister the writs of summons were withheld, there was no court which could enforce their issue. The excluded peers might hold a mass meeting in Trafalgar-sq. or organise a promenade towards Westminster (laughter)— but, being unable to get a mandamus against the Crown, they would have no legal redress. LORD CREWE WINDS UP. Lord Crewe wound up the debate for the Government in the last speech before the vote. He spoke coldly and calmly, impress- ing the revolutionary peers with the unshaken confidence and firmness with which the Government were going to accept the issue. ' "It may be," he said, "that when the new Parliament meets we shall be sitting where you sit now. It may be that we shall still be sitting here, but in either case we must, after the action you are taking, get ourselves to obtain guarantees, not old guarantees sanctioned by course of time and enforced by accommodation between the two Houses, but, if necessary, and if there is no other way, guaranteed, fenced about, and</p>
