        <p>• EFFORT FOR THE INFiRIARY. The harriers of Warrington are to be congratulated upon the public spirit which prompted them to assist ttie funds of the Infirmary by organising a great meet of the local clubs. Rslands' Recreation ground was the scene of this interesting athletic gatheting but unfortunately the depressing weather and the counter attraction at Wilderspool militated considerably against the attendance. Two races were contested by over if() runners from four clubs—Warrington A.C., Helens Cable AIC., Rylands' Workmen R.C.H., and the War- rington United H. In the first event—the five mile team race—the full number of competitors turned out but as rain descended pitilessly almost the whole of the time only about one-third completed the course which consisted of lilaps on the football field, ten laps inside the ground and finishing with a lap on the foot- ball field. .1. Paisonage, Warrington A.C., soon gained the lead and, 'taking easy strides, maintained - his</p>
